\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Why the Mississippi  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights case is so important\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2021\par 12:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The pivotal Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization could lead to weakening or overturning the landmark Roe v Wade\par \par \uc2 On 1 December, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is set to hear oral arguments in what has been called the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case in almost half a century \u8211\'20\'13 a case that could redefine reproductive rights across the country and have repercussions abroad for generations.\par \par \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization could lead to weakening or overturning the landmark 1973 supreme court ruling, Roe v Wade, which effectively legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US. If that were to happen, tens of millions people of reproductive age across the country would be affected.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the court\u8217\'20\'19s nine justices are expected to hear from the state of Mississippi, which has argued that the court should use the case to overturn Roe, and from the state\u8217\'20\'19s last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, which will argue to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as they stand.\par \par \uc2 What is happening at the supreme court?\par \par \uc2 In Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, Dobbs refers to \uc2 Dr Thomas Dobbs\uc2 , Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s health department director, and the official who would enforce the law. Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization is the last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Mississippi, which is also known as the Pink House.\par \par \uc2 The highest court in the US will hear from attorneys in the case, which centers on whether Mississippi can outlaw nearly all abortions at and after 15 weeks gestation.\par \par \uc2 The case directly challenges the precedent in Roe v Wade, which guarantees the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 Roe v Wade protects the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point a fetus can survive outside the womb, widely regarded as 24 weeks gestation. A full term pregnancy is 39 weeks gestation.\par \par \uc2 The court is now dominated by conservatives, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/oct/11/us-supreme-court-john-roberts-politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six of the nine}}}\uc2  justices leaning to the right. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  advocates}}}\uc2  see the Mississippi case as especially perilous for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights because justices could have dismissed Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s law as unconstitutional under Roe. However, the court chose to take the case, indicating at least four justices see a reason to revisit the historic ruling. A decision is expected in June 2022, but the hearing on 1 December could provide clues as to the justices\u8217\'20\'19 thinking.\par \par \uc2 What could happen to Roe v Wade?\par \par \uc2 Congress never enshrined the right to terminate a pregnancy in law. Instead, pregnant people in the US have relied on Roe v Wade for almost 50 years, and the precedent is now considered to be under threat.\par \par \uc2 The justices could decide to dismiss the Mississippi case, upholding Roe v Wade. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates worry that the court could instead rule in a way that may weaken or even overturn it.\par \par \uc2 The justices could decide that the 15-week law does not put a significant burden on people in Mississippi, and issue a ruling that would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be banned at a much earlier stage of pregnancy. This is would considerably restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and weaken Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 If Roe is overturned, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 states}}}\uc2  are expected to move to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in various ways, through state constitutional amendments, \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger\u8221\'20\'1D laws which go into effect as soon as possible should Roe be overturned, or limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beginning at six weeks gestation, before most know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Nearly all of these 26 states are in the midwest and south. Together, states considered to be hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are home to 58% of women of reproductive age, or 40 million of the 72 million women between 15 and 49 years of age.\par \par \uc2 In just one example, Louisiana has a \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D that will immediately outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe v Wade is overturned. Today, if a pregnant person in Louisiana wanted a safe legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they would drive 37 miles on average. However, if Roe v Wade were overturned, that distance would increase by 1,720% to 630 miles on average, because a person seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Louisiana would probably need to travel to Illinois, the closest state which affirms a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to obtain one.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have warned that if Roe v Wade falls, the procedure could become both inaccessible and a criminal offense. Criminal justice experts have said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans could lead to a wave of prosecutions under murder statutes and mass incarceration.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates argue that the procedure would also still continue, but would no longer be safely performed in a legal, clinical setting, putting the health of pregnant people at significant risk.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans would also probably compound the ways the US already fails many pregnant people, namely by increasing the maternal mortality rate, which is already the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-brief-report/2020/dec/maternal-mortality-united-states-primer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest among wealthy nations}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Would overturning Roe v Wade make the US an outlier in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights?\par \par \uc2 In recent decades, there has been a longstanding trend toward liberalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in countries across the world. Since 1994, at least 50 countries have liberalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, and now 970 million women or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://maps.reproductiverights.org/worldabortionlaws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 59% of the world\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive aged female population}}}\uc2  lives in a country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is broadly legal and available.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, there has also been movement to liberalize laws in Latin America, a region known for extremely punitive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 International law considers \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to be human rights. The \uc2 UN\uc2  rapporteur for the right to health, Dr Tlaleng Mofokeng, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/08/catastrophic-implications-un-health-expert-condemns-us-over-threat-to-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Guardian}}}\uc2  if the US allows states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  it would immediately violate international human rights law, including the convention against torture, to which the US is a party.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf that gets overturned,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mofokeng of Roe v Wade, \u8220\'20\'1Cit has catastrophic implications, not just for the US.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211201ehc1000um\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
