\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 What will US\u8217\'20\'19s future look like if  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  becomes a crime again?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza, Graphics by Zala \u352\'01\'60e\u353\'01\'61ko \par \uc2 1,955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2021\par 23:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As Roe v Wade faces a direct challenge, criminal defense attorneys, prosecutors, local judges and cops begin to lay out what it would look like to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In the early 1970s, law enforcement leaders in Chicago decided the practice of illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was intolerable in their city and, in a mostly forgotten chapter of history, undertook a campaign to root out those who performed the procedure in secret.\par \par \uc2 On a tip, police turned their attention to \u8220\'20\'1CCall Jane\u8221\'20\'1D, a feminist collective of young women who, since 1965, had provided safe but illegal abortions to roughly 3,000 Chicagoans per year. The collective was raided after two Catholic women told police their sister-in-law planned to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2018/10/14/us/illegal-abortion-janes.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 performed by the group}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2018/01/19/578620266/before-roe-v-wade-the-women-of-jane-provided-abortions-for-the-women-of-chicago"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chicago homicide detective}}}\uc2  was assigned to the case and traced \u8220\'20\'1CJane\u8221\'20\'1D to the South Shore neighborhood, bordered by the blue waters of Lake Michigan. There, police raided an apartment, arrested nearly 50 people for questioning, and sent three women who were actively undergoing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  treatment to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Seven women were charged with 11 counts of performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and conspiracy to commit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They would soon be dubbed the \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Seven\u8221\'20\'1D by newspapers. But the Call Jane members protected people they served \u8211\'20\'13 they even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/aug/05/doctors-who-provide-abortions-us-choice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ate index cards}}}\uc2  detailing patients\u8217\'20\'19 contact information.\par \par \uc2 Then, in 1973, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Seven had a reprieve. Prosecutors abandoned the case when supreme court justices issued a landmark ruling in the case of Roe v Wade, effectively legalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the US.\par \par \uc2 In Roe, the court affirmed that access to safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a constitutional right. The court ruled that states could not ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus can survive outside the womb, roughly considered to be 24 weeks gestation (a full term pregnancy is considered to be 39 weeks).\par \par \uc2 Now, Roe faces a direct challenge. \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices have taken up the case of Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Clinic, in which they will consider whether the state of Mississippi can ban nearly all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from 15 weeks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates believe the choice to take the case implies that at last four justices see it as a chance to reconsider the precedent set by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oral arguments in the case are set to be heard on 1 December, with a ruling expected in June 2022. But already, pro-choice campaigners are warning of a future where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may no longer be legal in the majority of the US, and where prosecutions like that of the \u8220\'20\'1CCall Jane\u8221\'20\'1D collective could once again become a reality.\par \par \uc2 In court briefs and reports, defense attorneys, prosecutors, judges, international human rights experts and academics have begun to unpick what a return to illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might look like in a country with a vast law enforcement apparatus, with the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest incarcerated population, and with women as America\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prisonpolicy.org/reports/women_overtime.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fastest growing imprisoned demographic}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like a thought experiment \u8211\'20\'13 to think about what \u8216\'20\'18Call Jane\u8217\'20\'19 would look like,\u8221\'20\'1D in the modern era, said Cynthia Conti-Cook, a technology fellow with the \uc2 Ford Foundation\uc2 . Her work in gender, racial and ethnic justice explores how law enforcement could use the data produced by digital infrastructure \u8211\'20\'13 phones, internet browsers, social media \u8211\'20\'13 to prosecute people who seek or aid abortions, should Roe v Wade be overturned, and the procedure become illegal in some states once again.\par \par \uc2 A single mobile phone could reveal the entire collective, Conti-Cook warned. Just one encounter with law enforcement \u8211\'20\'13 a traffic stop, a search, an arrest \u8211\'20\'13 could expose the entire network \u8220\'20\'1Cthrough digital connections\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18So much harsher\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 With the supreme court scheduled to hear oral arguments on the most consequential \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case in nearly five decades this week, Conti-Cook is not the only person entertaining such thoughts.\par \par \uc2 If Roe v Wade were to be overturned, at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 states}}}\uc2  hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  either immediately or as quickly as possible, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a pro-choice research organization. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates believe this would probably result in desperate people (who have the resources) seeking abortions in more liberal states where the procedure remains legal, such as Illinois or New York. In just one example, Louisiana has passed a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2020/02/louisiana-has-passed-89-abortion-restrictions-roe-its-about-control-not-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trigger law}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D designed to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe is overturned. A person from Louisiana seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to reach Kansas to obtain one legally.\par \par \uc2 However, most people are unlikely to make that long, expensive and arduous journey. Campaigners warn that poor, young and people of color are far more likely to turn to illegal methods, creating another layer of racism and classism in the criminal justice system, alongside the same preventable public health disaster seen before Roe.\par \par \uc2 Miriam Krinsky, the executive director of Fair and Just Prosecution, a group that educates newly elected local prosecutors, said her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/193080/20210920175211552_19-1392bsacCurrentAndFormerProsecutors.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 group\u8217\'20\'19s brief filed to the supreme court}}}\uc2  regarding the Mississippi case \u8220\'20\'1Creflects a growing recognition that overturning 50 years of precedent and potentially criminalizing personal healthcare decisions could have incredibly dire consequences\u8221\'20\'1D. The brief was co-signed by nearly 100 prosecutors, police and high-ranking former Department of Justice officials.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not just blue, urban-area elected prosecutors and law enforcement leaders \u8211\'20\'13 these are leaders from New York and California and everywhere in between \u8211\'20\'13 urban, rural, red states, blue states. District attorneys in Texas, Georgia, Missouri, Wisconsin,\u8221\'20\'1D said Krinsky, speaking about those who signed the brief. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just a voice that comes from so-called \u8216\'20\'18liberal bastions\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The nation\u8217\'20\'19s preeminent association for defense attorneys has also published a report ahead of the oral arguments that lays out a future in which the US could undertake \u8220\'20\'1Crampant criminalization\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cmass incarceration on an unprecedented scale\u8221\'20\'1D in the name of the unborn.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStates are laying the groundwork now, and have been laying the groundwork for criminal penalties that are completely different,\u8221\'20\'1D than the pre- Roe era, said Lindsay A Lewis, a criminal defense attorney in New York who co-authored a report on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the National Association of Criminal Defense Attorneys (NACDL), the first such report in the organization\u8217\'20\'19s history.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are so much more advanced, and so much harsher than what existed before Roe was enacted.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 State legislatures have spent recent decades \u8220\'20\'1Cmodifying their criminal codes\u8221\'20\'1D in ways that \u8220\'20\'1Ccompletely changes the calculus when it comes to what it would mean to go back to pre- Roe times,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lewis.\par \par \uc2 Criminal charges could come from specific \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, but also from criminal codes that penalize attempted crimes, conspiracies and accomplices to crime, all relics laws developed during the US\u8217\'20\'19s so-called war on drugs. Those laws \u8220\'20\'1Ccould subject a wide range of individuals to criminal penalties if Roe is overturned\u8221\'20\'1D, the NACDL report outlines, including prosecuting people from states where the procedure is illegal who attempt to seek abortions in states where it remains legal.\par \par \uc2 For example, Louisiana law defines an \u8220\'20\'1Caccomplice\u8221\'20\'1D to a crime as anyone involved in its commission, even tangentially, whether \u8220\'20\'1Cpresent or absent\u8221\'20\'1D if they aid, abet or even counsel someone. Experts say this could be deployed against a wide range of friends, loved ones or counselors, such as clergy or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund networks which help shepherd people to clinics.\par \par \uc2 As Lewis and her co-authors laid out, there are thousands of laws like Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s across the country. What\u8217\'20\'19s more, recent prosecutions of pregnant people also show how digital evidence can be used as powerful prosecutorial tools.\par \par \uc2 More than 2,000 police agencies across the US have already purchased \u8220\'20\'1Cmass extraction\u8221\'20\'1D technology that allows them to download, organize and archive a phone\u8217\'20\'19s entire contents. The technology is sometimes called a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.upturn.org/reports/2020/mass-extraction/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 window into the soul}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. This digital evidence has then been used to identify search queries for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, including in the prosecution of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationaladvocatesforpregnantwomen.org/victory-for-latice-fisher-in-mississippi/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latice Fisher}}}\uc2 , a mother of three in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Fisher gave birth to a stillborn child. The child was pronounced dead at Fisher\u8217\'20\'19s home by emergency responders. Prosecutors used Fisher\u8217\'20\'19s cell phone records to show she had searched for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online before her child was stillborn, and brought charges against her twice.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors dropped the first case amid criticism of an archaic test used to determine whether an infant is born alive. Prosecutors then tried to indict Fisher again, but a grand jury refused to indict her when presented with scientifically accurate information.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are many possibilities, and one way that I have tried to imagine what those possibilities could be is looking at the way surveillance technology is used today on investigating crimes related to sex work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Conti-Cook.\par \par \uc2 Conti-Cook said police may choose to set a \u8220\'20\'1Choney pot\u8221\'20\'1D, in which law enforcement sets up a fake website to entice people seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to provide their contact information. They could use \u8220\'20\'1Creverse geo-fencing\u8221\'20\'1D technology, to create a digital border around a location of interest \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps a clinic \u8211\'20\'13 and identify all phones that enter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe danger about a world where the states can criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , again, is that the tools they have to investigate every crime have increased exponentially with surveillance technology,\u8221\'20\'1D said Conti-Cook.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Every single one of these is a human rights violation\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Other American historians, such as Leslie J Reagan, the author of When \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Was a Crime, have warned of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.irishtimes.com/news/health/legacy-of-romania-s-contraception-ban-lives-on-1.958842"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ceausescu-like regimes}}}\uc2  where prenatal care becomes about ensuring \u8220\'20\'1Call pregnancies are progressing to term\u8221\'20\'1D, and authorities monitor menstrual cycles. In Missouri, health department officials have admitted to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/missouri-tracked-planned-parenthood-patients-periods-in-spreadsheet-top-health-official-says/2019/10/30/e96791d0-fb42-11e9-ac8c-8eced29ca6ef_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 monitoring periods}}}\uc2  to identify \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed medical abortions\u8221\'20\'1D, part of a bid to close the state\u8217\'20\'19s last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 In a brief to the supreme court, the United Nations special rapporteur on the right to health warned that overturning Roe v Wade and banning or criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be \u8220\'20\'1Cirreconcilable\u8221\'20\'1D with international human rights laws. Even so, some states have already instituted bans, such as Texas, which banned the vast majority of abortions.\par \par \uc2 Particularly in the south, prosecutions of pregnant people have already taken place recently, even when they have given birth to healthy babies. Advocates said these contested prosecutions, often dropped under public pressure or overturned on appeal, are evidence of the zeal some prosecutors have for criminalizing pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Alabama has prosecuted nearly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2015/09/when_the_womb_is_a_crime_scene.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 500 women}}}\uc2  since 2006 for allegedly exposing a fetus to a \u8220\'20\'1Ccontrolled substance\u8221\'20\'1D in the womb, even including prescription {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/when-the-womb-is-a-crime-scene"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 painkillers}}}\uc2 . The charge carries a potential 10-year prison sentence, if the child is born healthy (more if the baby is not), and the controlled substance was prescribed to the person under investigation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 Alabama supreme court\uc2  in two decisions says we treat the unborn as persons,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lynne Paltrow, executive director of National Advocates for Pregnant Women, which has helped provide counsel to people charged under such statutes.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, courts have said the word \u8220\'20\'1Cchild\u8221\'20\'1D in state laws should be interpreted to include unborn fetuses, meaning people could be held liable for child abuse during pregnancy. Oklahoma recently sentenced 21-year-old Brittney Poolaw to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/health-oklahoma-sentencing-3f6317fecc27d5c5081cb011b202745c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 four years in prison after miscarrying}}}\uc2 , following use of methamphetamine, though there is no evidence drug use caused the miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe most recent published figures cite 24 people since the year 2000 who have been criminally punished for ending a pregnancy or helping a loved one do so,\u8221\'20\'1D said Farah Diaz-Tello, senior counsel and legal director for If/When/How.\par \par \uc2 Diaz-Tello said her organization is now conducting research which will likely prove twice as many people per year have been prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery single one of these is a human rights violation,\u8221\'20\'1D said Diaz-Tello. \u8220\'20\'1CAny non-zero number should be shocking, should be alarming.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211129ehbt000mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
