\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 How Trump reshaped the fifth circuit to become the \u8216\'20\'18most extreme\u8217\'20\'19 US court\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,390 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2021\par 12:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ultra-conservative lawyers seek to have their cases heard in the fifth circuit in hope of a favourable ruling\par \par \uc2 One publicly mourned the \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral tragedy of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. Another suggested that same-sex marriage \u8220\'20\'1Cimperils civic peace\u8221\'20\'1D. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Wilson%20Responses%20to%20QFRs2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A third tweeted negatively}}}\uc2  about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  using the hashtags #CrookedHillary, #basketofdeplorables and #Scandalabra.\par \par \uc2 James Ho, Stuart Kyle Duncan and Cory Wilson are among six judges appointed by former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ca5.uscourts.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US court of appeals for the fifth circuit, }}}\uc2  skewing one of the most conservative \u8211\'20\'13 and influential \u8211\'20\'13 courts in America even further to the right.\par \par \uc2 The consequences of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s reshaping of the federal judiciary are being felt acutely at the fifth circuit on issues ranging from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to immigration to the coronavirus pandemic. The court\u8217\'20\'19s willingness to entertain Republican extremism has effectively made it their principal legal bulwark against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Supreme Court is, no doubt, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful court. But the 5th Circuit, the federal appeals court that covers Louisiana, Mississippi and Texas, is staking out a claim to be the most dangerous,\u8221\'20\'1D Ruth Marcus, deputy editorial page editor of the Washington Post, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/08/31/5th-circuit-is-staking-out-claim-be-americas-most-dangerous-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote in August.}}}\par \par \uc2 The supreme court does indeed have the last word on the constitutionality of contentious laws and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/may/23/us-supreme-court-trump-judicial-appointments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bears Trump\u8217\'20\'19s stamp}}}\uc2  with his three appointees. But the great majority of cases never make it that far. Instead 13 appellate courts, each covering a different region, get to rule on most legal appeals around the country.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.marquette.edu/faculty-and-staff-directory/detail/1996511"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Edward Fallone}}}\uc2 , an associate professor at Marquette University Law School, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re really error-correcting courts. Their function is primarily to correct the trial judge if they made a mistake. They don\u8217\'20\'19t have the power to overturn settled precedent from the supreme court but, if there are cases where there is no precedent and they\u8217\'20\'19re writing on a clean slate, then they get the first crack at defining what the law is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among the 13 appellate courts, which typically put cases before three-judge panels, the US court of appeals for the District of Columbia circuit in Washington is widely regarded as the biggest hitter. On Thursday, for example, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/11/donald-trump-white-house-records-court-capitol-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it temporarily blocked}}}\uc2  the release of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s White House records relating to the 6 January attack on the US Capitol.\par \par \uc2 But the fifth circuit, based in New Orleans, Louisiana, has long shown an ability to punch above its weight. Half a century ago it was seen as a trailblazer as it handled most civil rights cases. In 1964 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https:///[https//web.archive.org/web/20111213232115mp_/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,830884,00.html#ixzz1ce59AGoz]"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Time magazine quoted}}}\uc2  a leading lawyer as saying: \u8220\'20\'1CWithout the Fifth Circuit, we would be on the verge of actual warfare in the South.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s transformation mirrored the politics of the deep south, recruiting from increasingly conservative ranks of judges and legal scholars. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Court_of_Appeals_for_the_Fifth_Circuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Of its 17 active judges today,}}}\uc2  12 were named by Republican presidents.\par \par \uc2 When two vacancies arose during Barack Obama\u8217\'20\'19s presidency, Republicans managed to derail the nomination process and keep the seats open. (In 2015, notably, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2015/11/fifth-circuit-obama-immigration/415077/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the court upheld a decision}}}\uc2  blocking Obama executive orders protecting undocumented immigrants whose children are US citizens.)\par \par \uc2 Then came Trump, who named six judges to the court, more than a third of its total composition. All are relatively young: they include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fedsoc.org/contributors/andrew-oldham"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andrew Oldham,}}}\uc2  a former legal adviser to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s governor, Greg Abbott, who is in his early 40s. Federal judges have lifetime tenure and typically serve long after the presidents who nominated them have left office.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.richmond.edu/faculty/ctobias/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Carl Tobias,}}}\uc2  a law professor at the University of Richmond in Virginia, said of the fifth circuit\u8217\'20\'19s judges: \u8220\'20\'1CThe vast majority have been appointed by Republican presidents going back to \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , George HW Bush and \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 . Of course, the Bushes knew all of these people personally, I think, but Trump has really amped it up because he has chosen even more conservative people than the Bushes did.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This affects which cases the court is likely to hear. Appeals in the ruby red states of Louisiana, Mississippi and Texas naturally go before the fifth circuit. But its hard-right reputation is also an invitation to outsiders for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/forum_shopping"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cforum shopping\u8221\'20\'1D,}}}\uc2  in which a plaintiff or their backers choose a court that will treat their claims most favourably.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://demandjustice.org/christopher-kang/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Christopher Kang}}}\uc2 , co-founder and chief counsel of the progressive pressure group Demand Justice, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen over the last several years is that conservatives have stacked the fifth circuit with very ultra-conservative ideological judges and so, when particularly controversial issues come up, lawyers from across the country find a way to file in the fifth circuit, which then allows it to hear these cases and have an outsized impact on the development of the law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CThis has been a very intentional decision by conservative legal activists to file their cases in the fifth circuit so that they can get the most extreme ruling possible as early as possible in the process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In October the fifth circuit temporarily {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/08/texas-abortion-ban-temporarily-reinstated-latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reinstated Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  law,}}}\uc2  the most extreme in the country, which bars the procedure as early as six weeks into pregnancy and outsources enforcement of the ban to ordinary citizens. Earlier this month it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/11/06/1053234688/appeals-court-temporarily-halts-biden-vaccine-mandate-for-larger-businesses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a stay freezing}}}\uc2  the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to require workers at companies with at least 100 employees be vaccinated against Covid-19 or be tested weekly.\par \par \uc2 Kang remarked: \u8220\'20\'1CTime and time again, the fifth circuit is the place for the most extreme rulings to come forward and then the question is whether or not the supreme court will step in or how the supreme court will react.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So far the fifth circuit has frequently proven too extreme even for a supreme court that has a solid conservative majority. The higher court reversed six of seven decisions by the fifth circuit in the 2019-2020 term and five of seven decisions in the 2020-2021 term. Among them were fifth circuit rulings {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/591/18-1323/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 upholding a Louisiana \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  law}}}\uc2  and striking down the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 Trump appointed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/01/13/how-trump-compares-with-other-recent-presidents-in-appointing-federal-judges/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 200 judges}}}\uc2  to the federal bench, including almost as many federal appeals court judges in four years (54) as Obama did in eight (55) \u8211\'20\'13 partly because \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Republican majority leader in the Senate, thwarted many Obama appointees. Trump \u8220\'20\'1Cflipped\u8221\'20\'1D the balance of several appeals courts to a majority of Republican appointees.\par \par \uc2 Biden, however, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2021/11/03/president-biden-names-ninth-round-of-judicial-nominees/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 set about reversing the trend }}}\uc2  with impressive speed. Kang commented: \u8220\'20\'1CThere definitely is grounds for optimism in that President Biden and Senate Democrats are prioritizing judges like never before on the Democratic side. I do think they are on pace for President Biden to appoint more appellate judges than Trump did in his first year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s emphasis on professional diversity is also encouraging, Kang continued. \u8220\'20\'1CPublic defenders and civil rights lawyers and union-side labour lawyers \u8211\'20\'13 the kinds of lawyers who have been traditionally excluded from the bench are being elevated now and that is tremendously important.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe question is, how many vacancies will President Biden be able to fill? Because these are lifetime judgeships, you\u8217\'20\'19re only able to fill vacancies when a judge retires or passes away. President Biden is filling these vacancies very quickly but at some point there may not be any more vacancies to fill.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Conservatives, however, reject the premise that Trump warped the lower courts so they no longer represent the will of the people on reproductive rights and other issues. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.committeeforjustice.org/curt-levey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Curt Levey}}}\uc2 , president of the advocacy group the Committee for Justice, said: \u8220\'20\'1CPolling indicates that something like 70, 75% basically think that there should be some protection of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that it shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand \u8211\'20\'13 a moderate position which hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been able to be enacted because of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDepending on what the supreme court rules, it and the fifth circuit might very well be right in the middle of American public opinion. I could name a bunch of issues like that so I don\u8217\'20\'19t know that I buy that the fifth circuit is any more out of step with the American people than some of the more liberal circuits. The circuits tend to reflect America because it\u8217\'20\'19s a number of presidents, going back to Reagan, who have appointed the judges.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211115ehbf000xe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
