\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Take it from an Irish woman: if US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights keep slipping, dark days are coming\b0\par\par\uc2 Maeve Higgins \par \uc2 948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2021\par 10:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In 2018, Ireland finally voted to legalize terminations. Before that condoms, divorce and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were illegal and shameful\par \par \uc2 I am a woman in America who can bear children, and this means that there are powerful people coming for me, with detailed and strategic plans to control my body. Sounds dramatic, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t it? It is dramatic, more so because it\u8217\'20\'19s a straight-up fact. In 2021, state legislatures enacted more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions than in any previous year, according to an analysis by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research and policy body dedicated to advancing reproductive rights. Last month\u8217\'20\'19s decision by the supreme court to refuse to block a Texas law all but banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  signals that the court could well be on the way to overturn Roe v Wade, and soon.\par \par \uc2 National legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just one part of this. Reproductive justice advocates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2019/08/16/reproductive-justice-how-women-color-asserted-their-voice-abortion-rights-movement/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as far back as 1994}}}\uc2  understood that when women don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  it generally means that we don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to a whole host of other rights: affordable contraceptives, comprehensive sex education, pre-natal care, even screening and treatment for a variety of diseases including cancer and HIV. This is an overall form of oppression, and I know what\u8217\'20\'19s happening. I also fear I know what\u8217\'20\'19s coming.\par \par \uc2 I live in New York City, I am financially stable and I am white. These factors, as well as legal protections in New York, mean that I get to live a life free from coercion, with access to contraception, to reproductive healthcare, to a medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if I need one. I don\u8217\'20\'19t take this for granted, because this reality is worlds away from where I grew up \u8211\'20\'13 in Ireland, a country that only legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Here in the US, back in 1973, the supreme court affirmed the legality of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to have an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cnn.com/2013/09/18/health/abortion-fast-facts/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  under the 14th amendment to the constitution. Living in this far from perfect nation, I still have the right to make choices about my own health and future, meaning a life with more dignity and autonomy than I had growing up, and that is an extraordinary thing.\par \par \uc2 But even if I don\u8217\'20\'19t yet feel it, the threat of losing this hard-won freedom is all around me. In the words of \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , president and chief executive of the Planned Parenthood Federation of America, \u8220\'20\'1CThe moment is dark \u8230\'20\'26 No matter where you live, no matter where you are, this fight is at your doorstep right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McGill Johnson was speaking on 2 October, as women across the country organized through the Women\u8217\'20\'19s March protested against the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s refusal to block the Texas legislation. Talk about being up against it: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed three conservative justices, meaning that the court now has an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  majority and reproductive justice hangs in the balance. That is why on 4 October, Emma Whittman, a 22-year-old public health student from Arizona sat in the road, blocking traffic outside the supreme court in Washington DC. She was arrested for civil disobedience.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, after a compassionate but fierce campaign, almost two of every three Irish people voted to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not from Texas, but I feel like I\u8217\'20\'19m fighting for people in Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D she told me, as well as people \u8220\'20\'1Cin all of these other states that will probably get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and will be impacted when Roe v Wade is overturned. I feel like I\u8217\'20\'19m fighting for all women around the country.\u8221\'20\'1D It was Whittman\u8217\'20\'19s first arrest and the experience of being searched and held by the police was scary. Concerns about how an arrest and a potential criminal record may affect her future career worried her too. But Whittman was not alone. Her mother, an OB/GYN from Tucson, was there too, reassuring her daughter as she was zip-tied, telling her that she loved her and was proud of her.\par \par \uc2 Women take care of each other. We always have. In Ireland, in the darkest and most oppressive times, when our reproductive rights and our health were out of our hands, we did what we could to make each other safe. In 1980 Irish women could not get condoms, divorce was illegal and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was shameful, illegal and dangerous. In 2018, after a compassionate but fierce campaign, almost two of every three Irish people voted to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today in the US, the moment is indeed dark, and there are darker times ahead. Women, as ever, are fighting against that. Professor Terry McGovern, chair of the department of population and family health at the Columbia University medical center, was also arrested outside the supreme court that day. She points out that Texas already has a severe maternal mortality crisis with a disproportionate effect on Black women.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re not taking care of women and children,\u8221\'20\'1D she told me. \u8220\'20\'1CThey have the worst health outcomes. And then they\u8217\'20\'19re focusing on restricting women and girls and people\u8217\'20\'19s bodily autonomy?\u8221\'20\'1D That is why she showed up on the steps of the supreme court, and that is why she will continue to fight. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has absolutely always been true in history that when a law is as thoroughly amoral as this one, and it will be when they overturn Roe, that we have to do whatever we need to do to protect people\u8217\'20\'19s health.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Maeve Higgins is a comedian and the author of Maeve in America: Essays by a Girl from Somewhere Else\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211102ehb2001mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
