\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Supreme court appears open to allowing challenge to Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  law\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,019 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par 21:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices, in oral arguments, seem skeptical of law that gives private citizens right to sue to enforce six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par \uc2 A majority of the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/oct/22/supreme-court-texas-abortion-ban-arguments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appeared skeptical}}}\uc2  of a controversial Texas law that gives private citizens the right to file civil lawsuits to enforce a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 The threat of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/02/sonia-sotomayor-dissent-texas-abortion-ban-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expensive litigation}}}\uc2  posed by the ban has had a chilling effect and halted the vast majority of abortions in America\u8217\'20\'19s second-largest state, which is home to more than 6 million women of child-bearing age.\par \par \uc2 Over nearly three hours of arguments on Monday the nine-member bench heard from three parties: attorneys for Texas, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/oct/01/texas-abortion-law-federal-judge-biden-administrations-challenge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden administration}}}\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 The focus of arguments was whether Texas can allow private citizens to enforce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in direct contradiction of supreme court precedent in Roe v Wade, the 1973 ruling that provides a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to roughly 24 weeks, and whether the federal government then has the standing to stop the law in court.\par \par \uc2 Although laws deemed unconstitutional are often described as \u8220\'20\'1Cstruck down\u8221\'20\'1D by courts, in fact courts block specific officials from enforcing laws. Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/sep/02/us-supreme-court-texas-abortion-ban-law-joe-biden-us-politics-latest?page=with:block-6130d3908f0830dd895fdc93#block-6130d3908f0830dd895fdc93"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 made it difficult}}}\uc2  for courts to scrutinize the law by making it difficult to identify who they would block from enforcing it, having, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/sep/02/us-supreme-court-texas-abortion-ban-law-joe-biden-us-politics-latest?page=with:block-6130d3908f0830dd895fdc93#block-6130d3908f0830dd895fdc93"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the words}}}\uc2  of chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  last month, \u8220\'20\'1Cdelegated enforcement \u8230\'20\'26 to the populace at large\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/17/texas-abortion-ban-jonathan-mitchell-supreme-court-brief"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 This design}}}\uc2  by the Republican-led Texas legislature represented a purposeful effort to evade federal court scrutiny. While \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the constitutional right at issue, debate focused on the legality of the structure of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law, rather than the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The court heard two separate challenges to the Texas law during {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/oct/22/supreme-court-texas-abortion-ban-arguments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 oral arguments}}}\uc2 . Three liberal justices had already said they believed the law was unconstitutional. A fourth, the conservative Roberts, had said in the past he would have blocked the law while lower courts considered it. And two more justices, conservatives Amy Coney-Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , who both joined the bench during the Trump administration, had critical questions for attorneys representing Texas\u8217\'20\'19s case.\par \par \uc2 In separate suits, the Biden administration and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/oct/15/texas-abortion-ban-supreme-court-biden-administration-request"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked the court to block}}}\uc2  the law, claiming it is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/04/texas-abortion-ruling-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 incompatible with Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , caused ongoing and immediate harm to people in Texas seeking abortions, and was designed to evade court scrutiny in a way that threatened other constitutional rights. Attorneys for Texas argued \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers\u8217\'20\'19 conflict was with Congress, and blocking courts from accepting lawsuits related to the law, known as SB8, threatened the US system of government.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very difficult to find that relief, because Texas designed the law specifically to thwart it,\u8221\'20\'1D said the US solicitor general, \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe clear purpose and actual effect [of the law] has been to chill the right\u8221\'20\'1D to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Marc Hearron, attorney for the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , representing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas, argued the court should enjoin Texas court clerks from docketing cases, arguing the threat of litigation was the very point of SB8.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a unique law created because the state has delegated enforcement and taken away the normal executive officials who would enforce, and has weaponized the court system,\u8221\'20\'1D Hearron said.\par \par \uc2 SB8 is structured to favor plaintiffs in several ways. It provides plaintiffs a $10,000 fine for violations, plus attorney\u8217\'20\'19s fees. Defendants, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, are not eligible for any such fees if a case is found to be frivolous. It also allows plaintiffs to sue in any county in Texas, which could force defendants into far-flung courts, and for multiple suits to be brought for the same alleged violation. Furthermore, it allows anyone to sue, not just people who might be somehow hurt by the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hearron argued this made anyone involved in providing abortions a \u8220\'20\'1Cpermanent defendant\u8221\'20\'1D. Later, he added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe state has made the clerks an essential role in this machinery they have made.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Coney Barrett, who appeared skeptical of the enforcement mechanism, asked the Texas solicitor general, Judd E Stone II, whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers would need to bar citizens from suing one-by-one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou cannot get global relief,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barrett. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s on an individual by individual basis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stone responded that Barrett was effectively correct: each individual citizen would need to be enjoined from enforcing the law. Justices then asked whether the attorney general of Texas could be enjoined, and thus block further lawsuits from being filed, on the basis that citizens were acting as private attorneys general. Stone said no.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe has none of those sort of mechanisms whatsoever,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stone, and added the state had \u8220\'20\'1Cno authority\u8221\'20\'1D to stop private citizens\u8217\'20\'19 suits.\par \par \uc2 Another conservative on the bench, \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , said the Texas law \u8220\'20\'1Cexploited\u8221\'20\'1D a loophole in court precedent concerning when state officials can be barred from enforcing unconstitutional laws. He wondered if the court should \u8220\'20\'1Cclose that loophole\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Elena Kagan, a liberal justice, sarcastically said the law was written by \u8220\'20\'1Csome geniuses\u8221\'20\'1D to evade the broad legal principle that \u8220\'20\'1Cstates are not to nullify federal constitutional rights\u8221\'20\'1D. She argued that if Texas\u8217\'20\'19s argument prevailed, states would be invited to nullify other rights in the same way.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGuns, same-sex marriage, religious rights, whatever you don\u8217\'20\'19t like: go ahead.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Only two conservative justices, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , appeared moved by Texas\u8217\'20\'19s arguments. Gorsuch suggested attorneys for reproductive rights groups were \u8220\'20\'1Cline drawing\u8221\'20\'1D between the Texas law and others laws which constrain constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 The hearing came a month before arguably the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case in five decades: Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization. In that case, the court will consider whether Mississippi can ban nearly all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks, roughly nine weeks earlier than Roe v Wade permits.\par \par \uc2 Both the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision not to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law and its acceptance of the Dobbs case are viewed as an ominous signs for the future of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 Reuters contributed to this report\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211101ehb10015v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\