\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 How America\u8217\'20\'19s broken democracy led to our  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 Meaghan Winter \par \uc2 1,074 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2021\par 18:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The majority of Americans support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Redistricting has allowed extremism to flourish without fear of repercussion\par \par \uc2 America is at a crossroads when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 2021, state legislatures have passed an unprecedented 106 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills. State lawmakers in five states are preparing legislation similar to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s SB 8, an effective total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that enshrines a new kind of vigilantism directed at medical providers and private citizens.\par \par \uc2 In this dangerous moment, supporters of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must understand that raising our voices is not going to change anything unless we also push for major, immediate democratic reforms including ending the filibuster, enshrining federal voting rights, expanding the supreme court and establishing fair redistricting.\par \par \uc2 I understand why those goals may simultaneously seem too wonky to follow and too ambitious to achieve. But we cannot fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights without first repairing our democracy, because we will continue to lose.\par \par \uc2 The conservative movement and its ideological and corporate patrons have locked in structural power at nearly every level of government, and our lawmakers don\u8217\'20\'19t need to be responsive to public opinion or even long-enshrined civil and human rights. If we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have any chance of protecting ourselves and each other, on numerous urgent fronts, we need to agitate for immediate, ambitious democratic reforms that will ensure that our courts uphold our rights, and our elected officials are responsive to the will of the people. Otherwise, our rallies are collective screams into the void.\par \par \uc2 Many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have moved away from calling themselves \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D and instead have embraced the reproductive justice model, which defines itself as a movement to ensure the human right to bodily autonomy and to parent or not parent in a safe and sustainable community. Current threats to our democracy make crystal clear that the struggles for reproductive freedom, voting rights and economic, racial and climate justice are inextricably linked.\par \par \uc2 When I first began reporting on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in 2013, when I\u8217\'20\'19d ask \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates why extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state lawmakers seemed unafraid of running afoul of the majority of the American public, which supports legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they would answer, \u8220\'20\'1Cgerrymandering\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As I soon learned, because Republicans have gerrymandered districts in states across the nation, it no longer matters whether their policies defy most voters\u8217\'20\'19 beliefs and needs, because incumbents\u8217\'20\'19 seats are safe almost no matter what.\par \par \uc2 What we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing now accelerated after the 2010 election, which had existential ramifications for our democracy only now becoming visible. Ahead of that election, during an all-important redistricting year, the Republican party and conservative and corporate donors heavily invested in state-level elections so that they could gerrymander and give themselves a competitive advantage for a decade. It worked. They flipped legislative chambers across the country, and states started ramping up their envelope-pushing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills, as well as voting restrictions designed to make it more difficult for voters to throw them out of office.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Democrats failed to flip a single state-level chamber. Republicans now control 30 legislatures during yet another redistricting year, jeopardizing any chance of a progressive agenda in many states as well as Democratic control of Congress next year.\par \par \uc2 As states begin passing ever more extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and even bans, there\u8217\'20\'19s little reason to believe that the courts will stop them unless Congress gets serious about reforming the court system.\par \par \uc2 During the Trump administration, Republicans installed an unprecedented number of federal judges, many of them open ideologues with little experience on the bench, reshaping the judiciary for a generation. And, in case you\u8217\'20\'19ve forgotten, McConnell blocked Obama\u8217\'20\'19s nominee \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , changed the Senate rules for confirming justices to push through \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , ushered through the confirmation of \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  despite credible accusations of sexual assault against him, and rushed through the confirmation of Amy Coney Barrett while voting in the 2020 election had already begun.\par \par \uc2 The radical takeover of the courts was not a random fluke but the result of careful plotting and hundreds of millions of dollars investment by rightwing ideologues and billionaires \u8211\'20\'13 the same kind of long-term strategizing to change the rules of the game their allies used to gerrymander states and congressional districts.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the same movement of extreme, partisan donors and strategists behind the ever-more radical state laws has also installed federal judges and supreme court justices who are poised to uphold those laws. The issue isn\u8217\'20\'19t whether expanding the supreme court will throw into doubt the court\u8217\'20\'19s legitimacy. The supreme court is already partisan and ideological and therefore illegitimate. What\u8217\'20\'19s needed now is a major and swift corrective.\par \par \uc2 That brings us to Congress. What about a federal law enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights? To achieve that \u8211\'20\'13 and so much more, including expanding the supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 Congress needs to end the filibuster, the rule that requires 60 members of the Senate to pass legislation instead of 50. Our current Senate delivers nothing close to fair representation, which, as I write this, is on painful display as Republicans have filibustered yet another urgently needed voting rights bill, while two Democratic senators representing small states have killed provisions in the Build Back Better package that are \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D in the most literal sense \u8211\'20\'13 in support of healthcare and a viable planet. To have any chance of achieving reproductive justice in this country, we need to agitate for our members of Congress to end the filibuster now.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s time to re-examine what we consider pragmatic. Sticking with the status quo means surrendering to the profound irony that a movement that branded itself as \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D has helped usher in a ruling class committed not only to stripping away the social safety net but also doubling down on fossil fuels and imperiling the very existence of life on Earth.\par \par \uc2 Trying to advocate for reproductive justice without also demanding that our lawmakers immediately reform our voting laws, Congress and the courts that have been rigged by corporate and authoritarian interest groups isn\u8217\'20\'19t practical or hopeful \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s misguided if not delusional. Instead, supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights must join the chorus calling to end the filibuster and expand the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Meaghan Winter is a freelance magazine writer and author of the book All Politics is Local: Why Progressives Must Fight for the States\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211025ehap001gu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
