\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 Supreme court refuses to block Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban but will hear challenges\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza and agencies \par \uc2 726 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2021\par 14:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices will hear arguments on 1 November and said they will decide whether the federal government has the right to sue\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  allowed a Texas law that bans the vast majority of abortions to temporarily remain in effect, but will hear arguments on 1 November. The law, known as Senate Bill 8, bans the procedure after roughly six weeks gestation or before most women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The justices said they will decide whether the federal government has the right to sue over the law. The court\u8217\'20\'19s action leaves in place, for the time being, a law Texas clinics say has led to an 80% reduction in abortions in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s second-largest state.\par \par \uc2 The refusal by the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority to block the law while oral arguments are prepared was excoriated by the liberal justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , who called the decision \u8220\'20\'1Ccold comfort\u8221\'20\'1D to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s 6 million women of reproductive age.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court is right to calendar this application for argument \u8230\'20\'26 in recognition of the public importance of the issues these cases raise,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Sotomayor. \u8220\'20\'1CThe promise of future adjudication offers cold comfort, however, for Texas women seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Texas, SB8 has been in effect since September, aside from a district court-ordered pause that lasted 48 hours. It bans abortions once cardiac activity is detected, usually about six weeks.\par \par \uc2 While courts have blocked other state laws effectively banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus can survive outside the womb, the Texas law has so far avoided a similar fate because it leaves enforcement up to private citizens, a move that many critics have said effectively creates anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty hunters.\par \par \uc2 The law allows anyone, anywhere to bring a suit against anyone who helps a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and provides a $10,000 penalty against defendants found to violate the law. Defendants cannot recoup anything if they prevail in court.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, in its final pitch to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19s ban on most abortions, warned the supreme court that none of its decisions would be safe if it allows the Texas law to remain in force.\par \par \uc2 If the law stays in effect, \u8220\'20\'1Cno decision of this court is safe. States need not comply with, or even challenge, precedents with which they disagree. They may simply outlaw the exercise of whatever rights they disfavor,\u8221\'20\'1D the administration wrote in a brief filed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Other state-enforced bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the point at which a fetus can survive outside the womb have been blocked by courts because they conflict with supreme court precedents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTexas should not obtain a different result simply by pairing its unconstitutional law with an unprecedented enforcement scheme designed to evade the traditional mechanisms for judicial review,\u8221\'20\'1D the administration wrote.\par \par \uc2 A day earlier, the state urged the court to leave the law in place, saying the federal government lacked the authority to file its lawsuit challenging the Texas ban.\par \par \uc2 The justice department filed suit over the law after the supreme court rejected an earlier effort by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to put the measure on hold temporarily.\par \par \uc2 In early October, US district judge \uc2 Robert Pitman\uc2  ruled for the administration, putting the law on hold and allowing abortions to resume. Two days later, a three-judge panel of the fifth US circuit court of appeals put the law back into effect.\par \par \uc2 Texas said it opposed the early review by the supreme court, but that if the justices agree to the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s request, they also should use this case to directly overrule the Roe and Casey decisions.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is already slated to hear arguably the most consequential \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case in decades, a case called Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, which challenges Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and will be heard on 1 December.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs case is seen as a direct challenge to Roe v Wade, the landmark 1973 case that legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally to the point a fetus can survive outside the womb, generally regarded at 24 weeks gestation. The decision has protected the right to terminate a pregnancy for nearly five decades, including in hostile states which have worked zealously to, once more, make the procedure illegal within their borders.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211025ehap002pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
