\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden urges supreme court to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19 near-total  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 650 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2021\par 13:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The justice department wrote in its plea that the law defies the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s major decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has asked the supreme court to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19 extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban as a battle over its constitutionality plays out in the courts.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, which has halted most abortions in the state, defies the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s major decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8220\'20\'1Cby banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  long before viability \u8211\'20\'13 indeed, before many women even realize they are pregnant\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 US justice department\uc2  wrote in its plea to the court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe question now is whether Texas\u8217\'20\'19 nullification of this court\u8217\'20\'19s precedents should be allowed to continue while the courts consider the United States\u8217\'20\'19 suit. As the district court recognized, it should not,\u8221\'20\'1D the justice department wrote.\par \par \uc2 The law has been in effect since September, aside from a district court-ordered pause that lasted just 48 hours, and bans abortions once cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks.\par \par \uc2 The administration also took the unusual step of telling the justices they could grant the Texas law full review and decide its fate this term, which already includes a significant case about the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US.\par \par \uc2 No court has yet reached a decision on the constitutionality of the Texas law, and the supreme court rarely grants such requests.\par \par \uc2 The administration said the court could short-circuit the usual process and rule on the law\u8217\'20\'19s constitutionality this term, even though lower courts have yet to do so.\par \par \uc2 The justices have done this only a handful of times in recent decades, the last occasion being a 2019 dispute over the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s ultimately failed effort to include a citizenship question on the 2020 census. In that case, a deadline for finalizing the census was fast approaching.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the administration said, Texas\u8217\'20\'19 attempt to evade federal court review of its law and the possibility that other states could adopt similar measures justify the court\u8217\'20\'19s early involvement.\par \par \uc2 The high court ordered Texas to respond by midday Thursday.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said at Monday\u8217\'20\'19s news briefing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and that the justice department would lead efforts to ensure that people have \u8220\'20\'1Caccess to fundamental rights that they have to protect their own health\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not clear whether the administration will prevail at a supreme court with a conservative majority that has been fortified by three appointees of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The supreme court has already agreed to hear a challenge to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, a case from Mississippi that is considered the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most direct threat}}}\uc2  to women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights in nearly five decades.\par \par \uc2 While courts have blocked other state laws effectively banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus can survive outside the womb, roughly about 24 weeks, the Texas law has so far avoided a similar fate because of its unique structure, which leaves enforcement up to private citizens, rather than state officials. Anyone who brings a successful lawsuit against an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider for violating the law is entitled to claim at least $10,000 in damages.\par \par \uc2 The court has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/01/us/supreme-court-texas-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already declined}}}\uc2  once to block the law from taking effect, acknowledging in an unsigned order that there were \u8220\'20\'1Cserious questions regarding the constitutionality of the Texas law\u8221\'20\'1D but also \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplex and novel\u8221\'20\'1D procedural questions about whom to sue and whether federal courts had the power to stop the law from being enforced.\par \par \uc2 In a dissenting opinion after the 5-4 vote, chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  wrote that he would have put the \u8220\'20\'1Cunprecedented\u8221\'20\'1D law on hold so that court could consider \u8220\'20\'1Cwhether a state can avoid responsibility for its laws\u8221\'20\'1D by handing off enforcement. The court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices also dissented.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211019ehaj0015w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
