\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The courts have a new chance to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  law. They must take it\b0\par\par\uc2 Laurence H Tribe, Erwin Chemerinsky, Jeffrey Abramson and Dennis Aftergut \par \uc2 1,058 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2021\par 10:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 SB 8 not only stripped Texan women of their rights under Roe v Wade, it made a mockery of the US constitution and the supremacy of the federal courts\par \par \uc2 Sadly, predictably and appallingly, on October 14, a three judge panel of the US court of appeals for the fifth circuit has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/context/5th-circuit-declines-to-block-texas-abortion-law/1b007fb0-cc54-4692-a96b-7440a8f1ac05/?itid=lk_inline_manual_5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allowed}}}\uc2  Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CBounty-Hunter\u8221\'20\'1D anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to go back into effect while the court considers the case on the merits. Every day that the fifth circuit panel\u8217\'20\'19s unlawful order keeps the statute in operation brings irreversible injury to women in Texas. US Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has properly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/courts_law/doj-texas-abortion-ban-supreme-court/2021/10/15/bd5762e6-2dcc-11ec-8ef6-3ca8fe943a92_story.html?utm_source=alert&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=wp_news_alert_revere&location=alert&wpmk=1&wpisrc=al_news__alert-politics--alert-national&pwapi_token=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..xXth80rz7eZdfB-3Yuz14TBrRzWUDQ7UOyTSMTLWouc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decided}}}\uc2  to seek emergency relief from the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The justice department is right to accuse the State of Texas of seeking to destroy not only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but also the foundation of our constitutional Republic. In a nation whose history is fraught with battles between states\u8217\'20\'19 rights and national sovereignty, the case of United States v Texas raises issues basic to our national compact.\par \par \uc2 Texas set the current controversy in motion by passing SB8, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that legislators knew was unconstitutional. In doing so, they violated what Chief Justice Marshall explained two centuries ago was the bedrock of our young nation\u8217\'20\'19s rule of law \u8211\'20\'13 that our constitution reigns supreme.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSenate bill 8 (SB8) flouts that principle,\u8221\'20\'1D Monday\u8217\'20\'19s DoJ brief in the fifth circuit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fingfx.thomsonreuters.com/gfx/legaldocs/dwvkradrqpm/DOJ-oppo-Texas-stay-ca5-abortion-2021-10-11.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reads}}}\uc2 . The law does that \u8220\'20\'1Cby blatantly violating constitutional rights and severely constraining judicial review of its unconstitutional restrictions.\u8221\'20\'1D That \u8220\'20\'1Csets this case apart.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Put bluntly, Texas has sought not only to virtually eliminate women\u8217\'20\'19s rights under Roe v Wade, but also to reduce our Constitution\u8217\'20\'19s supremacy to a relic. Those twin dangers are why the stakes are high in the suit by the United States to enjoin the Texas anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statute. And that\u8217\'20\'19s why the October 14 Fifth Circuit order keeping the law in effect is so troubling.\par \par \uc2 This case stands on a very different footing from the one that a conservative 5-4 supreme court rejected on September 1 on procedural grounds. With the United States now suing, there is plenty of precedent for the federal government to come into court challenging a state law before it is enforced, and a state cannot hide behind sovereign immunity as a defense. The cases that the fifth circuit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/context/5th-circuit-declines-to-block-texas-abortion-law/1b007fb0-cc54-4692-a96b-7440a8f1ac05/?itid=lk_inline_manual_5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cited}}}\uc2  on Friday as reasons for refusing to block SB8 were entirely inapplicable because they have no relevance to a suit brought by the United States to force a recalcitrant state to obey the constitution.\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s reason for not arguing SB8\u8217\'20\'19s constitutionality is obvious. The supreme court has affirmed many times since Roe v Wade in 1973 that states cannot prohibit abortions before the fetus is viable and capable of surviving outside the womb. Viability occurs at about the 24th week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law makes all abortions illegal, without exceptions for rape or incest, once fetal cardiac activity can be detected \u8211\'20\'13 usually around six weeks after a woman\u8217\'20\'19s last menstrual period.\par \par \uc2 The fact that the law is enforced by vigilantes\u8217\'20\'19 private civil suits rather than by government prosecutions only aggravates its unconstitutionality. It is a Texas law that opens Texas courts to these bounty-hunting lawsuits. Since 1948, it has been settled law that individuals may not use state courts to deprive others of constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, 6 October, in a 113-page {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/21079947-pitman-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opinion}}}\uc2 , with some of the strongest language ever heard from a federal judge, US district court Judge \uc2 Robert Pitman\uc2  blocked Texas from enforcing this near-total ban on abortions. Judge Pitman\u8217\'20\'19s opinion explained that Texas concocted a transparent \u8220\'20\'1Cscheme\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1Cend run\u8221\'20\'1D the constitution. The court laid out the elaborate \u8220\'20\'1Cmachinations\u8221\'20\'1D Texas devised to avoid a court doing anything about a clearly unconstitutional law.\par \par \uc2 Judge Pitman also documented cases of women \u8211\'20\'13 sometimes minors \u8211\'20\'13 suffering \u8220\'20\'1Cgrievous wrong\u8221\'20\'1D, as they are forced to carry unwanted pregnancies or travel, if they can afford it, to another state to access their constitutional rights: \u8220\'20\'1CThe court can only speculate as to the hardships\u8221\'20\'1D these women have \u8220\'20\'1Chad to endure\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Having temporarily reinstated SB 8, the Fifth Circuit noted that it will expedite review of the merits of Judge Pitman\u8217\'20\'19s decision. That could affect the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s consideration of emergency relief to the United States. Whether now or later, this case will land on the court\u8217\'20\'19s docket.\par \par \uc2 Even justices who disagree with Roe v Wade should recognize the dire implications of letting any state deliberately design a blatantly unconstitutional statute in such a way that no court can block its enforcement until it\u8217\'20\'19s too late to prevent the statute from doing irreparable harm by deterring people from exercising their rights.\par \par \uc2 In the 1950s, states tried to disregard supreme court decisions interpreting the constitution when they engaged in a concerted effort to thwart desegregation orders. Then, too, the \uc2 United States government\uc2  interceded against the states. When the Arkansas governor Orval Faubus attempted to block desegregation, the supreme court, in Cooper v Aaron, unanimously and emphatically reaffirmed the supremacy of the constitution and federal law.\par \par \uc2 The court declared: \u8220\'20\'1CNo state legislator or executive or judicial officer can war against the constitution without violating his undertaking to support it.\u8221\'20\'1D All nine justices joined in declaring: \u8220\'20\'1CIf the legislatures of the several states may, at will, annul the judgments of the courts of the United States, and destroy the rights acquired under those judgments, the constitution itself becomes a solemn mockery.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That would be the result if Texas could destroy the constitutional rights of women before any court could enjoin its devious scheme. To ensure the constitution remains the supreme law of the land, and to protect all rights it guarantees, the fifth circuit and the supreme court must uphold Judge Pitman\u8217\'20\'19s injunction.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Laurence H Tribe is the Carl M Loeb University Professor emeritus and a professor of constitutional law emeritus at Harvard Law School. Erwin Chemerinsky is the dean of the School of Law at the University of California, Berkeley. Jeffrey Abramson is Professor of Law and Government at the \uc2 University of Texas, Austin\uc2 . Dennis Aftergut is a former federal prosecutor\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211017ehah000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
