\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  doctor in Texas. My patients are desperate\b0\par\par\uc2 Samuel Dickman \par \uc2 682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par 17:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Just as predicted, poor women and victims of sexual assault and abuse are suffering most under Texas\u8217\'20\'19s draconian new law\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m a Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. Since the state\u8217\'20\'19s new anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law \u8211\'20\'13 known as Senate Bill 8 \u8211\'20\'13 went into effect three weeks ago, my job transformed from physician to dystopian travel agent for a journey that, for most of my patients, will probably never happen.\par \par \uc2 SB8 is not just a set of roadblocks but rather a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Politicians have rendered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care impossible to access for people who can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to travel hundreds of miles out of state. Many of those who can\u8217\'20\'19t get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be pushed deeper into poverty as a result.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/02/16/opinion/medicaid-abortion-planned-parenthood-texas.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written}}}\uc2  previously about the ways that the US, and Texas especially, segregates care by class and race. This \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is no exception: wealthy Texans will find the means to obtain safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, as they always have. But patients I care for, who are mostly low-income and people of color, can\u8217\'20\'19t afford enormous travel expenses to get medical care. They can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to take multiple days off work, usually without pay. They can\u8217\'20\'19t afford the additional childcare or elder care. They can\u8217\'20\'19t afford the gas money, or the bus fare. They can\u8217\'20\'19t afford a safe place to sleep overnight. These costs are on top of the actual fee for the procedure, often $500 or more out of pocket, a sum that for many patients takes weeks or months to save. Most of my patients now can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but they also can\u8217\'20\'19t afford not to.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I had to tell a young woman already seven weeks pregnant that her only option for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an eight-hour drive to New Mexico. (I\u8217\'20\'19ve changed patient details to protect confidentiality.) She has no car and no access to a checking account. She sobbed as she explained that her father, whom she lives with, would probably become violent if he found out she got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Just over a month ago, I could have provided a medical intervention that protected her autonomy and privacy, in a way that kept her safe from an abusive father. Imagine me now: bearing witness to her plight, powerless to help \u8211\'20\'13 not for lack of tools but because of legal obstruction \u8211\'20\'13 and having to tell her about her limited options. Now imagine being her and having to hear them.\par \par \uc2 In the last days before SB8 went into effect, I provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a patient whose pregnancy was the result of being raped. I was able to help her end her pregnancy safely, and privately. If a patient like her came to see me today, there would be no legal option to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas. And because of the enormous cost of getting care outside of Texas, they would very likely end up having to carry that pregnancy to term.\par \par \uc2 This fight is not just about Texas. As other states pass copycat laws, the US will quickly become a patchwork of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  deserts, further isolating low-income patients like mine. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w26662/w26662.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Research}}}\uc2  shows that people denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are far more likely to experience serious adverse financial events like evictions and bankruptcies.\par \par \uc2 Enough. We must guarantee access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care at the national level by passing the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act, which would prohibit state-level bans and unnecessary restrictions on providers. We must also eliminate the Hyde amendment, which senselessly prohibits all federal programs like Medicaid from covering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, once again targeting those with the lowest incomes. And in the long term we must work to provide coverage of all reproductive health services, including contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , through a universal health program.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be a luxury available only to the rich \u8211\'20\'13 and that includes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Samuel Dickman, an internist, is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider and health policy researcher in Texas\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211008eha8002k4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
