\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US judge temporarily blocks Texas\u8217\'20\'19s near-total  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban in blow to contentious law\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 911 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par 16:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Judge excoriates \u8216\'20\'18unprecedented scheme\u8217\'20\'19 to deny women \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right as law faces uncertain future\par \par \uc2 A US federal judge has temporarily blocked the near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas, dealing the first legal blow against the contentious law and throwing its future into uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 The law, known as Senate Bill 8, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banned most abortions}}}\uc2  in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s second-most populous state and, until now, had withstood a wave of early challenges.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, which stems from a challenge brought by the Biden administration, will prevent the state from enforcing the Republican-backed law while litigation over its legality continues. But even with the law on hold, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in Texas may not instantly resume because doctors still fear that they could be sued without a more permanent legal decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTonight\u8217\'20\'19s ruling is an important step forward toward restoring the constitutional rights of women across the state of Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said in a statement late on Wednesday. \u8220\'20\'1CThe fight has only just begun, both in Texas and in many states across this country where women\u8217\'20\'19s rights are currently under attack.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Texas officials are likely to seek a swift reversal from the fifth US circuit court of appeals, which previously allowed the restrictions to take effect.\par \par \uc2 The law, signed by Republican governor Greg Abbott in May, prohibits abortions once cardiac activity is detected, which is usually around six weeks, before someone can even know they are pregnant. To enforce the law, Texas deputized private citizens to file lawsuits against violators, and has entitled them to at least $10,000 in damages if successful.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit was brought by the Biden administration, which has said the restrictions were enacted in defiance of the US constitution. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/oct/01/texas-abortion-law-federal-judge-biden-administrations-challenge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden administration argued}}}\uc2  that Texas has waged an attack on the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA state may not ban abortions at six weeks. Texas knew this, but it wanted a six-week ban anyway, so the state resorted to an unprecedented scheme of vigilante justice that was designed to scare \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and others who might help women exercise their constitutional rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brian Netter, justice department attorney, to the federal court on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In a 113-page opinion, judge Robert Pitman took Texas to task over the law, saying Republicans lawmakers had \u8220\'20\'1Ccontrived an unprecedented and transparent statutory scheme\u8221\'20\'1D to deny patients their constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrom the moment SB8 went into effect, women have been unlawfully prevented from exercising control over their lives in ways that are protected by the constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Pitman, who was appointed to the bench by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat other courts may find a way to avoid this conclusion is theirs to decide; this court will not sanction one more day of this offensive deprivation of such an important right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers say their fears have become reality in the short time the law has been in effect. Planned Parenthood says the number of patients from Texas at its clinics in the state decreased by nearly 80% in the two weeks after the law took effect.\par \par \uc2 Some providers have said that Texas clinics are now in danger of closing while neighboring states struggle to keep up with a surge of patients who must drive hundreds of miles. Others, they say, are being forced to carry pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 Other states, mostly in the South, have passed similar laws that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the early weeks of pregnancy, all of which judges have blocked. But Texas\u8217\'20\'19 version has so far outmaneuvered the courts because it leaves enforcement to private citizens to file suits, not prosecutors, which critics say amounts to a bounty.\par \par \uc2 At least one {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/20/texas-abortion-law-san-antonio-doctor-sued"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  provider}}}\uc2  has admitted to violating the law and been sued but not by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. Former attorneys in Illinois and Arkansas say they sued a San Antonio doctor in hopes of getting a judge who would invalidate the law.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law is just one that has set up the biggest test of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US in decades, and it is part of a broader push by Republicans nationwide to impose new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  begins a new term, which in December will include arguments in Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s bid to overturn 1973\u8217\'20\'19s landmark Roe v Wade decision guaranteeing the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last month, the court did not rule on the constitutionality of the Texas law in allowing it to remain in place. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers took that 5-4 vote as an ominous sign about where the court might be heading on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its conservative majority was fortified with three appointees from Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the new supreme court term, Planned Parenthood on Friday released a report saying that if Roe v Wade were overturned, 26 states are primed to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This year alone, nearly 600 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have been introduced in statehouses nationwide, with more than 90 becoming law, according to Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 In a statement following Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s order, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PPFA/status/1445923675761352710"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organization tweeted}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been 36 days since Texas deprived its citizens of their constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The relief granted by the court today is overdue. We will continue fighting this ban in court, until we are certain that Texans\u8217\'20\'19 ability to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protected.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211007eha70020b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
