\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t say \u8216\'20\'18privilege\u8217\'20\'19: can the left find better words for talking with people on the right?\b0\par\par\uc2 Sadiya Ansari \par \uc2 2,579 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2021\par 19:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Polarizing words may be throwing progressives off course \u8211\'20\'13 but research might hold lessons for the left on how to have more meaningful conversations\par \par \uc2 When Karin Tamerius first started arguing with her elderly uncle about rightwing memes he was posting on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , she surprised herself. She found herself launching personal attacks, making him feel he was objectively \u8220\'20\'1Cbad\u8221\'20\'1D for holding certain beliefs \u8211\'20\'13 behaviors she knew through her work as a therapist were unfair at worst, and didn\u8217\'20\'19t work at best. But she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t stop herself. She felt she had a moral imperative to act, a compulsion to make the case for her side.\par \par \uc2 In the lead-up to the 2016 election, Tamerius saw her uncle drifting into more extreme rightwing rhetoric thanks to his exposure to Fox, Breitbart and social media. They fought so much that eventually they stopped speaking.\par \par \uc2 Then Trump won the election, and Tamerius began to question her approach to politics and her persuasion strategies.\par \par \uc2 She started reaching out to her uncle and his friends to understand him \u8211\'20\'13 and half the country \u8211\'20\'13 better. Scouring through his \uc2 Facebook\uc2  posts, she found willing participants in conversations about gun policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and realized they had more in common than she imagined. She also realized how words had become a barrier between them.\par \par \uc2 Using the term \u8220\'20\'1Cgun control\u8221\'20\'1D could set people off, but making a small change \u8211\'20\'13 using the term \u8220\'20\'1Cgun safety\u8221\'20\'1D instead \u8211\'20\'13 opened up the conversation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe most important thing I learned was how much of the division between us is an illusion, that it has far more to do with speaking a different language and communicating poorly than then it does with an actual difference of opinion,\u8221\'20\'1D says Tamerius.\par \par \uc2 Of course, there are \u8211\'20\'13 and should be \u8211\'20\'13 instances of irreconcilable differences. People shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to debate their right to exist in the world as themselves; or whether they should be treated with respect and dignity. Racism is not a debate, for example, nor is being a trans person. Being open-minded isn\u8217\'20\'19t the same as letting people with archaic ideas bulldoze a conversation.\par \par \uc2 But Tamerius realized certain words she commonly leaned on \u8211\'20\'13 like \u8220\'20\'1Cprivilege\u8221\'20\'1D, or \u8220\'20\'1Ctriggering\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 were creating a wall between her and those who weren\u8217\'20\'19t as steeped in the discourse that came naturally to her.\par \par \uc2 She started a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group called Smart Politics to strategize how to have healthy political conversations with other left-leaning Americans. And that\u8217\'20\'19s when the penny dropped: \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t just have trouble talking with people on the other end of the political spectrum,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have trouble talking with people on the left.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Victims and villains\par \par \uc2 While progressives desire ambitious, structural change, it\u8217\'20\'19s surprising how often arguments descend over minutiae: the internet abounds with articles lambasting people for using the wrong terms, like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsweek.com/bipoc-isnt-doing-what-you-think-its-doing-opinion-1582494"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPOC\u8221\'20\'1D instead of \u8220\'20\'1Cbipoc\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  , or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/magazine/how-privilege-became-a-provocation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not recognizing one\u8217\'20\'19s privilege}}}\uc2  before speaking up. Other times, people simply call something \u8220\'20\'1Cproblematic\u8221\'20\'1D rather than defining the actual criticism.\par \par \uc2 While the right can cry wolf about \u8220\'20\'1Ccancel culture\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 many have landed lucrative book deals and TV slots after they complained about it \u8211\'20\'13 there is a valid reason to be wary of putting people off with words. If the left wants to ensure broad inclusion in political movements, it needs to talk to a broad range of people.\par \par \uc2 The left increasingly finds itself dividing rather than building allies \u8211\'20\'13 the massive rift in the Democratic party between traditional liberals and the new left being a prime example.\par \par \uc2 A 2017 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2017/10/24/political-typology-reveals-deep-fissures-on-the-right-and-left/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pew Research Center study}}}\uc2  tracking political typology in the US notes that while the gulf between values held by Democrats and Republicans is the widest in two decades, the fissures within both parties are also increasingly visible.\par \par \uc2 The report outlines four distinct groups within the Democratic party: two are more affluent and mostly white, while two are less affluent, \u8220\'20\'1Cmajority-minority\u8221\'20\'1D groups. The two groups who are more affluent are more likely to believe that life in the US is better for people like them compared to 50 years ago, while the two other groups disagree.\par \par \uc2 Concurrently, the influence of traditional Democratic constituents such as trade unions has waned , and blue collar workers typically aligned with the left were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/meet-the-press/gop-rapidly-becoming-blue-collar-party-here-s-what-means-n1258468"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  more likely to vote}}}\uc2  for Trump in the last election \u8211\'20\'13 especially those {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/news/daily-comment/bidens-and-the-democrats-path-to-winning-back-blue-collar-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  who are white}}}\uc2 . And yet, a persuasive left should be able to build unity with these groups, and to talk to the concerns of people who would benefit from their suggested reforms, such as higher minimum wages and universal healthcare coverage.\par \par \uc2 Research on overcoming polarization might hold lessons for the left on how to have more meaningful conversations within its ranks.\par \par \uc2 While distrust is a dominant emotion among the right, anger incites the left\par \par \uc2 One of the problems at the root of conversational blocks is social media \u8211\'20\'13 and how we talk to one another when using it. Megan Boler, a University of Toronto professor who studies online polarization, likens social media to melodrama, inflamed by loaded words to create a cast of victims and villains. And, she adds, this tendency to simplify reality is spilling offline.\par \par \uc2 The right and left are motivated by different aspirations. Researching polarization in the lead-up to the 2020 election, Boler found that while the right is driven by nostalgia \u8211\'20\'13 wanting to go back to a \u8220\'20\'1Csimpler time\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 the left is motivated by change, to ensure rights for all, whether that\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare or fair wages. Researchers have found the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cjr.org/analysis/breitbart-media-trump-harvard-study.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 right has a heavier hand in online polarization}}}\uc2 , but if those on the left want to mobilize against this highly organized rightwing media ecosystem, they will need to find a way to unite.\par \par \uc2 Boler found that while distrust is a dominant emotion among the right, anger incites the left online. For those on the left, pithy statements on social media amplify this anger, and not always in useful ways. Discussion of complex issues like structural racism are reduced to retorts like \u8220\'20\'1Ccheck your privilege.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When words like \u8220\'20\'1Cprivilege\u8221\'20\'1D first migrated from academic to mainstream discourse, it was an opportunity to have conversations about things like how your immigration status influences your {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/regional-economist/second-quarter-2017/comparing-income-education-and-job-data-for-immigrants-vs-those-born-in-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  earning potential}}}\uc2 , or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/articles/unequal-opportunity-race-and-education/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 how race affects educational opportunities.}}}\uc2  But academics unpack words over decades of research and hundreds of pages in books \u8211\'20\'13 280 characters doesn\u8217\'20\'19t exactly provide space for nuance.\par \par \uc2 Closing ranks\par \par \uc2 Peggy McIntosh\uc2 , senior research scientist at the \uc2 Wellesley Centers for Women\uc2 , helped popularize the term \u8220\'20\'1Cprivilege\u8221\'20\'1D, describing it as \u8220\'20\'1Ca combination of unearned advantage and unearned disadvantage in life\u8221\'20\'1D in a 2014 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/books/page-turner/the-origins-of-privileg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Yorker interview}}}\uc2 . Race is one variable, but so is your body type, education level, your place of birth \u8211\'20\'13 and \u8220\'20\'1Cit changes minute by minute, depending on where we are, who we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing, or what we\u8217\'20\'19re required to do,\u8221\'20\'1D McIntosh added.\par \par \uc2 The concept requires deep reflection, to see how the systems around us are influencing us, how that shapes what we have access to and what we don\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCheck your privilege,\u8221\'20\'1D by contrast, can shut down conversation by saying that having unearned advantages means you\u8217\'20\'19re not allowed to have an opinion, rather than examining what others have to deal with in life to form a more informed opinion.\par \par \uc2 Strict language policing has cropped up in part because the left has felt under attack for years, says Peter T Coleman, a Columbia professor who studies intractable conflict.\par \par \uc2 When groups are under duress and threat, they tend to close ranks and have very clear norms\par \par \uc2 Coleman found {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.michelegelfand.com/rule-makers-rule-breakers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}\uc2  from Michele Gelfand offered a compelling explanation for the increasing hostility within the left. Gelfand\u8217\'20\'19s insight, as Coleman explains it, is that \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen groups are under duress and threat, they tend to close ranks and have very clear norms. Violators of those norms are really shunned and shamed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Coleman points out that even though Democrats won the 2020 election, the left and its values are still under fire: Fox News {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2021/08/fox-news-ratings-august-1234825169/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attracts the most viewers}}}\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are being obliterated, and voter suppression tactics are being made law. This context creates a \u8220\'20\'1Ctight culture\u8221\'20\'1D within the left that demands people in the group \u8220\'20\'1Cfall in line.\u8221\'20\'1D While this behavior is motivated by the need to mobilize against an outside threat, ironically, it can result in arrested development.\par \par \uc2 The other issue suffocating meaningful conversation is the adversarial nature of debate embedded in American culture. \u8220\'20\'1CDebate is a game to win,\u8221\'20\'1D says Coleman, who published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thewayoutofpolarization.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Way Out: How to Overcome Toxic Polarization}}}\uc2  earlier this year. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m listening to find flaws in your argument that I can weaponize against you to show you that I\u8217\'20\'19m right.\u8221\'20\'1D That\u8217\'20\'19s the opposite of dialogue, which, when facilitated properly, is a process of discovery and learning.\par \par \uc2 Dialogue is what Coleman and his colleagues try to cultivate at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.earth.columbia.edu/projects/view/1912"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Difficult Conversations Lab}}}\uc2  at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . The lab facilitates conversations between people on opposing sides of divisive issues, like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gun control, and studies the results. Researchers track participants\u8217\'20\'19 emotions \u8211\'20\'13 asking them to listen to audio of the discussion, to follow what their emotions were like throughout the conversation.\par \par \uc2 Coleman is a fan of \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thewholestory.solutionsjournalism.org/complicating-the-narratives-b91ea06ddf63"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complicating the narrative}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Instead of pitting someone who is anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against someone who is pro-choice, the lab would frame \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a \u8220\'20\'1Chighly complicated, nuanced, personal set of decisions\u8221\'20\'1D that has medical, religious, health and gender equity implications. The pair is then asked to create a statement they can agree on from their conversation.\par \par \uc2 Participants report a variety of emotions \u8211\'20\'13 they can move from negative emotions towards their partner to thinking huh, that\u8217\'20\'19s a good point they made. And while participants still advocate for their position, in conversations that lead to a shared understanding, people tend to ask more questions, such as, \u8220\'20\'1CCan you explain that?\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1CTell me more about what you mean.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He also found that those who have a high degree of complexity in their own lives \u8211\'20\'13 for instance, who have friends across religious and political stripes \u8211\'20\'13 are able to have richer conversations.\par \par \uc2 Cultivate curiosity, and really listen\par \par \uc2 Tamerius learned that curiosity was the key to repairing her relationship with her uncle. She learned more about his views by approaching conversations as she would as a therapist: guided by questions while being supportive. Within a month, she saw a difference. She offered to look into memes when he wasn\u8217\'20\'19t sure of the source, for instance, and he would delete ones associated with white nationalists.\par \par \uc2 Soon, he started to post doubts about Trump \u8211\'20\'13 like wishing Trump would tweet less. \u8220\'20\'1CHe didn\u8217\'20\'19t feel like he had to always defend his initial position,\u8221\'20\'1D Tamerius says.\par \par \uc2 I want people to feel like it\u8217\'20\'19s okay to advocate for what you believe in \u8211\'20\'13 it turns out the way to do that is to listen\par \par \uc2 Her uncle died soon after Trump was inaugurated, but those conversations led her to build Smart Politics into an organization that holds workshops and is her full-time job.\par \par \uc2 While other organizations working at depolarization are explicitly nonpartisan, like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://braverangels.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Braver Angels}}}\uc2 , Tamerius is transparent about her partisan agenda: she wants liberals to be more effective in persuading people. \u8220\'20\'1CI want people to feel like it\u8217\'20\'19s OK to advocate for what you believe in,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CBut it turns out the way to do that is actually to listen to what they have to say and connect with them around it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 John Regalado grew up in a household that listened to \uc2 Rush Limbaugh\uc2  in a suburb outside Denver, but working as a video journalist for a left-leaning series at \uc2 Univision\uc2 , he knew there were large parts of the country his work wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t speak to. So he started working on a web series called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLBVNJo7nhINRqe3O0-hRKYESCFTOPTKrB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Middle Ground}}}\uc2 , which brings together six people on opposing sides of issues like gentrification and immigration.\par \par \uc2 Regalado\u8217\'20\'19s first assignment was in Jerusalem, bringing together Palestinians and Israelis to discuss the conflict. Prompts guide the conversations \u8211\'20\'13 each participant stands when hearing the prompt, and if they identify with it, they take a seat to discuss it, and eventually the others also join in.\par \par \uc2 The first prompt was: \u8220\'20\'1CSomeone I know has died because of this conflict.\u8221\'20\'1D Five out of six participants took a seat, and shared who they had lost. Even though it quickly became clear even within this small group that the Palestinian side suffered at a different scale, the conversation allowed participants to connect around the impact of their losses .\par \par \uc2 The prompts are designed to be simple jumping off points for discussion, not clearcut political statements where it\u8217\'20\'19s obvious who will agree or disagree. While there are heated moments in that first episode Regalado directed, at the end of it the participants all chose to hang out and share a meal together.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s clear when watching the show that when participants try to convince or lecture others, walls come up. And {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4536373/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  research shows}}}\uc2  that most people aren\u8217\'20\'19t looking to be persuaded. Tamerius likens the mind to a fortress, trying to protect its existing way of understanding the world. But there are still ways to effectively talk about subjects with those seemingly on the \u8220\'20\'1Cother side\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Tamerius has co-written two bots for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that model conversations on polarizing subjects. Her most recent, on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2021/05/20/opinion/covid-19-vaccine-chatbot.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  how to speak with people who don\u8217\'20\'19t want a vaccine}}}\uc2 , opens with a friend texting that getting a Covid vaccination seems like a \u8220\'20\'1Cbad idea\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The bot then prompts you to choose a response: one is an answer you know is wrong (\u8220\'20\'1CAre you an anti-vaxxer?\u8221\'20\'1D), the second is a more subtle form of persuasion (\u8220\'20\'1CCovid is scarier\u8221\'20\'1D), and the third is a question (\u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ve heard some things that make you worried and unsure?\u8221\'20\'1D). When you click on your favored response, you\u8217\'20\'19re told why it\u8217\'20\'19s a good or less effective choice. The third one is the best choice because people want to feel heard \u8211\'20\'13 so instead of spamming someone with links to convince them that vaccinations are necessary, first hear where their fear comes from.\par \par \uc2 Tamerius often gets comments from people who say they know what the right answer is, but just can\u8217\'20\'19t stop themselves and choose another. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is exactly what [it\u8217\'20\'19s] like when you\u8217\'20\'19re in a conversation,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe responses that are counterproductive or emotionally gratifying are kind of like the junk food of political discourse.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When you don\u8217\'20\'19t have a bot to help you along in those moments, Tamerius suggests two things. First, change the way you think about the other person. \u8220\'20\'1CStop thinking about the other person as \u8216\'20\'18them\u8217\'20\'19, and immediately start thinking in terms of \u8216\'20\'18us\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D she advises. And second, listen. But, like, actually.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean listen to respond; or listen to rebut; or listen to reply \u8211\'20\'13 or God forbid, listen just to take turns talking,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt means listening to understand \u8211\'20\'13 until the other person feels heard, not until you feel like you\u8217\'20\'19ve heard them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Intellectually, it makes perfect sense. But practically, how do you avoid reaching for the gratifying junk food when you\u8217\'20\'19ve been fed a steady diet of it for a decade?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ve got to set the intention and work at it,\u8221\'20\'1D says Tamerius. \u8220\'20\'1COver time, it builds into habit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 5 October 2021 to clarify the findings of the 2017 Pew study.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211001eha1000xf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
