\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  law: federal judge hears Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s challenge\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza and agencies \par \uc2 798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par 13:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justice department attorney argues state \u8216\'20\'18resorted to vigilante justice\u8217\'20\'19 at hearing on Friday to consider whether legislation is unconstitutional\par \par \uc2 A federal judge considered whether to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on Friday, the most restrictive anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in force in the US, in a suit brought by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Since September, the law has banned the vast majority of abortions and sent women racing to get care beyond the borders of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s second most populous state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA state may not ban abortions at six weeks. Texas knew this, but it wanted a six-week ban anyway, so the state resorted to an unprecedented scheme of vigilante justice that was designed to scare \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and others who might help women exercise their constitutional rights,\u8221\'20\'1D justice department attorney Brian Netter told the court.\par \par \uc2 The suit seeks to land the first legal blow against the Texas law known as Senate Bill 8 (SB8), which thus far has withstood an early wave of requests to block it, including at the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 The law, signed by the Republican governor, Greg Abbott, in May, prohibits abortions in Texas once embryonic cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks. That is before some women know they are pregnant, and 18 weeks before a fetus can survive outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 The case was heard by Robert Pitman, a US district judge and appointee of Barack Obama. The landmark 1973 supreme court decision Roe v Wade guarantees women the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until that point, called \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D by courts and generally understood to be about 24 weeks gestation.\par \par \uc2 However, lawmakers designed Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law to skirt judicial review by banning state officials from enforcing it. Instead, it deputizes private individuals to sue and seek a $10,000 penalty for anyone who aids a woman in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from doctors to cab drivers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not some kind of vigilante scheme,\u8221\'20\'1D said Will Thompson, defending the law for the Texas attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s Office. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a scheme that uses the normal, lawful process of justice in Texas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To date, at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/26/texas-doctor-abortion-sued-pro-lifers-backpedaling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two lawsuits}}}\uc2  have been filed against a Texas doctor who publicly said he performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that violated the law. In an essay published by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/09/18/texas-abortion-provider-alan-braid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post}}}\uc2 , Dr Alan Braid wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CI acted because I had a duty of care to this patient, as I do for all patients, and because she has a fundamental right to receive this care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Both suits were both filed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/26/texas-doctor-abortion-sued-pro-lifers-backpedaling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disbarred out-of-state attorneys}}}\uc2 . Another precedent-shattering aspect of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law allows anyone, anywhere, regardless of their connection to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to sue those who help a woman terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The law was allowed to go into effect because the supreme court refused to block it, while not commenting on the constitutional merits, in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/04/texas-abortion-ruling-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stunning}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/aug/30/abortion-law-federal-court-decision-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 five-four decision by the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the short time since the law took effect on 1 September, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said \u8220\'20\'1Cexactly what we feared\u8221\'20\'1D has become reality, and described Texas clinics in danger of closing, while neighboring states struggle to keep up with a surge of patients driving hundreds of miles to seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Other women, they said, are forced to carry pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care has almost completely stopped in our state,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Ghazaleh Moayedi, a Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, told the US House oversight and reform committee during a hearing over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear how soon Pitman will decide. It is also unclear how quickly any of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s nearly two dozen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics would move to resume normal operations if the law is set aside. Texas officials would probably seek a swift reversal from the conservative fifth US circuit court of appeals, the same court which allowed the restrictions to take effect, and set up the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision not to block the law.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law is one of the biggest tests of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US in generations, and it is part of a decades-long push by Republicans nationwide to end access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In December, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  will hear arguments on whether Mississippi can enforce a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Like Texas\u8217\'20\'19s SB8, the law blatantly violates the essential holdings of Roe v Wade, by banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nine weeks before a fetus can survive outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 The decision, likely to be released in June 2022, will have national ramifications for women\u8217\'20\'19s ability to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . More than half of US states are openly hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/06/17/key-facts-about-the-abortion-debate-in-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 59% of Americans}}}\uc2  say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211004eha4002sb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
