\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden signs bill averting shutdown as heated infrastructure debate drags on \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh (now) and Joan E Greve (earlier) \par \uc2 7,773 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2021\par 02:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bill passed by Senate and House will extend funding until 3 December as Democrats continue to wrangle over Biden\u8217\'20\'19s economic agendaGovernment avoids shutdown \u8211\'20\'13 but what happens next?Democrats barrel toward make-or-break voteSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.56am BST\par \par \uc2 Politics recap\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  signed a stopgap funding bill to prevent a government shutdown. The House and the Senate passed the bill this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 * House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said Democrats are \u8220\'20\'1Con a path to win\u8221\'20\'1D an infrastructure bill vote, which was originally scheduled to occur today. But it remains unclear whether Democrats will be able to pass the bill tonight, as many progressives have threatened to oppose the legislation until a reconciliation package advances as well.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has proposed a top-line cost of $1.5tn for the reconciliation package, angering progressives who view the current price tag of $3.5tn as a serious compromise. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never been a liberal in any way, shape, or form,\u8221\'20\'1D Manchin told reporters today. Addressing the criticism from progressives, he added, \u8220\'20\'1CFor them to get [their reconciliation package], elect more liberals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  shared the story of her rape and subsequent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a House committee hearing on reproductive rights. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the summer of 1994, I was raped, I became pregnant, and I chose to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Bush said during the oversight committee hearing. \u8220\'20\'1CTo all the Black women and girls who have had abortions and will have abortions, we have nothing to be ashamed of. We live in a society that has failed to legislate love and justice for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s politics team is continuing to keep an eye on Congress, and will be bringing you more updates tonight.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.34am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.42am BST\par \par \uc2 Pelosi: "More to follow"\par \par \uc2 In a letter to colleagues, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  signaled that discussions would continue after a \u8220\'20\'1Cday of progress\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Bipartisan Infrastructure bill has already had its rule passed and its debate has concluded,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CDiscussions continue with the House, Senate and White House to reach a bicameral framework agreement to Build Back Better through a reconciliation bill.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has said he does not see a deal coming tonight.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.56am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13am BST\par \par \uc2 Pelosi appears to still be hanging onto the possibility of a vote on the $1tn infrastructure bill today.\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders have signaled that no votes would occur before 10pm DC time, but have not ruled out the possibility of ironing out a deal by tonight. Progressives have said they would not vote on the bill until the $3.5tn reconciliation package is passed \u8211\'20\'13 but that\u8217\'20\'19s not going to happen given moderate \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s staunch opposition to the proposal.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.18am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.05am BST\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s still unclear when there will be a vote on an infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  had to say...\par \par \uc2 enltrSpeaker Pelosi:\par \par \uc2 - \u8220\'20\'1CYes\u8221\'20\'1D when asked if progress was being made on infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 - When asked about when the vote would be the Speaker replied, \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we bring it to the floor.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/AJsMFeFn7Z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/AJsMFeFn7Z}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jason Donner (@jason_donner) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jason_donner/status/1443726027449044992?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Biden signs resolution averting shutdown\par \par \uc2 The stopgap bill averts a government shutdown - until 3 December.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what that means:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/30/explainer-us-government-shutdown-budget-debt-ceiling-biden-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The US government avoided a shutdown \u8211\'20\'13 but what happens next?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.38am BST\par \par \uc2 What is the infrastructure bill and will it pass?\par \par \uc2 This is a cornerstone of the Biden agenda, investing $1tn in roads, railways, bridges, ports, airports, broadband internet and more.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday the House was scheduled to vote on the infrastructure bill that passed the Senate last month in a bipartisan vote (with 19 Republicans voting yes). But since then House Republicans have been backing away from the bill, in a move to deny Biden a victory.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi also faces a revolt from an influential group of progressive Democrats who warned she must hold it back until a bigger, $3.5tn social and environmental investment bill is nailed down.\par \par \uc2 The progressives want Pelosi to stand by earlier statements that both bills would move through Congress together. They fear that, once centrist Democrats have infrastructure in the bag, they will go cold on the bigger commitment.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 , a leader of House progressives, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not blindly trusting that these bills are going to get done in the Senate, without actually having that be guaranteed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is unlikely that Pelosi will go ahead with the vote unless she is assured of victory. So postponement to a later date remains possible, a move that centrists have warned would represent \u8220\'20\'1Ca significant breach in trust\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.12am BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2  avoided a shutdown \u8211\'20\'13 catch up on where we are now\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2  went into Thursday embroiled in a game of three-dimensional chess with time running out and trillions of dollars at stake.\par \par \uc2 The first dimension was a must-do: fund the government by midnight to avoid it shutting down. In a typical shutdown, hundreds of thousands of federal employees stop getting paid and many stop working; some services are suspended and numerous national attractions and national parks temporarily close.\par \par \uc2 The second dimension is an even bigger must-do: raise the national debt ceiling, an artificially imposed borrowing limit, before an estimated deadline of 18 October. Failure to pay its bills would see the US default for the first time in history. The House speaker, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/nancy-pelosi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nancy Pelosi}}}\uc2 , has warned that the effects would be \u8220\'20\'1Ccataclysmic\u8221\'20\'1D and cost 6m jobs.\par \par \uc2 The third dimension is not quite a must but it feels that way to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats: pass a $1tn bipartisan infrastructure bill and a $3.5tn partisan package that expands social services and tackles the climate crisis. Both are currently stalled by divisions between Democratic centrists and progressives, along with Republican eagerness to deny Biden a win.\par \par \uc2 Why has it gotten so sticky?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s power is spread across executive (the president), legislative (Congress), and judicial (supreme court and other courts) branches, which ensures checks and balances but can also get messy quickly.\par \par \uc2 While Democrats hold the presidency and both chambers of Congress, their margins in the latter are razor-thin. The Senate is evenly split 50-50, with the vice-president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , holding the tiebreaker vote. Democrats have a 220-212 margin in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/house-of-representatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 House of Representatives}}}\uc2  so can only afford a handful of defections.\par \par \uc2 Comparisons between Biden and \uc2 Franklin Roosevelt\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s New Deal in the 1930s were therefore somewhat fanciful since Roosevelt enjoyed much more comfortable majorities to rubber-stamp his priorities. Biden has the power of persuasion but not much else.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.09am BST\par \par \uc2 When questioned by Bloomberg reporter Ari Natter about the energy company he founded, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  was none too pleased:\par \par \uc2 enltrMANCHIN asked by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AriNatter?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @AriNatter}}}\uc2  whether an energy company he founded is a conflict of interest as he negotiates reconciliation:\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: "I've been in a blind trust for 20 years, I have no idea what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 Ari: You're still getting dividends.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: "You got a problem?"\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Frank Thorp V (@frankthorp) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/frankthorp/status/1443303879815438339?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 29, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Manchin earned $500,000 in 2020 the private coal brokerage firm Enersystems, which he founded in 1998, and which his son now runs. And he has received major contributions from \uc2 Exxon\uc2  lobbyists. Yet, he chairs Senate Energy and Natural Resources Committee, which regulates the industry.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 OPINION: \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , America\u8217\'20\'19s climate decider-in-chief, is a coal baron\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/mark-hertsgaard"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mark Hertsgaard}}}\uc2  writes for \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has never been this famous. People around the world now know that the West Virginia Democrat is the essential 50th vote in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  that president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  needs to pass his agenda into law. That includes Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate agenda. Which doesn\u8217\'20\'19t bode well for defusing the climate emergency, given Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s longstanding opposition to ambitious climate action.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that the Senator wielding this awesome power \u8211\'20\'13 America\u8217\'20\'19s climate decider-in-chief, one might call him \u8211\'20\'13 has a massive climate conflict of interest. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , investigative journalism has revealed, is a modern-day coal baron.\par \par \uc2 Financial records {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/jul/20/joe-manchin-big-oil-democratic-senator"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 detailed}}}\uc2  by reporter Alex Kotch for the \uc2 Center for Media and Democracy\uc2  and published in \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  show that Manchin makes roughly half a million dollars a year in dividends from millions of dollars of coal company stock he owns. The stock is held in Enersystems, Inc, a company Manchin started in 1988 and later gave to his son, Joseph, to run.\par \par \uc2 Coal has been the primary driver of global warming since coal began fueling the Industrial Revolution in Great Britain 250 years ago. Today, the science is clear: coal must be phased out, starting immediately and around the world, to keep the 1.5C target within reach.\par \par \uc2 Scientists {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03821-8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimate}}}\uc2  that 90% of today\u8217\'20\'19s coal reserves must be left in the ground. No new coal-fired power plants should be built. Existing plants should quickly shift to solar and wind, augmented by reducing electricity demand with better energy efficiency in buildings and machinery (which also saves money and produces more jobs).\par \par \uc2 This is not a vision that gladdens a coal baron\u8217\'20\'19s heart. The idea of eliminating fossil fuels is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery, very disturbing\u8221\'20\'1D, Manchin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/07/14/politics/joe-manchin-reconciliation-climate-change/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said in July}}}\uc2  when specifics of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate agenda surfaced. Behind the scenes, Manchin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/19/climate/manchin-climate-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly has objected}}}\uc2  to Biden\u8217\'20\'19s plan to penalize electric utilities that don\u8217\'20\'19t quit coal as fast as science dictates.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2021/sep/30/joe-manchin-climate-coal-baron-stocks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Manchin, America\u8217\'20\'19s climate decider-in-chief, is a coal baron | Mark Hertsgaard }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Top Republicans rub shoulders with extremists in secretive rightwing group, leak reveals\par \par \uc2 A leaked document has revealed the membership list of the secretive Council for National Policy (CNP), showing how it provides opportunities for elite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2 , wealthy entrepreneurs, media proprietors and pillars of the US conservative movement to rub shoulders with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and anti-Islamic extremists.\par \par \uc2 The Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  (SPLC), which monitors rightwing hate groups, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/hatewatch/2016/05/17/council-national-policy-behind-curtain"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 describes }}}\uc2  the CNP as \u8220\'20\'1Ca shadowy and intensely secretive group [which] has operated behind the scenes\u8221\'20\'1D in its efforts to \u8220\'20\'1Cbuild the conservative movement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The leaked membership list dates from September last year, and reveals the 40-year-old CNP put influential Trump administration figures alongside leaders of organizations that have been categorized as hate groups.\par \par \uc2 The group was founded in 1981 by activists influential in the Christian right, including Tim LaHaye, Howard Phillips and \uc2 Paul Weyrich\uc2 , who had also been involved in founding and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moral_Majority"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leading the Moral Majority}}}\uc2 . Initially they were seeking to maximize their influence on the new Reagan administration. In subsequent years, CNP meetings have played host to presidential aspirants like \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  in 1999 and \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  in 2007, and sitting presidents including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/council-national-policy-video/2020/10/14/367f24c2-f793-11ea-a510-f57d8ce76e11_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 videos obtained by the Washington Post }}}\uc2  in 2020, the CNP executive committee chairman, Bill Walton, told attendees of the upcoming election: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a spiritual battle we are in. This is good versus evil.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The CNP is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/hatewatch/2016/05/17/council-national-policy-behind-curtain"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 so secretive}}}\uc2 , according to reports, that its members are instructed not to reveal their affiliation or even name the group.\par \par \uc2 Heidi Beirich, of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globalextremism.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Global Project Against Hate and Extremism}}}\uc2 , said in an email that \u8220\'20\'1Cthis new CNP list makes clear that the group still serves as a key venue where mainstream conservatives and extremists mix\u8221\'20\'1D, adding that CNP \u8220\'20\'1Cclearly remains a critical nexus for mainstreaming extremism from the far right into conservative circles\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The document \u8211\'20\'13 which reveals email addresses and phone numbers for most members \u8211\'20\'13 shows that the CNP includes members of SPLC-listed hate groups.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/30/revealed-council-national-policy-republicans-extremists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Top Republicans rub shoulders with extremists in secretive rightwing group, leak reveals}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Senate approved \uc2 Rohit Chopra\uc2  as the director of the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB), voting 50-48.\par \par \uc2 Chopra, 39, served on the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  and worked with senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts to establish the CFPB in the aftermath of the 2008 financial crisis. His appointment was uncertain after he drew criticism from free-market conservatives who saw him as \u8220\'20\'1Canti-business\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In March, the Senate Banking Committee voted with a 12-12 tie on Chopra\u8217\'20\'19s nomination, failing to advance him to a broader vote - but senate leader Chuck Schumer chose to disregard of \u8220\'20\'1Cdischarge\u8221\'20\'1D that vote.\par \par \uc2 Chopra has promised to crack down on banks\u8217\'20\'19 use of online behavioral advertising and look at their data collection practices. He has also said he will focus on fair lending issues as CFPB director.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 Congress grills Facebook exec on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s harmful effect on children\par \par \uc2 Antigone Davis, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s global head of safety, faced a grilling before the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  on Thursday in a hearing examining the impacts of the company\u8217\'20\'19s products on children.\par \par \uc2 Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing of the Senate commerce, science and transportation subcommittee comes after a series of Wall Street Journal reports based on internal \uc2 Facebook\uc2  leaks, including a story that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/sep/27/facebook-pauses-instagram-kids-teen-mental-health-concerns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revealed}}}\uc2  research showing the harmful effects of \uc2 Instagram\uc2  on childhood mental health.\par \par \uc2 Senators took a hard line against the company, hammering into the research and highlighting \uc2 Facebook\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attempts to obfuscate it leading up to the hearing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Facebook\uc2  knows the disruptive consequences that \uc2 Instagram\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s design and algorithms are having on young people in our society, but it has routinely prioritized its own rapid growth over basic safety for our children,\u8221\'20\'1D said Richard Blumenthal, the chair of the subcommittee, in his opening statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis research is a bombshell,\u8221\'20\'1D Blumenthal said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is powerful, gripping, riveting evidence that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  knows of the harmful effects of its site on children, and that it has concealed those facts and findings.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Blumenthal noted that his office conducted its own research into \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , posing as a 13-year-old girl and following accounts associated with eating disorders to see what \uc2 Instagram\uc2  would recommend. It found the platform sent the account further into the rabbit hole of harmful content.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur research shows right now, in real time, \uc2 Instagram\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s recommendations latch on to a person\u8217\'20\'19s insecurities, a young woman\u8217\'20\'19s vulnerability, about their bodies and drag them into dark places that glorify eating disorders and self-harm,\u8221\'20\'1D said Blumenthal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIG stands for \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , but it also stands for Insta-greed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Senator Edward Markey, a Democrat from Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 The research that sparked the hearing, revealed in the Wall Street Journal report, was commissioned by \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , which is a subsidiary of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and showed that the photo app could affect {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/sep/18/teenage-girls-body-image-and-instagrams-perfect-storm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 girls\u8217\'20\'19 mental health}}}\uc2  on issues such as body image and self-esteem.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/sep/30/facebook-hearing-testimony-instagram-impact"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Congress grills Facebook exec on Instagram\u8217\'20\'19s harmful effect on children}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Progressive congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez sharply criticized moderate Democrats like \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  over their handling of the infrastructure negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Ocasio-Cortez reframed the moderate wing of her party as \u8220\'20\'1Cconservative,\u8221\'20\'1D accusing fellow Democrats of failing to engage with their colleagues in a substantive way.\par \par \uc2 enltrProgressives are frustrated that the Democrats in these negotiations are being described as \u8220\'20\'1Cmoderates\u8221\'20\'1D. ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AOC?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @AOC}}}\uc2  ? is trying to change that:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we are seeing from this conservative side, this small cadre of people, is a fundamentally unserious pattern of negotiation.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/2BNAD8Xujy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/2BNAD8Xujy}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ryan Nobles (@ryanobles) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ryanobles/status/1443662014182838277?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRight now, what we are seeing from the conservative side, this small cadre of people, is a fundamentally unserious pattern of negotiation,\u8221\'20\'1D Ocasio-Cortez told reporters on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Ocasio-Cortez is one of the dozens of House progressives threatening to vote against the infrastructure bill unless the reconciliation package advances at the same time.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from me today. My west coast colleague, Maanvi Singh, will take over the blog for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 * Congress passed a stopgap funding bill to prevent a government shutdown, sending the legislation to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s desk. The House and the Senate passed the bill this afternoon, and the president needs to sign the legislation before midnight to avoid a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 * House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said Democrats are \u8220\'20\'1Con a path to win\u8221\'20\'1D the infrastructure bill vote, which was originally scheduled to occur today. But it remains unclear whether Democrats will be able to pass the bill today, as many progressives have threatened to oppose the legislation until the reconciliation package advances as well.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has proposed a top-line cost of $1.5tn for the reconciliation package, angering progressives who view the current price tag of $3.5tn as a serious compromise. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never been a liberal in any way, shape, or form,\u8221\'20\'1D Manchin told reporters today. Addressing the criticism from progressives, he added, \u8220\'20\'1CFor them to get [their reconciliation package], elect more liberals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  shared the story of her rape and subsequent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a House committee hearing on reproductive rights. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the summer of 1994, I was raped, I became pregnant, and I chose to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Bush said during the oversight committee hearing. \u8220\'20\'1CTo all the Black women and girls who have had abortions and will have abortions, we have nothing to be ashamed of. We live in a society that has failed to legislate love and justice for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Maanvi will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has just signed the stopgap government funding bill, formally sending the legislation to the White House for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s signature.\par \par \uc2 The president will need to sign the bill before midnight to avoid a government shutdown, and he is expected to do so.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really is exciting to keep government open,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said as she signed the bill.\par \par \uc2 enltrHouse Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  signs legislation to fund the government through Dec. 3rd: \u8220\'20\'1CIt really is exciting to keep government open.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BGAsVz1EPY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/BGAsVz1EPY}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1443676429707882499?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was on Capitol Hill today to break a tie vote in the Senate over \uc2 Rohit Chopra\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s nomination to lead the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asked about the fate of the bipartisan infrastructure bill, Harris told CNN that she is \u8220\'20\'1Cconfident\u8221\'20\'1D the House will pass the proposal.\par \par \uc2 But the vice-president would not say whether she believed the bill would be passed today, as previously expected. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t have that answer,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said.\par \par \uc2 enltrVP Harris said she\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cconfident\u8221\'20\'1D the bipartisan infrastructure bill will pass\par \par \uc2 Asked if that\u8217\'20\'19ll happen today, she replied: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t have that answer\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/v3m6wkxUhL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/v3m6wkxUhL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ali Zaslav (@alizaslav) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/alizaslav/status/1443664928016117770?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 The House is now in recess, after the chamber passed the government funding bill to prevent a shutdown at midnight.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe Chair declared the House in recess at 4:01 PM subject to the call of the Chair.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 House Press Gallery (@HouseDailyPress) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HouseDailyPress/status/1443667387316850694?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 It remains unclear whether House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has the votes to hold a final vote on the bipartisan infrastructure bill today, as she previously planned to do.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi is expected to hold more meetings with members of the Democratic caucus as the House is in recess. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 House passes government funding bill to prevent shutdown, sending it to Biden\par \par \uc2 The House has now officially passed the stopgap government funding bill to avert a shutdown at midnight.\par \par \uc2 The final vote was 254 to 175, with 34 Republicans joining all 220 Democrats to get the bill passed. A simple majority of 218 votes was needed to pass the bill.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe House concurred in the in the Senate Amendment to H.R. 5305 Extending Government Funding and Delivering Emergency Assistance Act by a vote of 254-175.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 House Press Gallery (@HouseDailyPress) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HouseDailyPress/status/1443663165934424067?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The measure passed the Senate earlier today, so the bill now goes to the desk of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who is expected to quickly sign it and thus prevent a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The bill will keep the government funded at its current levels through December 3, giving Congress more time to negotiate over a more comprehensive piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 It remains unclear whether the House will also vote today on the infrastructure bill, as dozens of progressives insist they will oppose the legislation until the reconciliation package passes as well.\par \par \uc2 Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 The House vote on the stopgap government funding bill remains open, but there are now enough votes to pass the measure.\par \par \uc2 As of now, 233 House members, including 26 Republicans, have voted in favor of the bill, which would allow the country to avoid a government shutdown at midnight.\par \par \uc2 The bill needs a simple majority of 218 votes to pass, so it should have more than enough support to get approved. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 The House is now voting on the stopgap government funding bill, which the Senate passed about an hour and a half ago.\par \par \uc2 The House is expected to pass the bill as well and then send it to the desk of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who must sign the legislation before midnight to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe House is voting now on concurring in the Senate Amendment to H.R. 5305 Extending Government Funding and Delivering Emergency Assistance Act.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 House Press Gallery (@HouseDailyPress) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HouseDailyPress/status/1443654214786367493?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 A reporter asked \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  plans to speak publicly today about the negotiations over the infrastructure bill and the reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll see,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House press secretary said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t make a prediction for you now, but it is certainly possible. It\u8217\'20\'19s also possible he has more meetings. He\u8217\'20\'19ll certainly make more phone calls, possibly moves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  downplayed concerns about Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 ongoing squabbles over the infrastructure bill and the reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is how democracy works. I know that feels foreign since there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t a lot of it over the last couple of years,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House press secretary said, getting in a dig against the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Psaki said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will be devoting the rest of his day to trying to reach an agreement on the reconciliation package, thus freeing up House progressives to vote for the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 One reporter asked Psaki how Biden is following the ongoing negotiations and if he has, for example, a whiteboard with an updated whip count on the two bills.\par \par \uc2 Psaki said there is (unfortunately) no whiteboard because the president is \u8220\'20\'1Cmore of a paper-and-pen kind of guy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , is now holding her daily briefing, and she\u8217\'20\'19s providing an update on negotiations over the infrastructure bill and the reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the news that \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s preferred top-line cost of the reconciliation package is $1.5tn, which is far less than what progressives want, Psaki said, \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s messy, this sausage-making on Capitol Hill. Policymaking is messy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrPsaki: \u8220\'20\'1CThe President, the Speaker of the House, and the Leader have more experience getting legislation across the finish line than any group of Democratic leaders in history...It\u8217\'20\'19s messy, this sausage making on Capitol Hill. Policymaking is messy.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/CrcJT1MhjT"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/CrcJT1MhjT}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/5wF37Zt9qt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/5wF37Zt9qt}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ABC News Politics (@ABCPolitics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ABCPolitics/status/1443649040923648000?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Psaki acknowledged there are more hurdles to overcome before reaching a deal on the reconciliation package, but she expressed hope that the House would still be able to pass the infrastructure bill later today.\par \par \uc2 As of now, it\u8217\'20\'19s unclear whether the House will be able to pass the infrastructure bill because dozens of progressives remain opposed to the legislation unless an agreement is reached on the reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re working towards winning a vote tonight,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have several hours left in the day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 The House is now debating the Senate-approved government funding bill, with a final vote on the legislation likely to occur within an hour.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe House has begun up to one hour of debate on the Senate Amendment to H.R. 5305 Extending Government Funding and Delivering Emergency Assistance Act.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 House Press Gallery (@HouseDailyPress) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HouseDailyPress/status/1443645086932344834?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The House is expected to pass the proposal and send it to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who must sign the bill before midnight to avoid a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema is pushing back against criticism from progressives that she has not been transparent about her stance on the reconciliation package, saying she has shared specific concerns about the bill with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sinema, a key moderate vote in the debate over the reconciliation bill, released a statement saying that she communicated her concerns about the legislation in August.\par \par \uc2 enltrStatement on Budget Reconciliation Negotiations {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/QZpaMXxm7q"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/QZpaMXxm7q}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kyrsten Sinema (@SenatorSinema) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenatorSinema/status/1443630876362002432?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSenator Sinema said publicly more than two months ago, before Senate passage of the bipartisan infrastructure bill, that she would not support a bill costing $3.5 trillion,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement from her office says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn August, she shared detailed concerns and priorities, including dollar figures, directly with Senate Majority Leader Schumer and the White House. Claims that the Senator has not detailed her views to President Biden and Senator Schumer are false.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The statement adds that Sinema \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinues to engage directly in good-faith discussions with both President Biden and Senator Schumer to find common ground\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, Politico {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/sep/30/joe-biden-infrastructure-social-policy-congress-us-politics-live-latest-news?page=with:block-6155da348f089420bc418da2#block-6155da348f089420bc418da2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 obtained a memo}}}\uc2  showing \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  told Schumer in July that his preferred top-line cost for the reconciliation bill is $1.5tn, which is far less than the current price tag of $3.5tn.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Fifteen Senate Republicans, including minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , joined all 50 Senate Democrats in voting for the stopgap government funding bill.\par \par \uc2 Here is the full list of Republicans who supported the bill:\par \par \uc2 enltrRepublicans voting Yes on passage of the CR: Blunt, Burr, Capito, Cassidy, Collins, Cornyn, Graham, Kennedy, McConnell, Murkowski, Romney, Rounds, Shelby, Tillis, and Young.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senate Periodicals (@SenatePPG) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenatePPG/status/1443642080148996097?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 The stopgap bill will keep the government funded at its current levels through December 3, giving Congress more time to negotiate over a more comprehensive piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had originally hoped to include a debt ceiling suspension in the government funding bill, but Republicans blocked those efforts, as they remain unified in their opposition to raising or suspending the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Congress will need to approve a debt ceiling hike or suspension by October 18 in order to avoid a default, which could result in the loss of millions of US jobs.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Senate passes bill to avoid shutdown, sending proposal to House\par \par \uc2 The Senate has officially passed a stopgap government funding bill to avoid a shutdown at midnight.\par \par \uc2 The bill, which needed 60 votes to pass, was approved in a bipartisan vote of 65 to 35, with a handful of Republicans -- including minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  -- joining Democrats to advance the legislation.\par \par \uc2 enltrPassed, 65-35: Cal. #137, H.R.5305, Continuing Resolution (December 3rd), as amended.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senate Cloakroom (@SenateCloakroom) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenateCloakroom/status/1443635164895186947?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The bill now heads to the House, which is also expected to pass the legislation later today and send it to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s desk.\par \par \uc2 The president will then need to sign the bill before midnight to avoid a government shutdown. If he does so, at least one item will be off Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 legislative to-do list, but several others remain.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 Manchin confirms he wants $1.5tn reconciliation package, angering progressives\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  said he wants the top-line cost of the reconciliation package to be $1.5tn, confirming {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/sep/30/joe-biden-infrastructure-social-policy-congress-us-politics-live-latest-news?page=with:block-6155da348f089420bc418da2#block-6155da348f089420bc418da2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico\u8217\'20\'19s earlier report}}}\uc2  on the moderate Democrat\u8217\'20\'19s stance.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters on Capitol Hill, Manchin warned that a larger reconciliation package risked \u8220\'20\'1Cchanging our whole society to an entitlement mentality\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The West Virginia senator confirmed that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , another key Senate moderate, are aware of his opinion on the reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 enltrSen. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  (D-WV) says his maximum number for reconciliation is $1.5 trillion because he wants to avoid "changing our whole society to an entitlement mentality." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ylwVUrCf7H"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ylwVUrCf7H}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1443622277405413378?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never been a liberal in any way, shape, or form,\u8221\'20\'1D Manchin said. Addressing criticism from progressives, he added, \u8220\'20\'1CFor them to get [their reconciliation package], elect more liberals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s demand is already frustrating his progressive colleagues, who view the bill\u8217\'20\'19s current price tag of $3.5tn as a serious compromise.\par \par \uc2 Asked about Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s suggested top-line cost, Democratic senator Mazie Hirono {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/burgessev/status/1443627340085530630"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told reporters}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CPretty sad if you ask me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Minnesota representative \uc2 Ilhan Omar{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jeremyslevin/status/1443607985377447936?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spoke about her current frustrations}}}\uc2  with the stalled reconciliation package, a major tax and spending bill that has faced criticism from moderate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat do they want to cut? Childcare for families that desperately need it? Do they want to not address the climate crisis for a future generation? Do they not want homecare programs for our seniors and the disabled? These are the conversations we\u8217\'20\'19re waiting to have,\u8221\'20\'1D Omar said in an interview with MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Omar is one of the progressives threatening to oppose the infrastructure bill, which is still theoretically scheduled for a House vote today, unless the reconciliation package is approved simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 After meeting with speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus said her view remains unchanged on the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have called for the reconciliation bill to be passed first, and then we\u8217\'20\'19ll happily vote for the infrastructure bill,\u8221\'20\'1D congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  said, per Forbes.\par \par \uc2 Jayapal also reiterated that half of her 96-member caucus remains committed to voting against the infrastructure bill until a reconciliation package is approved.\par \par \uc2 enltrNEW: Jayapal, after meeting with Pelosi, says progressives\u8217\'20\'19 position on infrastructure is unchanged: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have called for the reconciliation bill to be passed first and then we'll happily vote for the infrastructure bill.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Andrew Solender (@AndrewSolender) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AndrewSolender/status/1443621636972945408?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 House minority leader Kevin McCarthy of California {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CBSNews/status/1443603041077301258"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stumbled through an answer }}}\uc2  on Thursday when asked if he had changed his views on same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 After \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  from Wyoming admitted in a recent interview with 60 minutes that she was \u8220\'20\'1Cwrong\u8221\'20\'1D to oppose equal marriage, a reporter asked McCarthy if his views had similarly changed.\par \par \uc2 enltrAfter GOP Rep. Liz Cheney told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/60Minutes?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @60Minutes}}}\uc2  she was "wrong" to oppose same-sex marriage, reporter asks Kevin McCarthy if his views have changed.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy: "Look \u8212\'20\'14 same-sex marriage is the law of the land, and it's what America holds, and that's the law of the land." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xgEj4Fimuw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/xgEj4Fimuw}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CBS News (@CBSNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CBSNews/status/1443603041077301258?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLook \u8212\'20\'14 same-sex marriage is the law of the land, and it\u8217\'20\'19s what America holds, and that\u8217\'20\'19s the law of the land,\u8221\'20\'1D McCarthy said, seemingly not confirming what his own views were on the issue of marriage equality.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/amid-shouts-of-shame-house-gop-defeats-gay-rights-measure"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aided in defeating}}}\uc2  a 2016 amendment aimed at upholding an executive order that banned discrimination against LGBT employees by federal contractors.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  shares story of rape and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at House hearing: 'I am not ashamed'\par \par \uc2 Missouri congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  has shared the story behind her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time, revealing that she had the procedure after being raped at the age of 17.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I was 17, I was raped, became pregnant, and got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Bush {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CoriBush/status/1443292467814666240"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted on Wednesday. }}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I am not ashamed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/the-reidout/watch/abortion-rights-championed-by-reps-barbara-lee-cori-bush-pramila-jayapal-who-share-personal-abortion-stories-122340933795"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joining representatives }}}\uc2  Barbara Lee of California and \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  of Washington, Bush and others shared details related to their personal abortions during a House Oversight Committee about reproductive rights on Thursday and in an exclusive interview with MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 enltrRep. \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 : "My \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  happened on a Saturday...Choosing to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the hardest decision I had ever made. But at 18 years old, I knew it was the right decision for me...in the summer of 1994 I was raped, I became pregnant and I chose to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/cKykuIIZOz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/cKykuIIZOz}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CSPAN (@cspan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/cspan/status/1443592081218277381?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday I sit before you as that nurse, as that pastor, as that activist, that survivor, that single mom, that congresswoman \u8212\'20\'14 that in the summer of 1994 I was raped, I became pregnant, and I chose to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bush during the oversight committee meeting.\par \par \uc2 While Bush has spoken about her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  publicly before, discussing her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time at a small rally in St. Louis earlier this month, and the sexual assault she experienced, this is the first time she has disclosed the whole story, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2021/09/cori-bush-congress-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 speaking with Vanity Fair}}}\uc2  before re-telling her story before committee members.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo all the Black women and girls who have had abortions and will have abortions, we have nothing to be ashamed of. We live in a society that has failed to legislate love and justice for us,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1443588085694926857"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said Bush}}}\uc2  during the committee proceedings.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said Democrats are \u8220\'20\'1Con a path to win\u8221\'20\'1D the infrastructure bill vote, which was originally scheduled to occur today. But it remains unclear whether Democrats will be able to pass the bill today, as many progressives have threatened to oppose the legislation until the reconciliation package advances as well.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has proposed a top-line cost of $1.5tn for the reconciliation package, according to a new report from Politico. The moderate senator\u8217\'20\'19s proposal will likely outrage progressives, who already view the bill\u8217\'20\'19s current price tag of $3.5tn as a serious compromise.\par \par \uc2 * Congress is expected to pass a government funding bill to avoid a shutdown at midnight. The Senate plans to soon hold a final vote on the funding bill, and Republican leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has indicated support for the proposal, likely ensuring its passage.\par \par \uc2 The blog will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Manchin proposed $1.5tn reconciliation package this summer \u8211\'20\'13 report\par \par \uc2 Over the summer, Democratic senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  proposed a top-line cost of $1.5tn for the reconciliation package, according to a new report.\par \par \uc2 Politico {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2021/09/30/manchin-proposed-15t-topline-number-to-schumer-this-summer-514803"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Manchin proposed a deal to Senate Majority Chuck Schumer this summer to limit the total cost of Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 sweeping spending bill to $1.5 trillion, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.politico.com/1e/ef/159cabd547868585f9b1a8f06388/july-28-2021.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 copy}}}\uc2  of the agreement obtained by POLITICO.\par \par \uc2 Manchin also suggested beginning debate no earlier than Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 The West Virginia senator has been distributing the document to Democratic colleagues and leaders in recent days to underscore that he has outlined his red lines on President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s jobs and families plan.\par \par \uc2 Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s proposal will likely outrage progressives, who already view the reconciliation bill\u8217\'20\'19s current price tag of $3.5tn as a serious compromise on their part.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 At one point during her press conference, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  described the reconciliation package as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe culmination\u8221\'20\'1D of her political career.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just told members of my leadership that the reconciliation bill was a culmination of my service in Congress because it was about the children,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said.\par \par \uc2 enltr. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JakeSherman?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @JakeSherman}}}\uc2  : "You said this is the culmination of your time in Congress. Are you trying to -- culmination means the end of an experience."\par \par \uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SpeakerPelosi?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @SpeakerPelosi}}}\uc2  : "Get out of here...So this is, it's so much and that's why I said that, yeah." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/E0GfyFfGuk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/E0GfyFfGuk}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CSPAN (@cspan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/cspan/status/1443597374555922437?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 A reporter later noted that the use of the phrase \u8220\'20\'1Cculmination\u8221\'20\'1D usually indicates the end of an experience, potentially pointing to Pelosi\u8217\'20\'19s retirement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGet out of here,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi replied, adding that the reconciliation package represents \u8220\'20\'1Cso much\u8221\'20\'1D of Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 agenda.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  left her press conference by urging reporters to \u8220\'20\'1Cthink positively\u8221\'20\'1D about the negotiations over the infrastructure bill and the reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 And yet, as Pelosi was making her comments, House majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  said he was not confident that the infrastructure bill would pass today, as Democratic leadership had previously hoped.\par \par \uc2 enltrLeader Hoyer is asked whether he\u8217\'20\'19s confident the infrastructure bill will pass today: \u8220\'20\'1Cnope\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Nicholas Wu (@nicholaswu12) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/nicholaswu12/status/1443594253108658181?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Despite the stress of the day, House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  seemed to be in her element as she discussed the state of negotiations over the infrastructure bill and the reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet me just tell you about negotiating. At the end, that\u8217\'20\'19s when you really have to weigh in,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democratic speaker said at her press conference.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou cannot tire. You cannot concede. This is the fun part.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 Pelosi says Democrats are 'on a path to win' infrastructure vote\par \par \uc2 House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  sought to assure Democrats who are concerned that Congress will only pass the infrastructure bill and not the reconciliation package, which includes major investments in childcare and climate initiatives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRemove all doubt in anyone\u8217\'20\'19s mind that we won\u8217\'20\'19t have a reconciliation bill,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe will have a reconciliation bill. That\u8217\'20\'19s for sure.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr"I just told members of my leadership that the reconciliation bill was a culmination of my service in Congress cause it was about the children," Speaker Pelosi says. "We will have a reconciliation bill, that's for sure." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JaQADJdb4M"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/JaQADJdb4M}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 MSNBC (@MSNBC) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MSNBC/status/1443591989665140737?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 But the speaker was vague on the exact timing of the vote on the infrastructure bill, which was originally scheduled to take place today.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m only envisioning taking it up and winning it,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re on a path to win the vote.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It remains unclear whether Pelosi has the votes to pass the bill, as progressives threaten to oppose the legislation until the reconciliation package advances.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is now holding her weekly press conference, and she is expected to provide an update on the timing of the infrastructure bill vote.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic speaker began her prepared remarks by applauding House Democrats for passing a bill yesterday to suspend the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi criticized Republicans for refusing to help Democrats in suspending the debt ceiling, reiterating that a potential default could cost the US millions of jobs and wipe out trillions of dollars in wealth for American families.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi suggested that Congress should in the future reconsider how the debt ceiling is handled so lawmakers don\u8217\'20\'19t have to go through this process year after year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll have to examine that process,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said.\par \par \uc2 The House-approved bill is expected to fail in the evenly divided Senate, where Republicans have repeatedly blocked efforts to suspend the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is meeting with her leadership team this morning, as she approaches a decision on whether to delay the vote on the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Leaving the meeting, House Democratic caucus chair \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  would not give any indication as to whether the vote would be delayed, per the Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 enltrIs the BIF vote still on? Rep. Jeffries, coming out of a House Democratic leadership meeting, says \u8220\'20\'1Cthe meeting is still ongoing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Siobhan Hughes (@siobhanehughes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/siobhanehughes/status/1443585666814648335?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has indicated support for the government funding bill, likely ensuring its passage today.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday, the Senate will consider and pass a government funding bill and do our part to avoid a shutdown,\u8221\'20\'1D the Republican leader said in a floor speech.\par \par \uc2 McConnell celebrated that the stopgap funding bill does not include the \u8220\'20\'1Cpoison pill\u8221\'20\'1D of a debt ceiling suspension, which Republicans have opposed for months.\par \par \uc2 The minority leader once again called on Democrats to use the reconciliation process to suspend the debt ceiling, which would not require any Republican support.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CClumsy attempts at partisan jams by the majority will not change that reality,\u8221\'20\'1D McConnell said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt didn\u8217\'20\'19t work on government funding, and it won\u8217\'20\'19t work on the debt limit. They will just be wasting valuable time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democrats have called on Republicans to help raise or suspend the debt ceiling in a bipartisan manner, which occurred during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s presidency.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Congress to vote on government funding bill to avoid shutdown\par \par \uc2 As Democrats try to advance \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s economic agenda, there is also the small matter that Congress needs to pass a government funding bill to avoid a shutdown at midnight.\par \par \uc2 The government will not be able to remain open into October unless Congress passes a stopgap funding bill today.\par \par \uc2 Both the Senate and the House appear ready to send a bill to Biden\u8217\'20\'19s desk, and Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said the upper chamber will vote on the matter this morning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have an agreement on the CR, the continuing resolution to prevent a government shutdown, and we should be voting on that tomorrow morning,\u8221\'20\'1D Schumer said last night.\par \par \uc2 If the bill can pass the Senate, the House will then have to move quickly to approve the bill as well. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 The White House appears to already be downplaying expectations that the House will vote on the bipartisan infrastructure bill today.\par \par \uc2 Energy secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  told CNN this morning, \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not some major cataclysm if there isn\u8217\'20\'19t a vote today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrSen. Manchin is "concerned about making sure we don't have too much spending without pay force," Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  says. "Mark my words, the infrastructure bill will be passed, and a version of the reconciliation bill will be as well." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/2o9E4WOvKi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/2o9E4WOvKi}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9hPBr4qjaF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9hPBr4qjaF}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 New Day (@NewDay) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NewDay/status/1443556408004419586?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 30, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Emphasizing that there is \u8220\'20\'1Cfull support\u8221\'20\'1D among congressional Democrats for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s Build Back Better agenda, Granholm said there is some \u8220\'20\'1Cnegotiation around the margins\u8221\'20\'1D that still needs to take place in regards to the reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut this will get through,\u8221\'20\'1D Granholm said. \u8220\'20\'1CMark my words. The infrastructure bill will be passed and a version of the reconciliation bill will be as well.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 The next 24 hours will make clear whether Democrats are on the verge of pushing through a once-in-a-generation expansion of the social safety net or nearing a complete collapse of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s ambitious domestic agenda.\par \par \uc2 The stakes are as high as ever as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  barrel toward a make-or-break vote on a $1tn public works measure, with almost no margin for error and little time left to break an impasse that threatens to imperil its passage \u8211\'20\'13 and possibly the entirety of the president\u8217\'20\'19s agenda.\par \par \uc2 Assurances of progress offered little comfort to nervous Democrats on Capitol Hill, where a series of legislative and fiscal deadlines loom.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re obviously at a precarious and important time in these discussions,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , told reporters on Wednesday, as Biden spent the day locked in negotiations with Democratic leadership and lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Returning from an Oval Office meeting with Biden on Wednesday afternoon, the House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , told reporters on Capitol Hill that she was prepared to move ahead with a vote on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the plan,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding that they were taking it \u8220\'20\'1Cone hour at a time\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/29/democrats-joe-biden-agenda-politics-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats barrel toward make-or-break vote as crucial 24 hours begins}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 House infrastructure vote presents crucial test for Biden's agenda\par \par \uc2 Greetings from Washington, live blog readers.\par \par \uc2 Today is the day \u8211\'20\'13 or it might be the day, depending on how things shake out. The House is expected to vote today on the bipartisan infrastructure bill, but it remains unclear whether the vote will be delayed.\par \par \uc2 The bill is one crucial part of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s economic agenda, but House progressives are threatening to oppose the bill unless the president\u8217\'20\'19s other legislative priority \u8211\'20\'13 the reconciliation package \u8211\'20\'13 advances at the same time.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats remain at an impasse over the reconciliation package, which would provide major investments in Medicare, childcare and climate initiatives.\par \par \uc2 enltrMy statement on infrastructure and reconciliation negotiations: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ivdAUn9Mdh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/ivdAUn9Mdh}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/6Y6ATeH8DR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/6Y6ATeH8DR}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  (@Sen_JoeManchin) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Sen_JoeManchin/status/1443330806987034628?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 29, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 In a statement yesterday, Democratic senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , one of the moderates who have argued the reconciliation package must be scaled down, said, \u8220\'20\'1CI cannot \u8211\'20\'13 and will not \u8211\'20\'13 support trillions in spending or an all or nothing approach that ignores the brutal fiscal reality our nation faces.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  does not have the votes to pass the infrastructure bill, the vote will likely be delayed. And if the vote is delayed, it could be an ominous sign for advancing any part of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 The blog will be keeping a close on Capitol Hill and the White House as the day unfolds, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210930eh9u001ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
