\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not just the right who can admit feeling uncomfortable about ending a pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 Rhiannon Lucy Cosslett \par \uc2 1,245 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2021\par 13:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 By allowing women space to talk about difficult abortions, we may be able to improve access to them\par \par \uc2 Like many people, I\u8217\'20\'19ve been thinking about the women in Texas, a US state that is now in the business of forced births. When you consider how pregnancy is dated from the first day of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s last period, its newly imposed six-week limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  essentially amounts to a ban, leaving such a tiny window for a woman to arrange a termination. As well as being horrified by this {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/07/un-experts-condemn-texas-anti-abortion-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cynical assault on human rights}}}\uc2 , I find myself once again counting my lucky stars that I live in England, a country that \u8211\'20\'13 unlike many others, including Northern Ireland, where women are still being forced to travel due to a postcode lottery \u8211\'20\'13 has access to safe, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  free of charge.\par \par \uc2 For as long as the assault on women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights continues around the world, it feels taboo to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any other way. It is a privilege to be able to make an appointment, take the pills (if it is early enough), and get on with our lives. But what has become difficult to acknowledge is that for some women, even a legal, safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/22/uma-thurman-texas-abortion-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a traumatic experience}}}\uc2 . This is a story that we have surrendered to the right, when it is an experience that deserves to be heard and could even serve to strengthen the case for better access.\par \par \uc2 An intensely traumatic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the theme of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/larger-than-an-orange-9781784744410"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Larger Than an Orange}}}\uc2  by Lucy Burns. This is not a story about a woman who has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and feels nothing but relief, then moves on, hardly giving it a second thought. It is the diary of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its aftermath, which sees the narrator alone in her grief and her pain, telling people about it compulsively and scrolling through anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  memes on the internet. It somehow manages to both truthfully convey the trauma of one woman\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while also being resolutely pro-choice. It\u8217\'20\'19s quite a balance, in these polarised times.\par \par \uc2 In conveying the gulf between politics and personal experience, Larger Than an Orange provides us with vital nuance, and articulates emotions that feel unspoken, even to women. \u8220\'20\'1CSo many of my women friends have said, \u8216\'20\'18I knew this to be true, it had to be true, but I have never heard anyone say it before.\u8217\'20\'19 Which is mad,\u8221\'20\'1D Burns tell me of their reaction to the book. (As for her male friends, many of them had no idea what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even involved.) Like Burns, I believe that what lies behind this silence is fear.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are fortunate enough to be able to have safe legal abortions in England. So people don\u8217\'20\'19t want to talk about all the bad sides, because they\u8217\'20\'19re worried that, when access feels so precarious, it will just be taken away from them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I wonder if they also worry that too much candour might frighten other women, particularly younger ones. In the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/jul/12/saint-frances-review-kelly-osullivan-taboo-busting-drama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 film Saint Frances}}}\uc2 , a woman has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bleeds for almost all the rest of the film, and for much of it refuses to admit \u8211\'20\'13 much to the frustration of the man she slept with \u8211\'20\'13 that she has any feelings about it at all. It feels radical, and new, and belongs in a similar space to Larger Than an Orange: a space of complex feelings and grey areas, coexisting with a powerful message about the importance of safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A space that, though Saint Frances is an American film, I\u8217\'20\'19m unsure the US is fully ready for, being as it is still bogged down in emotive, religious arguments about when life begins. (It is notable that Burns\u8217\'20\'19s book has not yet found a US publisher.)\par \par \uc2 Still, the situation in the UK is far from ideal. As Burns conveys, access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes with caveats. It is only legal insofar as it constitutes, in certain circumstances, an exception to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2017/mar/13/mps-win-right-to-challenge-victorian-law-criminalising-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Offences Against the Person Act 1861}}}\uc2 . It has to be signed off by two doctors, who verify that carrying a pregnancy to term would be a risk to the physical or mental health of the mother. There\u8217\'20\'19s a scene in the book where Burns sits with the nurse at an impasse. She knows that the nurse wants her to state something in a certain way in order for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be permitted, but she is unsure how to phrase the words.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI thought you just went to the clinic and said, \u8216\'20\'18Hello, I\u8217\'20\'19m pregnant, I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be pregnant.\u8217\'20\'19 And it was clear that it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t like that,\u8221\'20\'1D says Burns. \u8220\'20\'1CThose conditions kind of confirmed the trauma of it \u8230\'20\'26 I\u8217\'20\'19m sure some other people have had the same experience, where you feel as though you\u8217\'20\'19re being forced to say, \u8216\'20\'18I think if I\u8217\'20\'19m not able to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the impact on my mental health will be so extreme that I\u8217\'20\'19m at risk of hurting myself.\u8217\'20\'19 That\u8217\'20\'19s an extraordinary thing to have to admit to yourself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I find myself wondering if the legal need to prove the trauma of an unterminated pregnancy for some women precludes them from discussing the equally valid trauma of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself. Instead, there is a culture of silence around the potential mental health impact. The right shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t own uncomfortable feelings about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, particularly in Britain, talking about them could help lay a path to an improved legal situation.\par \par \uc2 Accepting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be traumatising for some women adds force to the argument that no one should have to jump through hoops or stick to a legally mandated script in order to access it. To have to subject your feelings about an unwanted pregnancy to a box-ticking exercise is not a sign of a humane, holistic system.\par \par \uc2 The importance of Burns\u8217\'20\'19s work lies in its permission-giving. Women who find their abortions traumatic shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t feel as alone in their pain as she did, and their reaction is part of a range of experiences that deserve to be heard. Listening should in no way undermine the case for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I can understand that American women might be reluctant to have this conversation, standing as they are at a precipice where their rights are crumbling before their eyes.\par \par \uc2 In 2015, the writer Monica Heisey {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gawker.com/how-should-an-abortion-be-1680939464"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote of her frustration}}}\uc2  at the lack of nuance about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , how women made soft, sympathetic eyes at her, when her decision to end a pregnancy had been far from the most difficult decision in her life. As Heisey wrote then, there are as many reasons for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as there are women who have them, and that \u8220\'20\'1Cdenying the range of women\u8217\'20\'19s experiences of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps the topic a taboo in reality and a vibrant topic of debate in the abstract. It is easier to give away the reproductive rights of an abstract idea.\u8221\'20\'1D Instead of forcing women into polarised positions, we need to acknowledge the full spectrum of emotions, including those of women such as Burns, who defines it as the worst experience of her life. I think we are ready for that conversation, challenging though it is.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rhiannon Lucy Cosslett is a Guardian columnist and author\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210926eh9q000xe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
