\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Doctor who publicly defied Texas law by performing  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  has been sued\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par 13:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dr Alan Braid of San Antonio became the first provider to reveal he violated the law in a Washington Post opinion piece\par \par \uc2 A San Antonio doctor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/19/texas-doctor-abortion-law-washington-post-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who said he had performed an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  in defiance of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s new law has been sued, setting up a potential test of the legality of the extreme, near-total ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Former attorneys in Arkansas and Illinois filed lawsuits on Monday against Dr Alan Braid, who became the first Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider to publicly reveal he violated the law when he wrote {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/09/18/texas-abortion-provider-alan-braid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an opinion piece}}}\uc2  in the Washington Post over the weekend. That piece all but dared supporters of the law to make an example by suing him, because it can only be enforced through private lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am taking a personal risk,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/09/18/texas-abortion-provider-alan-braid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Braid wrote}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CBut it\u8217\'20\'19s something I believe in strongly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have daughters, granddaughters and nieces. I believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an essential part of healthcare. I have spent the last 50 years treating and helping patients. I can\u8217\'20\'19t just sit back and watch us return to 1972.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Oscar Stilley, the former attorney in Arkansas who filed one of the lawsuits, has said he is not personally opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sued in order to force a court to review the ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the law is no good, why should we have to go through a long, drawn-out process to find out if it\u8217\'20\'19s garbage?\u8221\'20\'1D Stilley {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/courts_law/texas-abortion-doctor-sued/2021/09/20/f5ab5c56-1a1c-11ec-bcb8-0cb135811007_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Washington Post}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stilley, who said he lost his law license after being convicted of tax fraud in 2010, added in an interview with the Associated Press: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want doctors out there nervous and sitting there and quaking in their boots and saying, \u8216\'20\'18I can\u8217\'20\'19t do this because if this thing works out, then I\u8217\'20\'19m going to be bankrupt.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Texas law prohibits abortions once medical professionals can detect cardiac activity, which is usually around six weeks and before many women even know they are pregnant. Prosecutors cannot take criminal action against Braid, because the law explicitly forbids that. The only way the ban can be enforced is through lawsuits brought by private citizens, who are entitled to claim at least $10,000 in damages if successful.\par \par \uc2 Braid wrote that on 6 September he provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a woman who was still in her first trimester but beyond the state\u8217\'20\'19s new limit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI fully understood that there could be legal consequences \u8211\'20\'13 but I wanted to make sure that Texas didn\u8217\'20\'19t get away with its bid to prevent this blatantly unconstitutional law from being tested,\u8221\'20\'1D Braid wrote.\par \par \uc2 Braid also wrote about what it was like to work in Texas before Roe v Wade, saying, he had begun his residency in obstetrics and gynecology at a hospital in San Antonio in 1972: \u8220\'20\'1CAt the time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was effectively illegal in Texas \u8211\'20\'13 unless a psychologist certified a woman was suicidal. If the woman had money, we\u8217\'20\'19d refer her to clinics in Colorado, California or New York. The rest were on their own. Some traveled across the border to Mexico.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Two federal lawsuits are making their way through the courts over the law, known as SB8. In one, filed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and others, the supreme court declined to block the law from taking effect while the case makes its way through the legal system. In the second case, the justice department is asking a federal judge to declare the law invalid, arguing it was enacted \u8220\'20\'1Cin open defiance of the constitution\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , one of the plaintiffs in the first federal lawsuit, is representing Braid.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the center\u8217\'20\'19s president and CEO, said it stands \u8220\'20\'1Cready to defend him against the vigilante lawsuits that SB8 threatens to unleash against those providing or supporting access to constitutionally protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210922eh9m00262\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
