\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  to hear arguments in Mississippi case that threatens Roe v Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 565 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2021\par 19:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization concerns a law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancyUS politics \u8211\'20\'13 live coverage\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  has set a date to hear arguments in a Mississippi case that could overturn Roe v Wade, the near-50-year-old ruling that guarantees a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oral arguments in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization will be heard on 1 December. The case concerns a law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, a direct challenge to Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 News of the court date arrived amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/19/texas-doctor-abortion-law-washington-post-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 controversy over a Texas law, known as SB8}}}\uc2 , which effectively bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six weeks and empowers citizens to sue providers and anyone who helps a woman access their services.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the supreme court, now dominated by conservatives, allowed the Texas law to stand via an emergency ruling.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Monday, the White House said it \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly supports\u8221\'20\'1D a House bill meant to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, it said, \u8220\'20\'1Cblatantly violates existing supreme court precedent established under Roe v Wade\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The House is expected to advance legislation designed to stop states from enacting anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures like the one in Texas.\par \par \uc2 But though the bill is expected to pass the Democratic-held House, it will face an uphill battle in the evenly split Senate, where most legislation requires 60 votes. Most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and support calls to overturn Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 The White House statement said: \u8220\'20\'1CIn the wake of Texas\u8217\'20\'19 unprecedented attack, it has never been more important to codify this constitutional right and to strengthen healthcare access for all women, regardless of where they live.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the Mississippi case, lawyers for Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, the only licensed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facility in the state, have asked the court to reject the state\u8217\'20\'19s request to \u8220\'20\'1Cjettison a half-century of settled precedent\u8221\'20\'1D and warned that if the law is upheld, the \u8220\'20\'1Cfallout would be swift and certain\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The law, known as the Gestational Age Act, was passed in 2018. It allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks in cases involving \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical emergencies or for severe fetal abnormality\u8221\'20\'1D. Like the Texas law, it contains no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Under the Mississippi law, doctors could have medical licenses suspended or revoked if they perform abortions that do not comply with the law.\par \par \uc2 Also on Monday, NBC News reported that nearly 900 state legislators from 45 states had filed a brief with the supreme court, asking it to reject the Mississippi law.\par \par \uc2 Of the 897 state legislators who signed the brief, 895 were Democrats and two independents. The only states that did not provide at least one signatory were Mississippi, North Dakota, Oklahoma, Arkansas and Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, Cecile Richards, a former head of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , marked the first anniversary of the death of the liberal supreme court justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  by releasing an open letter emphasising the renewed threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/18/abortion-rights-campaigner-anniversary-rbg-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Speaking to the Associated Press}}}\uc2 , she said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are in a post-Roe world now. Here in the state of Texas, Roe is no longer in effect... and all it takes is a Republican governor and a Republican legislature. Your state could be exactly the same.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210920eh9k0030e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
