\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Women can say no to sex if Roe falls, says architect of Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Kirchgaessner and Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2021\par 18:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jonathan Mitchell writes in supreme court brief that \u8216\'20\'18women can \u8220\'20\'1Ccontrol their reproductive lives\u8221\'20\'1D without access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The legal architect of the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/185344/20210729162610813_Dobbs%20Amicus%20FINAL%20PDFA.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has argued in a supreme court brief}}}\uc2  that overturning Roe v Wade, the landmark decision which guarantees a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, could cause women to practice abstinence from sexual intercourse as a way to \u8220\'20\'1Ccontrol their reproductive lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Former Texas solicitor general Jonathan Mitchell, who played a pivotal role in designing the legal framework of the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, also argued on behalf of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Texas Right to Life that women would still be able to terminate pregnancies if Roe was overturned by traveling to \u8220\'20\'1Cwealthy pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D states like California and New York with the help of \u8220\'20\'1Ctaxpayer subsidies\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen can \u8216\'20\'18control their reproductive lives\u8217\'20\'19 without access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; they can do so by refraining from sexual intercourse,\u8221\'20\'1D Mitchell wrote in the brief. \u8220\'20\'1COne can imagine a scenario in which a woman has chosen to engage in unprotected (or insufficiently protected) sexual intercourse on the assumption that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be available to her later. But when this court announces the overruling of Roe, that individual can simply change their behavior in response to the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision if she no longer wants to take the risk of an unwanted pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is due to hear a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/184703/20210722161332385_19-1392BriefForPetitioners.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mississippi case}}}\uc2  this term that experts say could lead to the reversal of the Roe decision by the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority. The argument was made in an amicus, or \u8220\'20\'1Cfriend of the court\u8221\'20\'1D, brief in which outside parties can present arguments on cases before the court. The brief was filed on 29 July, about four weeks before Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban went into effect.\par \par \uc2 In the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/185344/20210729162610813_Dobbs%20Amicus%20FINAL%20PDFA.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 same brief}}}\uc2 , which calls for Roe to be overturned, Mitchell and co-counsel Adam Mortara, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist and lawyer who clerked for the supreme court justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , said such a decision could open the door for other \u8220\'20\'1Clawless\u8221\'20\'1D rights and protections to be reversed, including the right to have gay sex and the right to same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers argued that while it was not necessary for the high court to immediately overrule the legal cases that enshrine those rights, \u8220\'20\'1Cneither should the court hesitate to write an opinion that leaves those decisions hanging by a thread\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Those cases (Lawrence, which outlawed criminal sanctions against people who engaged in gay sex, and Obergefell, which legalized same-sex marriage) were \u8220\'20\'1Cfar less hazardous to human life\u8221\'20\'1D, they said, but just \u8220\'20\'1Cas lawless as Roe\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It is common for high-profile cases such as the Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case to elicit amicus briefs by activists and lawyers who are seeking to weigh in on the legal debate.\par \par \uc2 But Mitchell and Mortara\u8217\'20\'19s brief is significant because conservatives on the high court recently ruled in a controversial 5-4 decision to allow a Texas law to stand that was designed by Mitchell and in effect bans abortions after about six weeks, before most people know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 While the majority of the justices stressed that they had not yet ruled on the constitutionality of the Texas law itself, the ruling showed that the majority was receptive to Mitchell\u8217\'20\'19s legal strategy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case the supreme court will hear this term centers on the legality of a Mississippi law that can ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks gestation. Roe gives pregnant women the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to roughly 24 weeks, or the point at which a fetus can live outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to hear the case has alarmed reproductive rights advocates because it blatantly violates the standard set by Roe. Now, in the wake of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, the possibility that the court could overturn the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come into sharp focus. Such a ruling could come in spite of polls that show {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/06/17/key-facts-about-the-abortion-debate-in-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most Americans believe}}}\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most circumstances.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of Mitchell and Mortara\u8217\'20\'19s argument in the Mississippi case lies the view that overturning Roe would not outlaw all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, but would \u8220\'20\'1Cmerely\u8221\'20\'1D return the issue to individual states, which could individually decide whether to ban or restrict terminations. More than half of US states are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2019/08/state-abortion-policy-landscape-hostile-supportive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hostile}}}\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut women who reside in those states can travel to pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states to get their abortions \u8211\'20\'13 and there is no shortage of \u8216\'20\'18\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds\u8217\'20\'19 throughout the country that are eager to pay the travel costs and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related costs for indigent women who are seeking to abort their pregnancies,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 Mitchell has been the subject of media attention since it became clear that he had helped devise the Texas law, which allows private citizens to sue anyone \u8220\'20\'1Caiding or abetting\u8221\'20\'1D a pregnant woman in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past roughly six weeks.\par \par \uc2 This structure, which one legal expert called a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/04/texas-abortion-ruling-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fig leaf}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for the state, led to the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to block the law, with a 6-3 majority describing the law as presenting \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/04/texas-abortion-ruling-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complex antecedent procedural questions}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D that needed to be litigated.\par \par \uc2 Mitchell has been portrayed in some media accounts as an outsider in the conservative judicial network that has led the drive to seat anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  judges and justices in the past decades. But an examination of Mitchell\u8217\'20\'19s record has found that the former clerk for \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , the late conservative supreme court justice, has ties to groups and organizations that are at the heart of the conservative movement. Those organizations, in turn, have direct links to conservative members of the court.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, in emails that were released after a Freedom of Information Act request, Mitchell\u8217\'20\'19s name was raised by Henry Butler, the then dean of the \uc2 George Mason\uc2  Law School, as a person that he and \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , the head of the conservative \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , would consider hiring.\par \par \uc2 Leo, who is known to have selected a short list of potential supreme court nominees for Donald Trump when he entered office, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2018/jul/06/leonard-leo-supreme-court-replacement-trump-justice-nomination-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 been credited by conservatives}}}\uc2  for building a court that would \u8211\'20\'13 someday \u8211\'20\'13 overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Friends and colleagues of Mitchell say that Mitchell and Leo do not have an especially close relationship.\par \par \uc2 In 2019 the powerful, conservative religious law group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/display_990/541660459/05_2021_prefixes_54-56%2F541660459_202006_990_2021051818121539"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom}}}\uc2  (ADF) began paying Mitchell\u8217\'20\'19s private law firm for services listed as \u8220\'20\'1Creligious freedom\u8221\'20\'1D. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s3.documentcloud.org/documents/4388347/Jonathan-F-Mitchell-Financial-Disclosure.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $36,517 payment}}}\uc2  came as Mitchell was simultaneously building case law on his unusual legal theory, the very same provision that would come to define Texas\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban called SB8.\par \par \uc2 In an email, Mitchell declined to respond to the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s questions about the nature of his work for ADF.\par \par \uc2 ADF was in the spotlight in 2020 after it emerged that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s final nominee to serve on the court, Amy Coney Barrett, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/coney-barrett-christian-law-fellowship-blackstone/2020/09/27/7ae41892-fdc5-11ea-b555-4d71a9254f4b_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a paid speaker for a program run by ADF}}}\uc2 , which was established to inspire a \u8220\'20\'1Cdistinctly Christian worldview in every area of law\u8221\'20\'1D. The head of the organization, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/05/michael-farris-amy-coney-barrett-adf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michael Farris, attended the infamous}}}\uc2  Rose Garden event in which Barrett was nominated to replace \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsberg\uc2 . The event would later emerge as a super spreader event in which multiple individuals, including possibly Trump, contracted Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210917eh9h0025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
