\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s largest companies stay silent on state  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban despite outrage\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 1,018 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2021\par 08:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corporations, including American Airlines and \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2 , have not made statements about the ban that all but outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Despite the widespread outrage over a new Texas law that all but outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, only a handful of major companies have spoken out against the legislation that went into effect on 1 September.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 law}}}\uc2  relies on private citizens to carry out the ban by allowing people to file civil lawsuits against anyone who assists a woman in getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after embryonic cardiac activity is detected. A divided \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/sep/02/us-supreme-court-refuses-to-block-radical-texas-abortion-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined}}}\uc2  to block it, allowing it to remain in effect as its legality is worked out in lower courts.\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s largest corporate employers, including American Airlines, \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2 , \uc2 Dell Technologies\uc2 , \uc2 Oracle Corporation\uc2  and Hewlett-Packard Enterprises \u8211\'20\'13 all of which are headquartered in the state \u8211\'20\'13 have not made any public statements about the law.\par \par \uc2 Texas has some of the most business-friendly tax and regulation laws in the country, making it unsurprising that many businesses, including \uc2 Apple\uc2 , Toyota and Tesla, have been luring millions of workers to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s major cities through recent expansion of their operations in the state.\par \par \uc2 After the law went into effect, Governor Greg Abbott said that \u8220\'20\'1Ca lot\u8221\'20\'1D of residents and businesses in the state approved of the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not slowing down businesses coming to the state of Texas, it is accelerating the process of businesses coming to Texas \u8230\'20\'26 They are leaving the very liberal state of California,\u8221\'20\'1D he told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/09/02/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-texas-governor-greg-abbott-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-on-the-street-today.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNBC}}}\uc2 , a nod to the number of high-profile big tech companies that have opened Texas offices in recent years.\par \par \uc2 In the interview, Abbott said that he speaks to Tesla\u8217\'20\'19s CEO, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cfrequently\u8221\'20\'1D and said Musk approves of the state\u8217\'20\'19s social policies. Musk quickly responded on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/elonmusk/status/1433474893316722691?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twitter}}}\uc2  that he believes \u8220\'20\'1Cgovernment should rarely impose its will upon the people and when doing so, should aspire to maximize their cumulative happiness\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat said, I would prefer to stay out of politics,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 While it may be easiest for companies to similarly stay away from \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitics\u8221\'20\'1D, a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://perryundem.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/PerryUndem-TX-Abortion-Law_Survey-Findings_0901.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poll}}}\uc2  has found that the college-educated workforce the major companies hope to attract to Texas are likely to stay away from the state because of the law. Nearly 75% of women and 58% of men said that Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would discourage them from taking a job in the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COther states are competing for people,\u8221\'20\'1D Tammi Wallace, CEO of the Greater Houston LGBT Chamber of Commerce, told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-09-02/lurch-to-the-right-may-imperil-texas-s-attraction-for-employers?sref=6flipp83"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg News}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIf you look at what our state is doing, and then you see another state where they\u8217\'20\'19re not doing some of those things, you might say, \u8216\'20\'18Well, the money\u8217\'20\'19s good, but where do I want to raise my family?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Silence from major corporations is particularly notable given that companies have started to become vocal about progressive causes like LGBTQ rights, gender equality and racism within the last five years.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, hundreds of companies and CEOs signed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.civicalliance.com/votingaccess/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  against restrictive voting laws in April as Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s legislature was passing a series of voting restrictions. The CEOs of American Airlines and \uc2 Dell\uc2  were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/04/01/american-airlines-dell-voting-texas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vocally critical}}}\uc2  of similar voting restrictions that were going up through the Texas legislature.\par \par \uc2 Companies have also been vocal about other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the past. Leaders of more than 180 companies signed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dontbanequality.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  in June 2019 that took up a full-page ad in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  criticizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in light of the blitz of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that were being passed in several states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRestricting access to comprehensive reproductive care, including abortions, threatens the health independence and economic stability of our employees and customers,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement read. \u8220\'20\'1CSimply put, it goes against our values and is bad for business.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s governor, Brian Kemp, signed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in May 2019, leaders from major Hollywood studios, including \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2 , voiced concerns over the bill and said they would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/may/31/georgia-businesses-decline-to-take-stance-on-abortion-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 boycott}}}\uc2  filming in the state, which offers lucrative entertainment tax incentives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think many people who work for us will not want to work there,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , then-CEO of Disney, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/30/business/media/disney-georgia-boycott-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  at the time. A federal judge ultimately blocked the Georgia bill.\par \par \uc2 Jen Stark, senior director of corporate strategy at Tara Health Foundation, said that the law had \u8220\'20\'1Cjust really hit companies by surprise\u8221\'20\'1D and that many companies were trying to come up with a response.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere was a much longer runway as multiple states were passing restrictions in succession and a longer media spotlight\u8221\'20\'1D, Stark said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are many, many conversations being had behind the scenes with large, well-known brands,\u8221\'20\'1D Stark said, adding that the Don\u8217\'20\'19t Ban Equality coalition, which organized the 2019 corporate statement, was working on a statement against the law they were hoping companies would sign.\par \par \uc2 One exception to the general silence over Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law is the cloud-based software giant \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  which is offering to help relocate employees out of the state if they so wish. Referring to the \u8220\'20\'1Cincredibly personal issues\u8221\'20\'1D that the law creates, a message to the company\u8217\'20\'19s entire workforce sent last week said any employee and their family wishing to move elsewhere would receive assistance.\par \par \uc2 Bospar, a small California-based public relations firm, also said that it will offer $10,000 to its six Texas-based employees for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dallasnews.com/business/economy/2021/09/09/california-pr-firm-will-pay-for-its-six-texas-employees-to-relocate-following-abortion-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 relocation}}}\uc2  out of state because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Among the few other companies who have spoken out against the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban are the dating app companies \uc2 Match Group\uc2  and Bumble, ride-hailing companies Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 Yelp\uc2  and Benefit Cosmetics.\par \par \uc2 The city council of Portland, Oregon, is also trying to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/portland-oregon-boycott-texas-businesses-abortion-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 boycott}}}\uc2  millions of dollars of goods and services coming out of Texas, including barring business-related travel to Texas, because of the ban.\par \par \uc2 The Texas lieutenant governor, \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2 , called the boycott a \u8220\'20\'1Ca complete joke\u8221\'20\'1D on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DanPatrick/status/1434909622511546372"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twitter}}}\uc2  and said: \u8220\'20\'1CTexas\u8217\'20\'19s economy is stronger than ever. We value babies and police, they don\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210915eh9f001bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
