\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  vigilantes: how the ban empowers anti-choice citizens to sue\b0\par\par\uc2 Marie Solis \par \uc2 1,408 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par 20:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rupali Sharma of the Lawyering Project explains how SB8 lawsuits could damage the careers and lives of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and patients\par \par \uc2 Last week, the most severe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction in the country became law in Texas. SB8 is a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which amounts to more or less an outright ban, since many people don\u8217\'20\'19t know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant until after the six-week mark. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the state have estimated that the law bans somewhere between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/courts_law/texas-six-week-abortion-ban/2021/09/01/e53cf372-0a6b-11ec-a6dd-296ba7fb2dce_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 85% and 90%}}}\uc2  of the procedures they provide.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps the most shocking part of the law is how it\u8217\'20\'19s enforced: SB8 is enforced not by the state attorney general or other government officials, but by private citizens, who are encouraged to snitch on people who violate the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by filing civil lawsuits against them. (And they are promised a minimum of $10,000 in \u8220\'20\'1Cdamages\u8221\'20\'1D if they win the suit \u8211\'20\'13 what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are calling a \u8220\'20\'1Cbounty\u8221\'20\'1D.) The threat of litigation and financial ruin has forced clinics across the state to comply with the law, even though its core principle \u8211\'20\'13 banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 is still unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 This legal mechanism has made it impossible for reproductive rights groups to pre-empt the law, since they can\u8217\'20\'19t name the typical state enforcers as the defendants in their suits. The supreme court has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/02/sonia-sotomayor-dissent-texas-abortion-ban-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failed to intervene}}}\uc2 , arguing in a Wednesday night {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/sep/06/supreme-court-trump-texas-abortion-law-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shadow docket decision}}}\uc2  that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers didn\u8217\'20\'19t sufficiently address the \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplex and novel antecedent procedural questions\u8221\'20\'1D in their emergency appeal.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian spoke with Rupali Sharma, senior counsel and director at the Lawyering Project, who explained the legal mechanism that has allowed SB8 to go into effect and, as a practical matter, ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The following conversation has been edited for length and clarity.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen dozens of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans get passed by state legislatures across the country, but all of them have been blocked from going into effect until now. What\u8217\'20\'19s different about S\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 8?\par \par \uc2 In the most fundamental way, this is a six-week ban, so it shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have taken effect [because it violates Roe v Wade]. But Texas recognized that bans in other states were being enjoined [blocked by courts] and so lawmakers decided to try to prohibit the state from enforcing the law, and deputizing private residents instead, and that\u8217\'20\'19s the key difference.\par \par \uc2 The way private residents enforce SB8 is by filing lawsuits against anyone they think has violated that law. That in itself is absurd, because \u8220\'20\'1Cviolating the law\u8221\'20\'1D means exercising your constitutional rights. But another reason the law is so absurd is because, typically when you file a lawsuit against someone you have to show that you were injured by them in some way and have a connection to them and what they did. Under SB8 you don\u8217\'20\'19t have to have any connection to the person getting or providing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 you just have to suspect that someone took those actions and you can file a lawsuit against them. There\u8217\'20\'19s no limit on who can sue.\par \par \uc2 And those people are entitled to at least $10,000 in \u8220\'20\'1Cdamages\par \par \uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, which is basically a bounty.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes people get hung up on exactly what SB8 prohibits or permits. Obviously that\u8217\'20\'19s important, but what it really does is \u8211\'20\'13 beyond even encouraging lawsuits that have anything to do with SB8 \u8211\'20\'13 it encourages anyone who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, including people outside of Texas, to sue. It incentivizes frivolous lawsuits, meaning lawsuits that target people for actions that don\u8217\'20\'19t even violate SB8. The point is not to actually regulate [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] \u8211\'20\'13 the point is to stop providers from providing, isolate patients from their support networks and really intimidate and harass people who want to exercise this right. And whether the lawsuit is frivolous or not, people who get sued will have to hire an attorney and go to court, and that\u8217\'20\'19s a huge financial and emotional toll. Some of the plaintiffs in [our] suit are \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds and other practical support funds, and they don\u8217\'20\'19t have huge budgets. For them to get hauled into court would be ruinous.\par \par \uc2 What are the potential consequences for providers in particular? Could they have a hard time applying for licenses to practices in other states, for example? I know many of the providers in Texas actually fly in to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 Physicians face some of the consequences we already talked about, in terms of not necessarily having the resources to fight a frivolous lawsuit. But there\u8217\'20\'19s also the added implications for their licenses. Typically, you have to report if there are suits filed against you in a professional context, regardless of whether those suits have any basis. Before you could reasonably expect that no one would level 30 frivolous lawsuits against you, but SB8 changes that. So now we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about someone who already puts their life at risk to provide care in a really hostile state, and what could happen to their professional reputation. Licensing really depends on the medical licensing boards in each state, but when you provide in hostile states people are looking for a reason to paint you as an immoral or incapable provider.\par \par \uc2 We know that clinic protesters already know the names of many providers in Texas, and write down license plates of people who enter the clinics. How present\par \par \uc2 a threat are these suits for people providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care?\par \par \uc2 Because the supreme court failed to step in, everyone is complying with SB8, which means providers are prevented from doing their jobs. But it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter; they\u8217\'20\'19re still going to be hit with frivolous suits. Providers, clinic staff, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds and patients themselves have put a lot of work and energy into de-stigmatizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the years. They\u8217\'20\'19ve really put themselves out there, knowing the risks \u8211\'20\'13 even before SB8 was in effect \u8211\'20\'13 to position \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the good that it is in these communities. Their names are out there, their faces are out there, because they\u8217\'20\'19re proud of what they do. Our sense is that people who want to harm providers and patients have the information to do it and they\u8217\'20\'19re certainly mobilized to do it.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve already seen Texas Right to Life create this website that encourages its supporters to log tips and complaints about folks violating the law. Regardless of whether people are complying with SB8 \u8211\'20\'13 which they are \u8211\'20\'13 we fully expect that they\u8217\'20\'19re going to get sued nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 I appreciate people wanting to understand the private enforcement mechanisms, but I also want folks to remember that this is a ban and the other stuff is just to isolate it from judicial review.\par \par \uc2 Did the\par \par \uc2 supreme\par \par \uc2 court decision to let the law stand basically validate that the law can be isolated from judicial review?\par \par \uc2 There was a sense that Texas succeeded momentarily, but they won\u8217\'20\'19t succeed ultimately. I fully hope and expect that SB8 will eventually be struck down.\par \par \uc2 If it\par \par \uc2 gets struck down will other states still try to copy it?\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. Why wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t they? Usually whenever there\u8217\'20\'19s a restriction you see it in five other states. [Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers] will do anything to stop care, so why wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t there be copycat laws? That\u8217\'20\'19s another reason why the court needs to step in \u8211\'20\'13 even though Texans are the ones suffering currently, it\u8217\'20\'19s not just about Texas. It\u8217\'20\'19s a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2021/09/republican-states-seek-mimic-texas-abortion-law.html?via=rss_socialflow_twitter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national strategy}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is there anything else people should know about the way\par \par \uc2 SB8 works?\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure folks understand what sort of crisis situation Texas is in right now. Patients\u8217\'20\'19 appointments have been cancelled and no one in the state can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. Maybe some people have the money to travel out of state, and have family members and friends to help them, though that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make it any less wrong. But there are people who absolutely can\u8217\'20\'19t leave the state: They\u8217\'20\'19re undocumented, they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to drive that far, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have a reliable car, they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford gas, they can\u8217\'20\'19t take off of work, they can\u8217\'20\'19t get childcare. They\u8217\'20\'19re stuck. I want people to, for a second, focus on patients and think about what it must be like to be stuck like that.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210913eh9c002mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
