\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: \uc2 Taliban\uc2  name all-male Afghan cabinet\b0\par\par\uc2 Nicola Slawson \par \uc2 1,452 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2021\par 10:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The US is convening a meeting to discuss cooperation with new Afghan government. Plus, animals \u8216\'20\'18shapeshift\u8217\'20\'19 in response to climate crisis\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/taliban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Taliban}}}\uc2  have announced an all-male caretaker government including an interior minister wanted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , on a day when at least two people were killed by violent policing of street protests against the new authorities.\par \par \uc2 The leadership {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/07/taliban-name-afghanistans-new-government"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unveiled on Tuesday}}}\uc2  is drawn entirely from \uc2 Taliban\uc2  ranks, despite promises of an inclusive cabinet, and many of its senior figures are on \uc2 UN\uc2  sanctions lists, which is likely to complicate the group\u8217\'20\'19s search for international recognition.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/08/us-led-meeting-set-out-framework-taliban-cooperation-afghanistan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the US will convene a virtual meeting with as many as 20 countries}}}\uc2  to set a framework for cooperation with the new \uc2 Taliban\uc2  government, amid fears that isolating the militant group could backfire. The meeting is also likely to discuss the terms for giving humanitarian aid, after the \uc2 UN\uc2  warned this week that the Afghan economy was on the brink of collapse.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2021/sep/08/they-came-for-my-daughter-afghan-single-mothers-face-losing-children-under-taliban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 life for single mothers in Afghanistan}}}\uc2  has become increasingly desperate since the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took control.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What will Afghanistan be called? The country will once more be officially known as an Islamic emirate, as it was under \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rule in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Who is leading the country now? The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief, Hibatullah Akhundzada, will be supreme leader.\par \par \uc2 Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court rules criminal penalties for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unconstitutional\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/mexico"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mexico}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s supreme court has struck down a state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, ruling that criminal penalties for terminating pregnancies are unconstitutional, in a decision which advocates say provides a path to decriminalisation across the country.\par \par \uc2 In a unanimous 10-0 ruling, the top court ordered the northern state of Coahuila to remove sanctions for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its criminal code \u8211\'20\'13 with several justices arguing the prohibitions on voluntarily interrupting a pregnancy violated women\u8217\'20\'19s rights to control their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not about the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Justice Luis Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Aguilar, who wrote the court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion for overturning the Coahuila law. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s rather the right to decide of women and persons able to gestate to make decisions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, just across the border from Coahuila in Texas, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  after about six weeks of pregnancy}}}\uc2 , which allows citizens to pursue legal action against women seeking a termination, has led to the creation of a whistleblower website. The site ProLifeWhistleblower.com, however, has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/07/texas-abortion-whistleblower-website-forced-offline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 forced offline}}}\uc2  after being flooded with fake reports, memes and porn.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The decision in Mexico continues a trend in Latin America towards decriminalization as women waving green handkerchiefs have thronged the streets across the continent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theguardian.com/global-development/2021/jan/14/argentina-abortion-legalized-latin-america-reform"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to demand action on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Mexico follows Argentina, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/dec/30/argentina-legalises-abortion-in-landmark-moment-for-womens-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lawmakers voted in December to decriminalise \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  during the first 14 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Britney Spears\u8217\'20\'19 father files to shut down conservatorship\par \par \uc2 Britney Spears\u8217\'20\'19 father has filed an unexpected request to terminate the controversial conservatorship that has controlled the singer\u8217\'20\'19s life for 13 years.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Spears, the conservator of his daughter\u8217\'20\'19s estate, said \u8220\'20\'1Crecent events\u8221\'20\'1D called into question whether she still needed a court to oversee her personal affairs and finances.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for Jamie Spears said: \u8220\'20\'1CAs Mr Spears has said again and again, all he wants is what is best for his daughter. If Ms Spears wants to terminate the conservatorship and believes that she can handle her own life, Mr Spears believes that she should get that chance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Spears\u8217\'20\'19 lawyer, Mathew Rosengart, told the Associated Press news agency in an email that the termination request by her father was a victory and vindication for the singer, but added: \u8220\'20\'1CIt appears that Mr Spears believes he can try to avoid accountability and justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Why is Spears in this situation? She was placed under the conservatorship \u8211\'20\'13 a complex legal arrangement usually reserved for the very old or infirm \u8211\'20\'13 in 2008 after a series of breakdowns.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What has she said about it? The singer has twice addressed the court in recent months, describing the conservatorship as \u8220\'20\'1Cabusive\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What happens next? The next hearing is scheduled for 29 September.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A powerful earthquake has struck south-west Mexico near the beach resort of Acapulco, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/08/mexico-earthquake-tsunami-warning-acapulco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 killing at least one man}}}\uc2  who was crushed by a falling post, and causing rock falls and damaging buildings.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Thousands rallied in the capital of Brazil urging firing squads and coups in support of Jair Bolsonaro after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/07/jair-bolsonaro-supporters-brazil-rally"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polls suggested his presidency was coming off the rails}}}\uc2  in the run-up to next year\u8217\'20\'19s elections.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In the 20 years since 9/11, far-right extremists killed more people in the US than did American-based Islamist fundamentalists. Experts warn this is a form of terrorism has ballooned into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/08/post-911-domestic-terror"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the biggest domestic security threat in the US}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Opening arguments in the highly anticipated trial of Elizabeth Holmes are\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/sep/07/elizabeth-holmes-theranos-trial-begins-latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to begin on Wednesday}}}\par \par \uc2 , with jurors expected to hear prosecutors make the case that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/theranos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Theranos}}}\uc2  founder knowingly defrauded investors and patients.\par \par \uc2 Stat of the day:\par \par \uc2 nearly 300% more Covid patients in US hospitals at weekend than a year ago\par \par \uc2 The number of Covid-19 patients in hospitals across the US this Labor Day weekend was nearly 300% higher than this time last year, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coronavirus.jhu.edu/map.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data}}}\uc2  from \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 . Over the past weekend there were 1.146m weekly cases, compared with 287,235 last year. The average number of deaths was more than 86% higher than the same period last year. The surge in patients comes as the highly contagious Delta variant continues to spread across the US.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: how juggling kids and work in a global pandemic brought us to the brink\par \par \uc2 On social media it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to miss how the usual stresses and strains of parenting have been intensified by the pandemic. Parents write about the difficulties of home schooling while trying to work, of self-isolating with young children or looking after a new baby with no outside help. There\u8217\'20\'19s also the delicate task of managing children\u8217\'20\'19s anxieties throughout the pandemic, and coping with any financial pressures brought about by such a turbulent time. The past 18 months have left many parents and carers feeling overwhelmed, irritable and wrung bone dry. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2021/sep/08/parental-burnout-how-juggling-kids-and-work-in-a-global-pandemic-brought-us-to-the-brink"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Can balance ever be restored?}}}\par \par \uc2 Or this: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19d rather be alone\u8217\'20\'19: the influencers pushing for \u8216\'20\'18relationship minimalism\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A group of young people are not only eschewing excess material items, but also meaningless relationships and \u8220\'20\'1Cemotional clutter\u8221\'20\'1D. If their friendships are non-satisfactory, they declutter, opting for fewer but more quality relationships. If the city they live in no longer sparks joy, they move. And in a quest to trim the amount of unnecessary emotional engagement with the world, some do not even use smartphones or social media. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19d rather be alone than with people who make me feel alone,\u8221\'20\'1D the YouTuber Kelly Stamps says, explaining why she chooses to have just four friends.\par \par \uc2 Climate check:\par \par \uc2 animals \u8216\'20\'18shapeshifting\u8217\'20\'19 in response to climate crisis, research finds\par \par \uc2 Animals are increasingly \u8220\'20\'1Cshapeshifting\u8221\'20\'1D because of the climate crisis, researchers have said. Warm-blooded animals are changing their physiology to adapt to a hotter climate, the scientists found. This includes getting larger beaks, legs and ears to better regulate their body temperature. When animals overheat, birds use their beaks and mammals use their ears to disperse the warmth. Some creatures in warmer climates have historically evolved to have larger beaks or ears to get rid of heat more easily. These differences are becoming more pronounced as the climate warms.\par \par \uc2 Want more environmental stories delivered to your inbox?\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2015/oct/19/sign-up-to-the-green-light-email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up to our Green Light newsletter}}}\uc2  to get the good, bad and essential news on the climate every week\par \par \uc2 Last thing:\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I was sliding towards the drop and couldn\u8217\'20\'19t stop\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 the writer who fell from a mountain\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a career spent walking in and writing about mountains,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2021/sep/08/every-climbers-worst-nightmare-sliding-drop-writer-fell-mountain-cuilllin-skye-black-ridge-wild-peaks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Simon Ingram writes}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cyou do sometimes wonder what you would do if you fell. I lost a friend that way, so thinking about it is unavoidable. Would I scream? Would I go down with stoic acceptance \u8211\'20\'13 or flail and shriek, full of anger or fear? Nobody wants to find out. Of course, if you had the luxury of time, eventually your feelings might stop being about yourself and instead focus on those you were going to let down because of this selfishly dangerous activity. But nobody does that. When it happens, thinking is replaced by instinct, reflex and panic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210908eh98001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
