\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a straight line from US racial segregation to the anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  movement\b0\par\par\uc2 Randall Balmer \par \uc2 914 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2021\par 10:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Leaders of the religious right would have us believe that Roe v Wade mobilized apolitical Christians. The real story is very different\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19s restrictive new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law suggests that the end to country-wide legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be at hand. For white evangelicals, the rank and file of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement who have worked tirelessly to overturn the 1973 Roe v Wade decision, this represents the culmination of efforts that date back to \u8211\'20\'13 well, about 1980.\par \par \uc2 Although leaders of the religious right would have us believe that the Roe decision was the catalyst for their political mobilization in the 1970s, that claim does not withstand historical scrutiny. What prompted evangelical interest in politics, in fact, was a defense of racial segregation.\par \par \uc2 Evangelicals considered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1CCatholic issue\u8221\'20\'1D through most of the 1970s, and there is little in the history of evangelicalism to suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would become a point of interest. Even James Dobson, who later became an implacable foe of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acknowledged after the Roe decision that the Bible was silent on the matter and that it was plausible for an evangelical to hold that \u8220\'20\'1Ca developing embryo or fetus was not regarded as a full human being\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I first began researching the origins of the religious right after a meeting at a Washington hotel conference room in November 1990. The gathering marked the ten-year anniversary of Ronald Reagan\u8217\'20\'19s election to the presidency and, for reasons that are still not entirely clear to me, I was invited to this closed-door celebration. There I encountered a veritable who\u8217\'20\'19s-who of the religious right, including (among others), \uc2 Ralph Reed\uc2  of Christian Coalition; \uc2 Donald Wildmon\uc2  from the \uc2 American Family Association\uc2 ; Richard Land of the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 ; Ed Dobson, one of \uc2 Jerry Falwell\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s acolytes at Moral Majority; Richard Viguerie, the conservative direct-mail mogul; and \uc2 Paul Weyrich\uc2 , cofounder of \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  and architect of the religious right.\par \par \uc2 In the course of the first session, Weyrich tried to make a point to his religious right brethren (no women attended the conference, as I recall). Remember, he said animatedly, that the religious right did not come together in response to the Roe decision. No, Weyrich insisted, what got the movement going as a political movement was the attempt on the part of the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  (IRS) to rescind the tax-exempt status of Bob Jones University because of its racially discriminatory policies, including a ban on interracial dating that the university maintained until 2000.\par \par \uc2 During a break following that session, I approached Weyrich to ensure that I had heard him correctly. He was emphatic that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had nothing whatsoever to do with the genesis of the religious right. He added that he\u8217\'20\'19d been trying since the Goldwater campaign in 1964 to interest evangelicals in politics. Nothing caught their attention, he insisted \u8211\'20\'13 school prayer, pornography, equal rights for women, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 until the IRS began to challenge the tax exemption of Bob Jones University and other whites-only segregation academies.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, in 1971 the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  had passed a resolution calling to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When the Roe decision was handed down, some evangelicals applauded the ruling as marking an appropriate distinction between personal morality and public policy. Although he later \u8211\'20\'13 14 years later \u8211\'20\'13 claimed that opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the catalyst for his political activism, \uc2 Jerry Falwell\uc2  did not preach his first anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sermon until February 1978, more than five years after Roe.\par \par \uc2 Falwell, who had founded his own segregation academy in 1967, was eager to join forces with Weyrich and others to mount a defense against the IRS and its attempts to enforce the Brown v Board of Education decision of 1954 and the Civil Rights Act of 1964. \u8220\'20\'1CIn some states,\u8221\'20\'1D Falwell famously groused, \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s easier to open a massage parlor than a Christian school.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So how did evangelicals become interested in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? As nearly as I can tell from my conversation with Weyrich, during a conference call with Falwell and other evangelicals strategizing about how to retain their tax exemptions, someone suggested that they might have the makings of a political movement and wondered what other issues would work for them. Several suggestions followed, and then a voice on the line said, \u8220\'20\'1CHow about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Still, it took some time for opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take hold among evangelicals. According to Frank Schaeffer \u8211\'20\'13 who produced a series of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  films called Whatever Happened to the Human Race?, featuring his father, \uc2 Francis Schaeffer\uc2 , and C Everett Koop, who later became Ronald Reagan\u8217\'20\'19s surgeon general \u8211\'20\'13 the evangelical response was at best tepid when the films appeared early in 1979.\par \par \uc2 And when Reagan addressed 20,000 cheering evangelicals in August 1980, he mentioned his support for creationism and criticized the IRS for its supposed vendetta against evangelical schools. He said nothing whatsoever about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Only in the early 1980s did opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  finally become an evangelical battle cry.\par \par \uc2 The beauty of the religious right\u8217\'20\'19s embrace of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a political issue is that it allowed leaders to camouflage the real origins of their movement: the defense of racial segregation in evangelical institutions.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Randall Balmer, a professor at Dartmouth College, is the author of more than a dozen books, including Bad Faith: Race and the Rise of the Religious Right\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210908eh98001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
