\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18whistleblower\u8217\'20\'19 website forced offline\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul \par \uc2 636 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2021\par 00:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Site was removed from GoDaddy before being censored by new host Epik, known for providing services to far-right groups\par \par \uc2 Troubles are mounting for a Texas website used to report violators of the state\u8217\'20\'19s extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation after the site was forced offline by two different web hosting platforms.\par \par \uc2 The site ProLifeWhistleblower.com was removed from its original web host by the provider GoDaddy on Friday before being suspended by its new host, an agency known for providing services to far-right groups .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor all intents and purposes it is offline,\u8221\'20\'1D Ronald Guilmette, a web infrastructure expert, told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThey are having technical difficulties. My personal speculation is that they are going to have trouble keeping it online moving forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As of Tuesday, ProLifeWhistleblower.com redirects to Texas Right to Life\u8217\'20\'19s main website.\par \par \uc2 Created by Texas Right to Life, an evangelical Christian group, the site allowed people to anonymously submit information about potential violations of the new law \u8211\'20\'13 which makes it illegal to help women in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas}}}\uc2  access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the sixth week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, internet users have protested against the site by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/02/texas-abortion-law-tiktok-reddit-whistleblower"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flooding it with false reports}}}\uc2 , memes and even porn in the hopes of rendering it less effective.\par \par \uc2 The website\u8217\'20\'19s difficulties were compounded when GoDaddy, which provides the servers where the website lives, said the site had violated its privacy policies that bar the sharing of third-party personal information including data related to medical issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The site was then moved to Epik, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whois.com/whois/prolifewhistleblower.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 domain registration data}}}\uc2  first {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2021/09/texas-abortion-snitch-website-kicked-off-godaddy-for-invading-peoples-privacy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported by Ars Technica}}}\uc2 . Epik is known for its \u8220\'20\'1Canything goes\u8221\'20\'1D attitude towards web hosting, servicing sites that other companies have deplatformed elsewhere for hate speech and other content violations \u8211\'20\'13 including 8chan, Gab and Parler.\par \par \uc2 According to reports from the Daily Beast, Epik contacted the website about potential violation of its own rules concerning the collection of medical data about people obtaining abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe contacted the owner of the domain, who agreed to disable the collection of user submissions on this domain,\u8221\'20\'1D it said in a statement to the Daily Beast.\par \par \uc2 Epik representatives did not respond to a message seeking comment on Tuesday. As of the time of publishing at 2pm PST on Tuesday, Epik remained the host for ProLifeWhistleblower.com, according to domain registration data available online, although the site does not appear to be operational.\par \par \uc2 A Texas Right to Life spokeswoman, Kimberlyn Schwartz, said on Tuesday that the website was in the process of moving to a new host, but was not yet disclosing which one.\par \par \uc2 The original website allowed anyone to submit an anonymous \u8220\'20\'1Creport\u8221\'20\'1D of someone illegally obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including a section where images can be uploaded for proof.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAny Texan can bring a lawsuit against an abortionist or someone aiding and abetting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D the website reads, and those proved to be violating the law liable for a minimum of $10,000 in damages.\par \par \uc2 Schwartz said they were working to get the tipster website back up but noted that in many ways it was symbolic since anyone can report a violation. And, she said, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics appear to be complying with the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think that people see the whistleblower website as a symbol of the law but the law is still enforced, with or without our website,\u8221\'20\'1D Schwartz said, adding, \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not the only way that people can report violations of the law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Parma, Texas Right to Life\u8217\'20\'19s senior legislative associate, said they expected people to try to overwhelm the site with fake tips, adding \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re thankful for the publicity to the website that\u8217\'20\'19s coming from all of this chatter about it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed to this report\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210908eh980002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
