\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 The post-Trump supreme court: where hard-won rights die in darkness\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Pilkington in New York \par \uc2 1,374 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2021\par 06:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Orders \u8211\'20\'13 such as on the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law \u8211\'20\'13 issued in the dead of night on the so-called \u8216\'20\'18shadow docket\u8217\'20\'19 threaten the court\u8217\'20\'19s legitimacy and US democracy\par \par \uc2 It began with a whimper, and ended in a bang. Last Tuesday night, as the clock struck midnight, a booming silence engulfed the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  in Washington \u8211\'20\'13 and with it an extreme new anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law was allowed to go into effect in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive inaction \u8211\'20\'13 remaining quiet as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legiscan.com/TX/text/SB8/id/2395961"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SB 8}}}\uc2  was enacted \u8211\'20\'13 overturned the constitutional right of more than 6 million Texas women to control their own bodies and punched a giant hole through the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s own groundbreaking 1973 ruling, Roe v Wade. The bizarre disappearing act of the court\u8217\'20\'19s nine justices led to nationwide bemusement: how could the top judges in the land allow a pillar of American democracy to be toppled at dead of night, with barely any discussion, and without a single word of explanation?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court\u8217\'20\'19s failure to rule on the request for an injunction before the Texas law took effect was a grave institutional failure,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kate Shaw, a law professor at Cardozo Law who co-hosts the Strict Scrutiny {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://strictscrutinypodcast.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 podcast}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe silence sent a clear message that states can act in ways that are flagrantly unconstitutional under settled law where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One night later, also under cover of midnight, the court\u8217\'20\'19s whimper burst into a full-blown explosion. By a majority of five votes to four, the rightwing core of justices \u8211\'20\'13 including all three of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/07/26/politics/trump-kavanaugh-gorsuch-barrett-supreme-court/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s controversial appointees}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 refused to intervene to block the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 Their decision, unsigned and laid out in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/21a24_8759.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 single paragraph}}}\uc2 , left close observers of the supreme court shellshocked. Here was the highest court in the land willingly allowing the state of Texas to ride roughshod over its own legal authority by violating the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the court itself had established almost half a century ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court\u8217\'20\'19s hostility to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has led it to approve a law that is not only plainly unconstitutional, but a threat to the social fabric and to the rule of law,\u8221\'20\'1D Shaw told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Roe bans abortions after a fetus reaches {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2021/05/18/scotus-abortion-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 viability}}}\uc2  at around 24 weeks, SB 8 imposes an effective six-week ban that would prevent up to 90% of all Texas terminations from going ahead. \u8220\'20\'1CThe law is a blatantly unconstitutional ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  around six weeks in pregnancy, before most people know they are pregnant. It violates nearly 50 years of Supreme Court precedent,\u8221\'20\'1D said the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Brigitte Amiri, a member of the legal team that had petitioned the court to intervene and stop the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s extreme turn this week has already had a devastating impact on the women of Texas, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/politics-news/texas-abortion-clinics-turning-away-patients-ahead-draconian-new-law-n1278184"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turning patients away}}}\uc2 . SB 8 encourages private citizens to act as vigilantes by suing anyone who helps a woman obtain care.\par \par \uc2 If the consequences for women are dire, the implications for the long-term health of American democracy are no less ominous. In her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/21a24_8759.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dissenting opinion}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  describes SB 8 as a \u8220\'20\'1Cbreathtaking act of defiance \u8211\'20\'13 of the constitution, of this court\u8217\'20\'19s precedents, and of the rights of women seeking abortions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 By stipulating that no state official could execute the new anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provisions, and by outsourcing enforcement instead to ordinary citizens, the Republican framers of SB 8 had played a dastardly trick on the US legal system. As Sotomayor explained, it was an audacious ploy designed to evade the scrutiny of federal courts by removing the accountability of state officials.\par \par \uc2 Despite the unsubtle nature of the ruse, the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s rightwing majority blithely went along with it. Sotomayor called her fellow justices\u8217\'20\'19 decision \u8220\'20\'1Cstunning\u8221\'20\'1D, and wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CToday, the court finally tells the nation that it declined to act because, in short, the state\u8217\'20\'19s gambit worked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stephen Vladeck, a professor of constitutional law at the University of Texas at Austin, warned that the majority\u8217\'20\'19s willingness to play along with the antics of hyperpartisan state Republicans could have calamitous long-term consequences. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a court telling Republican legislators that tricks are fine, that shenanigans are fine \u8211\'20\'13 as long as we like the bottom line that those tricks and shenanigans produce.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Vladeck points to a decision in April in which the same five rightwing justices \u8211\'20\'13 including Trump\u8217\'20\'19s three appointees, \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett \u8211\'20\'13 did the exact opposite to what they did this week. In another {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/04/10/politics/supreme-court-california-covid-restrictions-religious-activities/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unsigned order}}}\uc2 , issued similarly at dead of night, they moved this time to block the provision at hand.\par \par \uc2 In this instance, the five justices combined forces to foil the will of officials in California \u8211\'20\'13 a Democratic state \u8211\'20\'13 who were attempting to prevent the spread of Covid-19 by imposing restrictions on at-home gatherings. Same five justices, same late-night order, diametrically opposed outcome. Republicans get their way in Texas, Democrats are blocked in California. The contrast left little to the imagination.\par \par \uc2 That is the part that Vladeck said he was finding hardest to fathom. By being seen to operate in such overtly contradictory and partisan ways, the dominant rightwing group of justices runs the risk of existentially damaging the reputation and standing of the highest court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is increasingly apparent that a majority of the justices are simply indifferent to what a large number of Americans think about the court as an institution,\u8221\'20\'1D Vladeck said. \u8220\'20\'1CDo they not appreciate that they are taking steps widely perceived to be harming their legitimacy or, even worse, do they just not care?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The irony is that only three months ago, at the end of the 2020-21 term, many leading observers were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/18/us/politics/supreme-court-conservatives-liberals.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 commenting}}}\uc2  that the new court remade in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s image had failed to have the extreme rightward thrust that had been widely predicted. As the New York Times\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court correspondent, Adam Liptak, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/18/us/politics/supreme-court-conservatives-liberals.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2 , the \u8220\'20\'1Cliberal members are having a surprisingly good run\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It now seems that Liptak and other analysts may have been looking in the wrong direction. By focusing on the merits rulings of the court \u8211\'20\'13 the fully fledged cases involving oral hearings and expansive judgments \u8211\'20\'13 the pundits downplayed where the real action was happening: in the shadows.\par \par \uc2 The so-called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/aug/31/supreme-court-us-cases-shadow-docket"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cshadow docket}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 in which emergency orders are issued in the dead of night with virtually no debate and exceedingly little public justification \u8211\'20\'13 has mushroomed in recent years. It now accounts for most of the court\u8217\'20\'19s business, and as it grows it is increasingly dragging the nation\u8217\'20\'19s most significant jurisprudence into the darkness.\par \par \uc2 The liberal justice Elena Kagan raised the shadow docket specifically in her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/21a24_8759.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dissent}}}\uc2  to Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s Texas decision. It was the first time that any member of the court had challenged head-on the untrammeled rise of the practice.\par \par \uc2 Kagan berated the rightwing majority for allowing the patently unconstitutional Texas law to go ahead after less than 72 hours\u8217\'20\'19 thought and with minimal review of the evidence. She said the majority \u8220\'20\'1Cbarely bothers to explain its conclusion\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 All in all, she wrote, the events of this week were \u8220\'20\'1Cemblematic of too much of this court\u8217\'20\'19s shadow-docket decision-making \u8211\'20\'13 which every day becomes more unreasoned, inconsistent, and impossible to defend.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The proliferation of unaccountable orders issued at midnight from a majority of justices who are willing to cooperate with the undisguised trickery of ideologically driven state Republicans hardly bodes well for the future of the supreme court, or America. When the new term starts next month, the court will be considering Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, a case focusing on Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s law banning abortions at 15 weeks which has the potential to put the final nail in the coffin of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe bloom is off the rose that this new supreme court is going to have any moderation,\u8221\'20\'1D Vladeck said. \u8220\'20\'1CNo one who watches the court closely would put any money on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  looking the same at the end of this upcoming term as it does today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210906eh96000jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
