\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Feminists warned about America\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  crisis for years. We were written off as hysterical\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,271 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Why has the effective end of Roe v Wade been met with shock by so many corners of political life?\par \par \uc2 This was predictable. In fact, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2018/oct/10/kavanaugh-supreme-court-appointment-abortion-roe-v-wade-future"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it was predicted}}}\uc2 . The end of Roe v Wade and nationwide protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights became likely in 2016, the night that Donald Trump was elected. It became inevitable in 2018, when \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2 , the fifth pro-choice vote, retired and handed his seat to Trump to fill. But the end of nationwide legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America has been coming for decades, and there has been no ambiguity about the appetite for Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturn on the American right. And crucially, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecut.com/2019/05/how-extreme-abortion-bans-in-alabama-and-georgia-happened.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 feminists have been sounding the alarm}}}\uc2  for decades, warning in increasingly desperate terms that gradual erosions of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s protections in the law had led to a rapid and widespread loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the form of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s eventual downfall was a surprise. Few thought that Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fatal case would be over Texas\u8217\'20\'19s new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, with its privatized enforcement system of bounty-hunting civil suits designed to elide judicial review. And among a sea of legal observers, only Cardozo law professor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://strictscrutinypodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/Wilding-Out-Transcript.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kate Shaw}}}\uc2  seems to have predicted that the court would dispose of a long-established constitutional right in so rushed and perfunctory a proceeding as a late-night order on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/aug/31/supreme-court-us-cases-shadow-docket"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shadow docket}}}\uc2 . But this outcome was never in doubt. Trump promised to appoint antichoice judges. He kept that promise. This week his three appointees \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett, joined by \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 did what all of them know they were put on the court to do. They allowed the first state to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its borders.\par \par \uc2 So why has the effective end of Roe v Wade, coming in a one paragraph order in the wee hours of Thursday morning, been met with shock by so many corners of political life? The Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s control of the federal judiciary had left little doubt that those judges most inclined to strip women of their rights would have both the power and the opportunity to do so. And yet politicians, pundits, and legal observers had for years assured the public that the justices would not gut \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, despite the clear evidence that they would. We were assured that the Republicans on the court were less determined to gut Roe than they appeared to be, and that those worried about the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were overreacting.\par \par \uc2 The court would not gut Roe, we were told by politicians and academics, because they said they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t. Kavanaugh, the ruddy-faced Trump appointee, had referred to Roe as \u8220\'20\'1Cimportant precedent\u8221\'20\'1D. That this rather tepid comment was a disingenuous bit of posturing meant to ease his confirmation to the court was evident to everyone. Nevertheless, defenders of the confirmation process implored the public to treat it as if it had been uttered in good faith.\par \par \uc2 In a speech announcing her decision to vote to confirm Kavanaugh, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2018/10/why-susan-collins-voting-brett-kavanaugh-supreme-court/572341/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senator Susan Collins}}}\uc2  said that she believed Kavanaugh would not vote to overturn Roe, or to gut it procedurally, because \u8220\'20\'1Chis views on honoring precedent would preclude attempts to do by stealth that which one has committed not to do overtly.\u8221\'20\'1D Of course, the court, with Kavanaugh\u8217\'20\'19s help, did effectively overturn Roe \u8220\'20\'1Cby stealth\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 in an unsigned order in the middle of the night.\par \par \uc2 Of the feminists who opposed his nomination, Collins was dismissive, even patronizing. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have seen special-interest groups whip their followers into a frenzy by spreading misrepresentations and outright falsehoods about Judge Kavanaugh\u8217\'20\'19s judicial record.\u8221\'20\'1D She condemned these women\u8217\'20\'19s concerns as \u8220\'20\'1Cover-the-top rhetoric and distortions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The court would not gut Roe, we were told by the legal world, because the justices were too professional. Barrett, the third of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s appointees, had been a member of an antichoice faculty group while a law professor at Notre Dame. She had given a lecture to a Right to Life group; she had signed a letter condemning Roe and its \u8220\'20\'1Cbrutal legacy\u8221\'20\'1D. And yet despite Barrett\u8217\'20\'19s extremist and evidently very passionately held views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people posing as serious told us that we could not know how she would vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, that the opinions and worldviews of judges would somehow not affect their legal judgement. \u8220\'20\'1CMy personal views don\u8217\'20\'19t have anything to do with the way I would decide cases,\u8221\'20\'1D Barrett told Senator \uc2 Patrick Leahy\uc2  when she was asked about her lengthy history of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy. The statement insulted both Leahy\u8217\'20\'19s intelligence, and ours.\par \par \uc2 And yet as conservative, antichoice judges consolidated their power, several myths about the court persisted. We were told that the people who looked like rabidly conservative justices were really reasoned moderates; or that at least they would be professional and impartial in their judgements; or that at least the removal of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would move slowly. These myths were presented as the only serious way to understand the court. Feminist claims that what appeared to be happening really was happening \u8211\'20\'13 that the judiciary really had been taken over by antichoice zealots, that the ability of women to control their own bodies and lives would soon be stripped away \u8211\'20\'13 were labeled as delusional and silly. Faith in the integrity of the conservative justices was cast as informed, mature, and intelligent. And it was contrasted with the supposed hysteria of feminists, whose passion and fear was taken as a sign of their own delusion, not as an indication of the seriousness of the problem.\par \par \uc2 Feminist claims that what appeared to be happening really was happening were labeled as delusional and silly\par \par \uc2 This notion, that the only intelligent response to a threat to women\u8217\'20\'19s rights is to be calm, blas\u233\'00\'E9, and preemptively assured that nothing very bad or important will result, has been weaponized with particular insidiousness over the course of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate during the past five years. In the halls of power, contempt for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists was nearly complete.\par \par \uc2 After Kennedy\u8217\'20\'19s resignation, the CNN host Brian Stelter took to social media to scold a liberal activist for her fear of a Roe reversal. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not \u8216\'20\'18a few steps away from the Handmaid\u8217\'20\'19s Tale\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think this kind of fear-mongering helps anybody.\u8221\'20\'1D Confronted with women opposed to the confirmation of Kavanaugh, Senator {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rewirenewsgroup.com/article/2018/09/05/ben-sasse-women-arent-being-hysterical-theyre-begging-the-government-not-to-kill-them/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ben Sasse}}}\uc2  all but rolled his eyes. There had been, he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cscreaming protesters saying \u8216\'20\'18women are going to die\u8217\'20\'19 at every hearing for decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The insistence that Roe is not in danger, and that women\u8217\'20\'19s fear is silly, persists even now, after the court has effectively ended Roe. \u8220\'20\'1CNow breathe,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote the law professor Jonathan Turley in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jonathanturley.org/2021/09/02/supreme-court-rejects-injunction-of-texas-abortion-law-media-erupts-with-roe-obituaries/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blogpost}}}\uc2  urging women\u8217\'20\'19s rights advocates to calm down, as if they were toddlers in the midst of a temper tantrum. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is ridiculous to say that it was some manufactured excuse for a partisan ruling.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Is it ridiculous? The public has no real reason to believe that the supreme court is acting in good faith \u8211\'20\'13 aside from the repeated assurances of supposed experts whose predictions have usually been wrong. Instead, it was the so-called alarmist feminists, the ones warning about manufactured excuses for partisan attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who got their predictions mostly right. Maybe these women are not so ridiculous after all. Maybe it\u8217\'20\'19s time to start listening to them.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210904eh940003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
