\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Roe v Wade is a husk\u8217\'20\'19: anguish and anger in Texas after  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par 06:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Climate of fear descends on state for clinic workers, patients and others after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority decision on Wednesday\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty hunters began calling Amy Hagstrom Miller\u8217\'20\'19s chain of four independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Texas just hours after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/sep/02/us-supreme-court-refuses-to-block-radical-texas-abortion-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the supreme court issued a two-paragraph order}}}\uc2  that effectively ended access to 85% of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in the state.\par \par \uc2 Their apparent hope is to make an appointment for an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , catch out the clinic and sue for a $10,000 reward, the bounty Texas lawmakers have placed on the heads of anyone \u8211\'20\'13 from cab drivers to clergy \u8211\'20\'13 who dare aid a woman in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks gestation, before most know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 For clinic workers, as well as so many other people, a climate of fear has now descended on Texas. \u8220\'20\'1CThe staff are directly experiencing people\u8217\'20\'19s anguish and fear and anger about the law that\u8217\'20\'19s coming from all the patients,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hagstrom Miller, founder and CEO of Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been really, really rough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Before the stunning order, women nationally in the US had a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the point a fetus can survive outside the womb, generally understood to be 24 weeks. The right was secured by the landmark 1973 supreme court case Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Congress never secured the right in statute, and relied on a supreme court precedent for nearly five decades. Social conservatives took a different tack, and over the same period passed more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/04/2021-track-become-most-devastating-antiabortion-state-legislative-session-decades"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1,300 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions}}}\uc2 , challenging Roe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/may/17/abortion-case-supreme-court-threatens-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 again}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2020/mar/04/us-supreme-court-louisiana-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 again}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 again}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, on Wednesday, conservatives were granted an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/aug/30/abortion-law-federal-court-decision-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extraordinary victory}}}\uc2 . The court refused to block one of their patently unconstitutional laws. As quickly as a court decision had granted a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, it excised Texas women from its protections and allowed a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to be imposed on 6 million women of reproductive age as the case winds through courts, ending access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for most pregnant people in the state now and for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 In handing down the order, the court also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/03/texas-abortion-republicans-six-states-arkansas-florida-indiana-mississippi-north-south-dakota"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laid out a roadmap}}}\uc2  for severe restrictions in dozens of states hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, almost certainly emboldened anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists globally and broke with a decades-long global movement to liberalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Crucially, it also revealed one of Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s greatest successes \u8211\'20\'13 confirming three supreme court justices on a bench of nine and more than 200 federal court judges. Now, the decades-long rightwing project to appoint conservative jurists appears to have one of its greatest prizes nearly in its reach \u8211\'20\'13 the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America and the gutting of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRoe v Wade is a husk, a desiccated dry husk of a ruling at this point,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anu Kumar, the CEO of Ipas, a US-based reproductive rights organization whose work focuses on helping women internationally obtain access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIf this Texas law did not trigger the supreme court to intervene, then we all need to be very concerned about what will.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since May, a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and activists have fought to stop Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law, called SB8, which makes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal after embryonic cardiac activity can be detected, about six weeks.\par \par \uc2 But, unlike \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans passed by states before, all universally blocked by courts, Texas tried a long-shot legal strategy to evade federal court scrutiny. The state banned officials from enforcing the law, and gave private citizens a right to sue one another should they suspect someone \u8211\'20\'13 anyone \u8211\'20\'13 helped a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past this early point in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A citizen\u8217\'20\'19s prize for a successful suit would be $10,000 and attorneys\u8217\'20\'19 fees. Defendants would not be able to recoup any such losses. Lawmakers also rewrote the rules of civil litigation to favor these new bounty hunters, allowing anyone anywhere to sue. In doing so, legislators tossed into the fire the foundational principles of \u8220\'20\'1Cstanding\u8221\'20\'1D (you actually need to be involved to sue) and venue (one of the parties must live or work where the suit is filed).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is obvious to anyone who has taken first-year constitutional law that this Texas law is unconstitutional,\u8221\'20\'1D said Melissa Murray, the Frederick I and Grace Stokes professor of law at New York University, and an expert on reproductive rights law. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is an incredibly stunning development in terms of how cases are litigated and how constitutional rights are protected in this country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Increasingly frantic attorneys for a coalition of reproductive rights groups worked until the 11th hour to stop the law from going into effect. They were blocked by an appeals court, then petitioned to the supreme court. They were met by 23 hours of supreme court silence after the law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 That silence from a court known to act swiftly in emergencies was followed by what is called a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/aug/31/supreme-court-us-cases-shadow-docket"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shadow docket}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D decision, in which an order is issued without public argument or trial. In the order, a five-four majority said the court would let the law stand because of \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplex antecedent procedural questions\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 in other words, the very byzantine enforcement structure the state itself had crafted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court silently acquiesced in a state\u8217\'20\'19s enactment of a law that flouts nearly 50 years of federal precedents,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Justice Sonia Sotomayer, considered part of the court\u8217\'20\'19s liberal wing.\par \par \uc2 Even Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , a conservative skeptic of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/21a24_8759.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joined liberal justices in dissent}}}\uc2 , arguing the law should be blocked as it winds through courts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA law like this upheld in a fashion like this is corrosive of community and of the rule of law and does a disservice to any principle it could possibly hope to vindicate,\u8221\'20\'1D said Reva Siegel, the Nicholas deB Katzenbach professor of Law at Yale Law School. Its complex enforcement mechanism was little more than a \u8220\'20\'1Cfig leaf\u8221\'20\'1D for the state, she said.\par \par \uc2 The state\u8217\'20\'19s largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobbying organization quickly embraced its new role as enablers of enforcement. The organization has published a website for \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prolifewhistleblower.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 whistleblower}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D investigations, and encouraged citizens to compile dossiers on those suspected of \u8220\'20\'1Caiding and abetting\u8221\'20\'1D those seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 It is obvious to anyone who has taken first-year constitutional law that this Texas law is unconstitutional\par \par \uc2 Melissa Murray\par \par \uc2 Hagstrom Miller, whose clinics are high-profile because she has challenged unconstitutional Texas laws in the past, said she is already being targeted. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can tell the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  folks are calling, we can tell they\u8217\'20\'19re booking appointments,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1COur staff are on alert and trying to be as warm and comforting of our patients, while also being on watch for these vigilantes \u8211\'20\'13 both.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the hours preceding the ban, Hagstrom Miller\u8217\'20\'19s staff in Forth Worth worked until midnight the day the ban went into effect to help as many women as possible, performing 67 abortions in 17 hours, according to the news outlet {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2021/09/abortion-texas-whole-womans-clinic/?utm_campaign=19th-social&utm_source=twitter&utm_medium=social"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 19th}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All this comes in the context of upcoming hearings in another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case from Mississippi, Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, in which the supreme court was slated to consider a 15-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That case now also appears likely to further strangle the rights provided in Roe v Wade. However, few expected the court to so clearly signal it is \u8220\'20\'1Cutterly hostile to the prospect of reproductive rights\u8221\'20\'1D, in Murray\u8217\'20\'19s words, so early, and in such a fashion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been gut-wrenching for the staff to really be agents of the state and tell people they can no longer have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hagstrom Miller.\par \par \uc2 Hagstrom Miller has already turned away every single woman who sought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at her Rio Grande Valley location on the Texas-Mexico border in McAllen. They were all past six weeks gestation.\par \par \uc2 The decision has immediately opened whole categories of questions.\par \par \uc2 Most immediately, what will happen to pregnant people seeking abortions in Texas? Most will now simply be unable to travel out of state for abortions, and these people will disproportionately be the poor, people of color and the young. Illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not, after all, end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It only ends safe, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What will happen to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics of Texas? The longer the law stands, the more will shutter, eroding rights permanently no matter what twists and turns the legal and political fight now takes. How many states will move to follow Texas\u8217\'20\'19s lead? Many, perhaps half of US states. Lawmakers in Florida have already signaled their intent to do so.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been gut-wrenching for the staff to really be agents of the state and tell people they can no longer have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\par \par \uc2 Will Congress amend its nearly five-decade failure to enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in law? And where does this new law place the US, which holds itself up as the beacon of freedom, in the global landscape? With this policy, the US joins Brazil, Egypt, El Salvador, Nicaragua, Iraq and Mauritania, according to Kumar, and clearly violates international human rights frameworks.\par \par \uc2 What is clear is while there is much shock, there is less surprise. The Texas court decision was the obvious end of a nearly five decade-long campaign waged by social conservatives to end the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US \u8211\'20\'13 a campaign at which they have been succeeding, particularly in the last decade.\par \par \uc2 This is the \u8220\'20\'1Cnew Texas\u8221\'20\'1D, as Hagstrom Miller said, a state the conservative movement has chosen as a proving ground for restrictions on both voting and reproductive rights, both of which are emblematic of the post-Trump conservative movement\u8217\'20\'19s rejection of broader democratic principles.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVigilantism is the key in this law, this is why this is so incredibly scary,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kumar. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd so close to fascism.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210904eh9400038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
