\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not just Texas \u8211\'20\'13 anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  activists are targeting women\u8217\'20\'19s rights in Europe\b0\par\par\uc2 Mara Clarke \par \uc2 1,145 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par 17:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Draconian laws in European countries and the effects of Brexit and Covid-19 are making safe terminations harder to access \uc2 Mara Clarke\uc2  is the founder of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Support Network\par \par \uc2 The state of Texas now has the most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restrictive \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  law}}}\uc2  to be passed in the US since Roe v Wade legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973. Texas residents must now add out-of-state travel to the many barriers that already exist to getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There has been an outpouring of support for Texas women from people in the UK and \uc2 European Union\uc2 , but it\u8217\'20\'19s worth remembering that while the UK and Europe are an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  utopia compared with some American states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is still not guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s start with the UK. Due to the herculean efforts of activists (and despite the best efforts of a foot-dragging, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  government), Northern Ireland now has limited access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Alliance for Choice, the campaign group in Northern Ireland, says that proper access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still not guaranteed across the country. Women are still waiting for the department of health to commission \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, telemedicine and more types of available \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Currently, the majority of abortions happen in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy and are performed with pills, which carry a small risk of failure. Because there is virtually no provision for abortions after the first trimester, people seeking these services are still forced to travel to England.\par \par \uc2 Many people in Scotland who need late abortions also have to travel to England due to a lack of facilities, despite the law allowing terminations at 24 weeks. In England and Wales, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still regulated by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bma.org.uk/media/1963/bma-removal-of-criminal-sanctions-for-abortion-position-paper-july-2019.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminal code}}}\uc2 , and waiting times for abortions vary across the country. Whether you are able to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly often depends on where you live.\par \par \uc2 The charity I founded, \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Support Network (ASN), has years of experience helping people who have been forced by a combination of draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and poverty to access abortions abroad. We offer practical information and financial support, and are funded almost entirely by private individuals. We do this work because we believe \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t afford an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D should never be the only reason someone continues a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Initially we worked in Ireland (where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now legal up to 12 weeks, and in very restricted circumstances after this), the Isle of Man (where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was made legal in 2019) and Northern Ireland (where decriminalisation only happened very recently). In 2019, we expanded to Malta, which has some of the most draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in Europe: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal even in cases where it will save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life. We expanded to Gibraltar in the same year. Although it recently voted to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some circumstances, Gibraltar\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were previously more draconian than those in Texas; \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only allowed in cases where it would save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 At the end of 2019 we expanded our work to Poland as part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Without Borders initiative. Poland, which already had one of the most prohibitive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in Europe, recently made its law even more severe, ruling that 98% of the 1,000-2,000 legal abortions performed by the state each year were \u8220\'20\'1Cunconstitutional\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Despite how busy our charity is, we are now looking at expanding into additional countries that need support. Andorra has joined Malta in making all abortions illegal, even where they would save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life. Other European countries have decent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws but still effectively limit access, such as Italy, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/mar/11/italian-gynaecologists-refuse-abortions-miscarriages"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 69% of gynaecologists}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cconscientiously object\u8221\'20\'1D to performing abortions, Romania, where activists say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being quietly phased out, and Hungary, where the government has successfully reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Our work is particularly focused on ensuring that people can access abortions beyond the first trimester. Most countries in Europe offer access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the first 12 to 14 weeks of pregnancy. In countries that don\u8217\'20\'19t, many people rely on NGOs such as Women Help Women and Women on Web, which send early medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to countries where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is restricted. Thankfully, people are increasingly aware of these pills, which are on the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s list of essential medicines and offer a safe means of ending a pregnancy. When we first founded ASN, women in Ireland and Northern Ireland told us they had tried far more dangerous things than pills.\par \par \uc2 Getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in a pregnancy, when pills are no longer suitable, can be difficult depending on where you live. Every country has conditions determining when later abortions are permitted and how they are performed. Navigating access to these services can be difficult, especially for people who are marginalised, young or poor. It can be impossible for those with insecure immigration or refugee status. In Europe, the best places to travel to are the Netherlands, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is available on request in clinics up to 22 weeks, England and Wales, where you can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 24 weeks, and in some cases Spain.\par \par \uc2 And here we return to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/02/texas-where-to-get-abortion-legally"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas}}}\uc2 . Many will say that if a person in Texas now requires an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks, they can travel to a different state. We have been hearing this argument for years. Irish women can \u8220\'20\'1Cjust travel\u8221\'20\'1D. Polish women can \u8220\'20\'1Cjust travel\u8221\'20\'1D. But this fails to take into consideration the obstacles to travelling for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as childcare, insecure immigration status, the fear of stigma or ostracisation, money and Brexit, which is making it hard for non-EU or UK residents to come to England.\par \par \uc2 These obstacles predate Covid-19, but the pandemic has made the problem worse. Many of our service users have been affected by airport closures in Poland and Malta, and expensive PCR tests required for people entering and returning to a country. Some of the obstacles have been farcical: one non-English-speaking service user arrived at her booked hotel in England to find all the rooms had been given to key workers and rough sleepers, while another woman had her flights cancelled and rebooked seven times for one journey. And travelling during a global pandemic carries extra health risks. The irony of risking your health to access healthcare is not lost on us.\par \par \uc2 Helping people get abortions requires a strong constitution. We will never be able to reach everyone who needs our help. But we know that as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funders we are part of a growing global network of activists who are finding ever more creative ways to help people access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and that we will continue this work until our helplines stop ringing.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Mara Clarke\uc2  is founder of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Support Network\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210903eh930025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
