\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats rush to find strategy to counter Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  law\b0\par\par\uc2 Hugo Lowell \par \uc2 664 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par 06:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s options are limited and filibuster poses roadblock to federal legislation\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and top Democrats are scrambling for a strategy to counter Republican restrictions on women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights amid the fallout from a Texas statute that has banned abortions in the state from as early as six weeks into pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 but the options available to the administration are thin.\par \par \uc2 The conservative-dominated supreme court in a night-time ruling refused an emergency request to block the Texas law from taking effect, in a decision that amounted to a crushing defeat for reproductive rights and threatened major ramifications in other states nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Even as the US president on Thursday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/02/biden-supreme-court-texas-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused the court of carrying out an assault on vital constitutional rights}}}\uc2  and ordered the federal government to ensure women in Texas retained access to abortions, the future of reproductive rights remains in the balance.\par \par \uc2 The challenges facing Biden and Democrats reflect the deep polarization of Congress, and the difficulty in trying to force bipartisan consensus on perhaps the most controversial of issues in American politics.\par \par \uc2 Now, top Democrats in Congress have developed a multi-part strategy to roll back restrictions pushed by Republican-led states that rests on attempting to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protections into federal law \u8211\'20\'13 and potentially to reform the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 The House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , announced on Thursday that Democrats would vote within weeks to pass the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act, a bill that would ensure the right to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and for medical providers to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUpon our return, the House will bring up congresswoman Judy Chu\u8217\'20\'19s Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act to enshrine into law reproductive healthcare for all women across America,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said in a statement that also admonished the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 Separately, liberal Democrats led by progressives including the New York congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez are urging Biden to strike down other restrictions on access to abortions and the Hyde amendment, a measure that prohibits federal funding for most abortions.\par \par \uc2 Seizing on the Texas decision, liberal Democrats have also called anew for an expansion of the supreme court from nine to 13 seats, which would enable Biden to appoint four liberal-leaning justices to shift the politics on the bench.\par \par \uc2 The legislative response is aimed at reversing more than 500 restrictions introduced by Republican state legislatures in recent months and \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger laws\u8221\'20\'1D that would automatically outlaw abortions if the supreme court overturned its ruling in the landmark Roe v Wade case that was supposed to cement \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US.\par \par \uc2 But while such protections are almost certain to be straightforwardly approved by the Democratic-controlled House, all of the proposals face a steep uphill climb in the face of sustained Republican opposition and a filibuster in the 50-50 Senate.\par \par \uc2 The Senate filibuster rule \u8211\'20\'13 a procedural tactic that requires a supermajority to pass most bills \u8211\'20\'13 was in part why the Senate Republican leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , focused on stacking the supreme court with conservative justices rather than pursue legislation to enact \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions at a federal level.\par \par \uc2 Forty-eight Democrats currently sponsor the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act in the Senate. Two Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 Senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski \u8211\'20\'13 have previously indicated support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but the numbers fall far short of the 60-vote threshold required to avoid a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Against that backdrop, a majority of Senate Democrats have called for eliminating the filibuster entirely. But reforming the filibuster requires the support of all Democrats in the Senate, and conservative Democratic senators including West Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s Joe Manchin and Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Kyrsten Sinema are outspoken supporters of the rule.\par \par \uc2 The broad concern demonstrates how urgent the issue has become for Democrats, and with the Texas law in effect after the failure of the emergency stay, many reproductive rights advocates worry that Democrats will be unable to meet the moment with meaningful action.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210903eh93000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
