\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Roe v Wade died with barely a whimper. But that\u8217\'20\'19s not all\b0\par\par\uc2 Laurence H Tribe \par \uc2 1,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par 16:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Financial rewards given to those shredding the US constitution? That is the reality of the Texas law on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 For years, as the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s composition kept tilting right, reproductive rights have been squarely on the chopping block. Now they are on the auction block as well.\par \par \uc2 Observers have speculated how today\u8217\'20\'19s new ultra-right court would commence the slicing: by chipping away slowly at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bBp9dX96Nw_U4zBXyQda3qulh4kKyt0iB56vSDbBg6c1qIvbR9gRJbpYS-SMQGnw1LrHKCYDo1j1Y2mPfpTgbCUPPoAxtEkZRJOMbYMZbQf6Ghg2xCSSWS_Wmk-AHjaDV"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  ? Or by taking the political heat and overruling it outright? Few imagined that the court would let a statute everybody concedes is flagrantly unconstitutional under the legal regime of Roe not only go into effect without being judicially reviewed but become the centerpiece of a totally unique state scheme that puts a bounty of at least $10,000 on the head of every woman who is or might be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just Roe that died at midnight on 1 September with barely a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bi0Sft_DdDs8Z0nkAaJr6kaRfOpFEfF9pNX68oKToUi18j46v4ju5fpUJc7HislTB49BcxNGYcSBkdX3-c_EClQ~~"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 whimper, let alone a bang}}}\uc2 . It was the principle that nobody\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional rights should be put on sale for purchase by anyone who can find an informant or helper to turn in whoever might be trying to exercise those rights.\par \par \uc2 That, after all, is how the new Texas law works. Its perverse structure, which delegates to private individuals anywhere a power the state of Texas is forbidden to exercise itself until Roe is overruled, punishes even the slightest form of assistance to desperate pregnant women. Doctors, family members, insurance companies, even Uber drivers, are all at risk if they help a woman in need. And the risk is magnified by the offer of a big fat financial reward for whoever successfully nabs a person guilty of facilitating an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once a heartbeat can be detected, typically six weeks after a woman\u8217\'20\'19s last period, well before most women even know they are pregnant. There is not even an exception for pregnancies resulting from rape or incest. No law remotely like this has ever been allowed to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of hefty bounties will breed a system of profit-seeking, Soviet-style informing on friends and neighbors. These vigilantes will sue medical distributors of IUDs and morning-after pills, as well as insurance companies. These companies, in turn, will stop offering reproductive healthcare in Texas. As of a minute before midnight on 31 August, clinics in Texas were already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bICsFZ6nnVxpA0uE5v7dGzaqGimAHrYKjImFQ-J1uRps8a_8_8l2gP3hffgQ-iOWUBQG7LOV-r5HdO9EYScpVBp31DgjCcHE1EDeBi_cyk_v0-u4oFERNj3udX4I7shi5RotCaIRpwhj_Zv6HIQdaqVGPSGYAnBBq1dkiVL1G2to~"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turning patients}}}\uc2  away out of fear. Even if the law is eventually struck down, many will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bOHHRnL39xwVaXSVamdXG1Ug6Qxo-QveH4iUcov4ypcM7cZ-U8PQliTUAZc50hed0BnZ-Zbd6A_knrZLdrg4ABIFlqYxkznK4p4HrOAQ4awTHszt7FaGmjs95inom0AKZ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 probably close}}}\uc2  anyway.\par \par \uc2 Worse still, if women try to escape the state to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, their families will be on the hook for offering even the smallest aid. If friends or family of a woman hoping to terminate her pregnancy drive her across state lines, or help her organize money for a plane or bus ticket, they could be liable for \u8220\'20\'1Caiding and abetting\u8221\'20\'1D a now-banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if the procedure itself takes place outside Texas.\par \par \uc2 Adding insult to injury, if a young woman asks for money for a bus ticket, or a ride to the airport, friends and parents fearful of liability might vigorously interrogate her about her intentions. This nightmarish state of affairs burdens yet another fundamental constitutional privilege: the right to interstate travel, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bBp9dX96Nw_U4zBXyQda3qvIEiCPp43WfOX54jOR2lCtJIwFd_FeIUjJ37ipad0EPcvs9VLL4AoD0BEMFdy0I1errkQ5B9textVGycrYd2-vjrxDwIsjw_jqXgo-13T_s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recognized}}}\uc2  by the supreme court in 1999 as a core privilege of federal citizenship. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Handmaid%27s_Tale"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Welcome to Gilead!}}}\par \par \uc2 Many wealthy women will presumably still find ways to access care. But their poor, disproportionately minority sisters will be stuck, forced to face down the barrel of unimaginably cruel choices. Desperate women will still seek abortions but will be forced to do so on the black market and in back alleys. Fewer Samaritans will risk heavy fines or imprisonment to help them. Some will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bF4fGdUh8OG3b1ATUwolbogY7jTYIx0EBl8W1pa0YeqaW9MBo80wlZx6ccn0fMPSGx_KXhxLn1ApcLOlJxE1GeeG0-tNjkqZPwRWBWgB-cadwfxuCQtQU4NlmWwMG0pgm78WTPoTafuMWQC5U2K7KmjHwno-yvvmtvTpm3FO-8r0~"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 die trying}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What can be done? We can give up on this court and try pressuring Congress to pass the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?b7UE2yfMelFt9Xz7NIwUT97wdnHZTDrRwJccJ31Ge3b1EsFlfIkCEiPHCFfl8l_BFGEt715DKdejKks2eS0qO_dzdnDD1dzK78KXRzAfNJUVsUsQhKSTGIXqyUfCa6n7t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bUS0wvqrhA6Td9JZPjsr9BosGyYpgYwP2WmVscR9DaRhjxW7-3UAyLi7fybIJBo0ZqkQ73jo9D0KbEWbfKjFXdc4E4coeoaBmbYCvM5Yy23IVV51Xi89kCfMq8wUUDR4j"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which would}}}\uc2  enshrine a federal statutory right to provide and receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care free of these sorts of state schemes. But such a bill would die at the hands of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the minority leader, in a Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 And what if it were somehow to pass? Odds are that a court majority, despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://url.emailprotection.link/?bBp9dX96Nw_U4zBXyQda3qlU9xRV-5Tf4uAbHkG5INxP6FldEyW_SSCRVBDTIpgOHLoNUMWThGepb6QWcO4wbzyaS8BJdu8t8o_7aOcWf5xP9O0Qwy3t9BNVDGnm_04S8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 having held}}}\uc2  that Congress is empowered to enact a nationwide ban on certain late-term abortions because medical procedures are part of interstate commerce, would suddenly \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscover\u8221\'20\'1D new limits on the reach of the commerce clause as a source of congressional power and strike the act down. When the court so casually lets a law that flouts its precedents take effect, all bets are off.\par \par \uc2 Or are they? Maybe even justices deeply hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights can be persuaded to balk specifically at the unprecedented financial incentives this grotesque law creates to put a price on the head of every pregnant woman or girl. Shades of sex slavery and prostitution might put this privatization of law enforcement in a light even conservative jurists find unbearable. What if women chilled by this business model, or those seeking to help them to avoid unwanted motherhood, were to sue the Texas authorities who stand ready to disburse $10,000 bounties for each forbidden \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  detected or prevented?\par \par \uc2 As Justice Sotomayor said in her dissent \u8211\'20\'13 there were four dissents in all \u8211\'20\'13 the Texas law \u8220\'20\'1Cis a breathtaking act of defiance \u8211\'20\'13 of the constitution, of this court\u8217\'20\'19s precedents, and of the rights of women seeking abortions throughout Texas\u8221\'20\'1D. After a puzzling silence of a day and night, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe court finally [told] the Nation that it declined to act because, in short, the State\u8217\'20\'19s gambit worked.\u8221\'20\'1D Even if not a single justice in the 5-4 majority rejects the ability of a state to \u8220\'20\'1Cevade federal judicial scrutiny by outsourcing the enforcement of unconstitutional laws to its citizenry\u8221\'20\'1D, and even if all five of the justices in that majority stand ready to trash Roe v Wade, maybe at least one of those justices would agree that no state can hand out financial rewards to people \u8211\'20\'13 not only citizens in Texas but people from anywhere in the country, perhaps the world \u8211\'20\'13 shredding the constitution of the United States?\par \par \uc2 At least it\u8217\'20\'19s worth a try.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Laurence H Tribe is the Carl M Loeb University professor and professor of constitutional law emeritus at Harvard University and an accomplished supreme court advocate. Follow him on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @Tribelaw\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210902eh92002bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
