\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Texas poised to ban most abortions as court denies emergency motion\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 957 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2021\par 15:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Law letting individuals sue those helping women access service will go into effect on 1 September unless federal court intervenes\par \par \uc2 Texas could become the first state in decades to ban most abortions, if a federal court allows a law called SB8 to take effect on 1 September.\par \par \uc2 A hearing was originally scheduled on Monday on whether the court should block the law. But the fifth circuit court of appeals {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/08/29/texas-abortion-law-5th-circuit-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cancelled}}}\uc2  the hearing late on Friday, and denied reproductive rights group an emergency motion on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf this law is not blocked by September 1, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas will come to an abrupt stop,\u8221\'20\'1D said Marc Hearron, senior counsel at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  in a statement.\par \par \uc2 SB8 effectively puts a $10,000 \u8220\'20\'1Cbounty\u8221\'20\'1D on the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and anyone else who helps a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past roughly six weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation, by allowing private citizens to sue those who \u8220\'20\'1Caid and abet\u8221\'20\'1D women in exercising this constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 Opponents have warned the law could also provide a backdoor to attack other controversial civil rights, such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2021/07/19/texas-sb8-abortion-lawsuits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gun rights or free speech}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe law is really unprecedented in the sense that it bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but then has no government criminal penalties to enforce the law,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brigitte Amiri, deputy director of the American Civil Liberties Union\u8217\'20\'19s Reproductive Freedom Project, and an attorney representing a group of plaintiffs who have sued to stop the law from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt authorizes anyone in the country to file a lawsuit against any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, or anyone who helps someone get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and seek a penalty of that person of at least $10,000 per \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amiri.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became legal across the United States following the 1973 supreme court decision Roe v Wade. The decision provided women a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point a fetus can survive outside the womb, generally around 24 weeks. A full-term pregnancy is 39 weeks.\par \par \uc2 However, that right has been under assault by state legislators for decades, and incursions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights became particularly aggressive after Tea Party Republicans helped win control of state legislatures in 2011.\par \par \uc2 In addition to a $10,000 penalty, SB8 would saddle violators of the law with their opponents\u8217\'20\'19 attorneys fees. It provides no such relief for defendants, even if they win. The result would be crushing legal expenses and duplicative lawsuits that would in effect end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Proponents of the law \u8211\'20\'13 including a self-described {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/27/us-tiny-towns-anti-abortion-ordinances"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 virgin and traveling preacher, and a well-heeled former Texas solicitor general}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have already found success in the strategy. In Lubbock, Texas, a similar law was passed via referendum and has forced a Planned Parenthood in the city of 253,000 to stop providing abortions while it fights the case in federal court.\par \par \uc2 The new law, called \u8220\'20\'1Cheinous\u8221\'20\'1D and the \u8220\'20\'1Csue thy neighbor\u8221\'20\'1D law by opponents, is part of a three-prong strategy to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas, including a separate ban on the most common surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure after 15 weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation, and a third campaign to outlaw medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after seven weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur creator endowed us with the right to life and yet millions of children lose their right to life every year because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Greg Abbott, Texas\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, said at a signing ceremony, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/05/18/texas-heartbeat-bill-abortions-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Tribune}}}\uc2  reported in May. Abbott had in effect banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for one month in April 2020 by arguing it was not \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediately medically necessary\u8221\'20\'1D because of the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers representing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/07/13/texas-heartbeat-bill-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 20 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  providers}}}\uc2  are hopeful they will prevail. However, a reprieve in the form of an injunction could come just hours before the law goes into effect, and any delay holds the potential to throw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas into chaos.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s astounding, because what it is doing is deputizing private citizens to become prosecutors in a way,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nina Ginsberg, a criminal defense attorney who recently examined how states have built a criminal framework to prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should Roe v Wade be overturned, in a report for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nacdl.org/Document/AbortioninAmericaLegOverreachCriminalizReproRights?_zs=ck20Q1&_zl=4H7P6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Association of Criminal Defense Lawyers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Should SB8 succeed, it would ban about 85% of abortions, since most women do not know they are pregnant at six weeks. Although proponents describe SB8 as a \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal heartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D bill, the term can be misleading because, though embryos have cardiac activity at this stage, they do not have functioning hearts.\par \par \uc2 SB8\u8217\'20\'19s provisions, which allow anyone, anywhere, unconnected to an event to sue, make it highly unusual both as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and in terms of civil litigation broadly. It would essentially upend the way civil courts consider \u8220\'20\'1Cstanding\u8221\'20\'1D, the concept that people must be sued where they live or work and by plaintiffs who have been harmed in some way.\par \par \uc2 The law is so broad and unconventional that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://avowtexas.org/2021/04/28/more-than-370-texas-lawyers-raise-legal-concerns-express-strong-opposition-to-hb-1515-and-sb-8-in-open-letter/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 370}}}\uc2  Texas lawyers, former judges, legal professors and local officials signed an open letter opposing it.\par \par \uc2 The Rev Daniel C Kanter, senior minister at First Unitarian church in Dallas, said he became a plaintiff in the suit against the law because outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows \u8220\'20\'1Cjustice and compassion\u8221\'20\'1D to be \u8220\'20\'1Coverrun by ideological issues\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 but also because he could be sued under its provisions, limiting his ability to advise pregnant women on options to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an incredibly dangerous thing, especially around religious liberty,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kanter.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/27/us-tiny-towns-anti-abortion-ordinances"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tiny towns across Texas and Nebraska}}}\uc2  have adopted measures similar to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s SB8, calling themselves \u8220\'20\'1Csanctuary cities for the unborn\u8221\'20\'1D. However, those ordinances have had little more than a chilling effect on women, since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers rarely operate in such rural locations.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210830eh8u001p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
